    <div type="book" osisID="Matt" canonical="true">
        <chapter sID="Matt.1" osisID="Matt.1" n="1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.1" osisID="Matt.1.1" n="1"/>The scroll of the birth of Yeshua the Messiah, the son of David, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="1" osisRef="Matt.1.1" osisID="Matt.1.1!note.1">In Matthew 1:1, 1:6, 1:17, 1:20, 9:27, 15:23, David’s name is spelled as דוד . Yet, in 12:3, his name is spelled with the added yod ( דויד ). In the Dead Sea Scrolls (DSS), the name of King David is spelled with the added yod (י) mater lectionis: דויד .</note>the son of Avraham (Abraham).<verse eID="Matt.1.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.2" osisID="Matt.1.2" n="2"/>Abraham begat Yitzhaq (Isaac), and Yitzhaq (Isaac) begat Ya’akov (Jacob); and Ya'akov (Jacob), Yehudah (Judah), and his brothers.<verse eID="Matt.1.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.3" osisID="Matt.1.3" n="3"/>Yehudah (Judah) begah Peretz (Perez) and Zerah from Tamar; Peretz begat Hetsron (Hezron), and Hetsron, Aram (Ram).<verse eID="Matt.1.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.4" osisID="Matt.1.4" n="4"/>Aram (Ram) begat Aminadab (Amminadab); and Aminadab (Amminadab), Nahshon; and Nahshon, Salmon.<verse eID="Matt.1.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.5" osisID="Matt.1.5" n="5"/>And Salmon begat Boaz from Rahab, and Boaz begat Obed from Ruth, and Obed begat Yishai (Jesse).<verse eID="Matt.1.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.6" osisID="Matt.1.6" n="6"/>Yishai (Jesse) begat David; David, Shelomoh (Solomon), from the wife of Uriyyah (Uriah).<verse eID="Matt.1.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.7" osisID="Matt.1.7" n="7"/>Shlomoh (Solomon) begat Rehabam (Rehoboam); and Rehabam begat Abiyyah (Abijah); Abiyyah, Asa.<verse eID="Matt.1.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.8" osisID="Matt.1.8" n="8"/>Asa begat Yehosaphat (Jehoshaphat); Yehoshaphat, Yoram (Joram), and Yoram, Uzziah.<verse eID="Matt.1.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.9" osisID="Matt.1.9" n="9"/>Uzziyyahu (Uzziah) begat Yotham (Jotham), Yotham begat Ahaz, Hezekiah.<verse eID="Matt.1.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.10" osisID="Matt.1.10" n="10"/>Hezqiyyah (Hezekiah) begat Menasheh (Manasseh); Menasheh, Amon; Amon, Yoshiyah (Josiah).<verse eID="Matt.1.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.11" osisID="Matt.1.11" n="11"/>Yoshiyah (Josiah) begat Yekonyah (Jeconiah) and his brothers in the exile of Babel.<verse eID="Matt.1.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.12" osisID="Matt.1.12" n="12"/>From the exile of Babel, Yekonya (Joconiah) begat She’alti’el (Shealtiel); She’alti’el, Zerubbabel.<verse eID="Matt.1.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.13" osisID="Matt.1.13" n="13"/>And Zerubbabel begat Abihud; and Abihud, Elyaqum (Eliakim); and Elyaqum begat Azor.<verse eID="Matt.1.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.14" osisID="Matt.1.14" n="14"/>Azor begat Zadok; and Zadok, Akhin (Achim); and Achin (Achim), Eliyyud (Elihud).<verse eID="Matt.1.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.15" osisID="Matt.1.15" n="15"/>Eliyyud (Elihud) begat Eleazar; Eleazar, Mattan; and Mattan, Ya’akov (Jacob).<verse eID="Matt.1.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.16" osisID="Matt.1.16" n="16"/>And Ya’akov (Jacob) begat Yoseph (Joseph), the man <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.1.16" osisID="Matt.1.16!note.6">Some manuscripts say, “husband,” and others say, “father.” While this should be Miriam’s father as the genealogy of Yeshua comes through Mariam and she married a man with the same name as her father. And by being Miriam’s father in this case, this counts to fourteen generations which is stated in Matthew 1:17.</note>of Miriam (Mary), from whom was born Yeshua, who is called the Messiah.<verse eID="Matt.1.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.17" osisID="Matt.1.17" n="17"/>All this, from Abraham until David’s kindred, was fourteen, and from David until the exile of Babel (Babylon), fourteen generations, and from the exile of Babel until Messiah, fourteen generations.<verse eID="Matt.1.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.18" osisID="Matt.1.18" n="18"/>The birth of Yeshua Messiah was accordingly: when his mother, Miriam (Mary) the betrothed of Yoseph (Joseph), though not yet been joined together, she was found to be pregnant from the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit).<verse eID="Matt.1.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.19" osisID="Matt.1.19" n="19"/>And Yoseph (Joseph), this husband of hers, was righteous and did not want to publicize, but to pardon and hide, and cause to leave.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.1.19" osisID="Matt.1.19!note.10">Meaning possibly, “this situation” to leave.” This verb form is a Nif’al and is reflective (i.e. “I am doing this to myself”) or reciprocal in action (“I want between you and me to be pardoned”. Therefore, this verb indicates that he desired to have he and his betrothed to be pardoned together.</note> Alternate # Translation: And Yoseph (Joseph), this husband of hers, was righteous and did not want to publicize, but pardon her to hide and to leave.<verse eID="Matt.1.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.20" osisID="Matt.1.20" n="20"/>When he had this intent, the messenger of MarYah (Master Yehovah) became visible to him in a dream and said to him, “Son of David, do not fear to take Mirayam (Mary), your wife, for she is pregnant from the Holy Spirit.<verse eID="Matt.1.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.21" osisID="Matt.1.21" n="21"/>She bears this son, and you will call His name, Yeshua, for He redeems <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.1.21" osisID="Matt.1.21!note.13">This is an active participle verb form meaning, “redeemer,” one who redeems by purchasing. See Isaiah 47:4; 48:17; 49:7; 59:20 and see 52:9. Then notice the Greek, from the translation of The Scriptures clearly uses a future tense of “saving” while the Peshitta Aramaic also uses a future tense.</note>the people from sins,<verse eID="Matt.1.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.22" osisID="Matt.1.22" n="22"/>This was from what was said of the prophet:<verse eID="Matt.1.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23" n="23"/>“Behold! The maiden <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23!note.15">See Isaiah 7:14.</note>is pregnant and bears <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23!note.16">This is an active participle meaning, “one who bears” or simply, “bears.” It is not a future tense, but a present tense, unlike the Greek and the Peshitta Aramaic. The phrase “Our God is with us” is another example of the Aramaic language influencing the Hebrew language, known as “Aramaism.”</note>a son, and you shall call His name,” Immanu’el,” which translates to, “Our God is with us.”<verse eID="Matt.1.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.24" osisID="Matt.1.24" n="24"/>And when he rose from his sleep, he did as the messenger of the MarYah (Master Yehovah) ordered him, and he lifted himself up, and his wife.<verse eID="Matt.1.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.25" osisID="Matt.1.25" n="25"/>And he did not know her <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.1.25" osisID="Matt.1.25!note.20">This is a Hebrew euphemism for having sexual relations.</note>until she bore her firstborn son, and he called his name Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.1.25"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.1"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.2" osisID="Matt.2" n="2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.1" osisID="Matt.2.1" n="1"/>When Yeshua was born in Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah) in the days of Horodos (Herodes), the king, magicians from the East came to Yerushalem (Jerusalem),<verse eID="Matt.2.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.2" osisID="Matt.2.2" n="2"/>and they said, “Now the king of the Yehudim (Jews) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.2.2" osisID="Matt.2.2!note.21">Literally, “wise ones.”</note>is born,” for they saw a star of the east, they came to bow down to Him.<verse eID="Matt.2.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.3" osisID="Matt.2.3" n="3"/>And Horodos (Herodes) the king, heard this and trembled a̶nd all Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) with him.<verse eID="Matt.2.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.4" osisID="Matt.2.4" n="4"/>And he gathered all the chief priests and scribes of the people, and asked them, “Where is the Messiah born?”<verse eID="Matt.2.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.5" osisID="Matt.2.5" n="5"/>They said, “In Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah), according to the writing of the prophet.<verse eID="Matt.2.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.6" osisID="Matt.2.6" n="6"/>And you, Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah), were not young from the thousands of Yehudah; from you to me will go out a king, an approved one<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Matt.2.6" osisID="Matt.2.6!note.24">This is an important difference between the Greek and Hebrew as King Herod the Great was not approved by Israel. He was an Edomite/Nabatean. He was appointed by the Romans to be king of Israel. Harod was very oppressive to the people of Israel and was not accepted as a legitimate king by the people.</note> for my people Israel.<verse eID="Matt.2.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.7" osisID="Matt.2.7" n="7"/>So now Horodes (Herodes) called the magicians and the sages <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.2.7" osisID="Matt.2.7!note.21">Literally, “wise ones.”</note>and learned from them what time the star became visible.<verse eID="Matt.2.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.8" osisID="Matt.2.8" n="8"/>And he sent them to Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) and said to them, “Go in secrecy because of the boy, who was born, and when you have found him, come, and you tell me, and also I will go and bow down to Him.”<verse eID="Matt.2.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.9" osisID="Matt.2.9" n="9"/>When they heard from the king, they went, and this star that they saw in the east went before them, until they came, and it stood up to the place where the boy was.<verse eID="Matt.2.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.10" osisID="Matt.2.10" n="10"/>When they saw the stars, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="25" osisRef="Matt.2.10" osisID="Matt.2.10!note.25">This being plural seems to suggest it is a celestial event on display, matching the report of many planetary alignments in 2 BCE /3 BCE.</note>they rejoiced with great and delightful joy.<verse eID="Matt.2.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.11" osisID="Matt.2.11" n="11"/>And they entered into the house and saw the boy with his mother, Miryam (Mary), and they bowed down to Him and opened their treasures and offered to Him an offering of gold and musk and frankincense.<verse eID="Matt.2.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.12" osisID="Matt.2.12" n="12"/>And it appeared to them in a dream that they should not return to Horodos (Herodes), and they went a different path to their place.<verse eID="Matt.2.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.13" osisID="Matt.2.13" n="13"/>When they went, in a dream appeared the MarYah (Master Yehovah) to Yoseph and said, “Rise, take the boy and His mother and escape to Mitzrayim (Egypt), and there you shall remain until I tell you, for Horodos (Herodes) wants to kill the boy.”<verse eID="Matt.2.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.14" osisID="Matt.2.14" n="14"/>This Yoseph (Joseph) rose and took the boy and His mother at night and escaped to and was there until he made it to Mitzrayim (Egypt).<verse eID="Matt.2.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.15" osisID="Matt.2.15" n="15"/>And he remained there until Horodos (Herodes) died to complete what was spoken by the MarYah (Master Yehovah) to the prophet who said, “And from Mitzrayim (Egypt) I have called my Son.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Matt.2.15" osisID="Matt.2.15!note.26">Hosea 11:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.2.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.16" osisID="Matt.2.16" n="16"/>And now, when Horodos (Herodes) saw that he was degraded by the magicians, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="27" osisRef="Matt.2.16" osisID="Matt.2.16!note.27">The magi were considered as scientists and were from the Parthian Empire. They were centered on Iran and had peace agreements with Rome. There is a report of a large caravan of Parthian soldiers showing up in Jerusalem around the time of Yeshua’s birth. See the article entitled “The Time of the Magi: When Magic, Religion, and Science Were One,” by Harvard University Press. Website: https://www.hup.harvard.edu/features/the-time-of-the-magi-when-magic-religion-and-science-were-one.</note>he schemed much and sent/ordered to kill all the boys of Bet Lehem (Bethlehem) in all its region, from two years and under, according to when it was declared to him by the magicians.<verse eID="Matt.2.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.17" osisID="Matt.2.17" n="17"/>And now was taken place what was spoken by Yirmeyah (Jeremiah) the prophet, who said,<verse eID="Matt.2.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.18" osisID="Matt.2.18" n="18"/>“A voice in Ramah was heard, there is weeping and bitter wailings: Rakhel (Rachel) lamenting over her children, refusing to be comforted, for they are not.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="28" osisRef="Matt.2.18" osisID="Matt.2.18!note.28">Jeremiah 31:15.</note><verse eID="Matt.2.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.19" osisID="Matt.2.19" n="19"/>When Horodos (Herodes) the king died, a messenger of the LORD (Yehovah) appeared in a dream to Yoseph (Joseph) in Mitzrayim (Egypt) and said to him,<verse eID="Matt.2.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.20" osisID="Matt.2.20" n="20"/>“Rise, take the boy and His mother and go to the land of Yisra’el (Israel), for those seeking the soul of the boy have died.”<verse eID="Matt.2.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.21" osisID="Matt.2.21" n="21"/>And Yoseph (Joseph) rose and took the boy and His mother and came to the land of Yisrael (Israel).<verse eID="Matt.2.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.22" osisID="Matt.2.22" n="22"/>When he heard that Archeleos (Archelaos) was king in Judah instead of Horodos (Herodes), his father feared going there. However, he had a dream to go to the location of Gilil (Galilee).<verse eID="Matt.2.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.23" osisID="Matt.2.23" n="23"/>Then he came and dwelt in the province called Nazareth, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="30" osisRef="Matt.2.23" osisID="Matt.2.23!note.30">See Jeremiah 31:6. This verse is commonly translated to be about watchmen. However, the word used in Hebrew is “Notzrim,” which can be translated as Nazarene. Therefore, this verse in Jeremiah would be, “For there shall come a day when they will be called Nazarenes in the hill country of Ephraim.” Thus, Jeremiah 31:6 directly correlates to Matthew. 2:23 as they are in the Galilee region. Also see Isaiah 11:1: “There shall come forth a Rod from the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots.” The Hebrew word used for Branch is “Netzer.” The town of Nazareth derives its name from Netzer, which means "branch town."</note>as that would take place what was said of the prophet, “What of whom will be called of Nazarene?”<verse eID="Matt.2.23"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.2"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.3" osisID="Matt.3" n="3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1" n="1"/>On that day, Yochanan (John) the sinker of man (babti),,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="31" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.31">The literal transliteration of this Hebrew word is “babtista”. The Greek transliteration of “Baptism” is βαπτίζω (pronounced as “baptizō”). The literal Greek for “John the Baptizer” is "baptistés." None of the Greek transliterations correspond directly to the transliteration from Greek to the Cochin Hebrew Matthew. It is possible that the term is slang derived from another language or a multilingual slang term. One potential example is a slang term from the Mandean Aramaic language. “Metathetic form, possibly simply a scribal oversight, since it is only attested once, whereas ṬBA is common in Mandaic. Page refs. in other dictionaries: Drower/Macuch: 58a.” website: https://cal.huc.edu/oneentry.php?lemma=b%2By%20V&amp;cits=all. This word may be a slang Mandaic term, an Aramaic dialect of the Mandeans. The “Mandaeans (Mandaic) (Arabic: نويئادنملا al-Mandāʾiyyūn), also known as Mandaean Sabians (نويئادنملا ةئباصلا al-Ṣābiʾa al- Mandāʾiyyūn) or simply as Sabians (ةئباصلا al-Ṣābiʾa),[b] are an ethnoreligious group who are followers of Mandaeism. They believe that John the Baptist was the final and most important prophet. They may have been among the earliest religious groups to practice baptism and among the earliest adherents of Gnosticism, a belief system of which they are the last surviving representatives today.[24][25]:109,” website: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandaeans. This active participle form means, “sinkers of man.”</note>,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="32" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.32">Buckley, Jorunn Jacobsen (2010). Turning the Tables on Jesus: The Mandaean View. In Horsley, Richard (March 2010). Christian Origins. Minneapolis: Fortress Press. pp. 94–11. ISBN 978-1-4514-1664-0.</note>,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="33" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.33">McGrath, James (January 23, 2015), "The First Baptists, The Last Gnostics: The Mandaeans", YouTube-A lunchtime talk about the Mandaeans by Dr. James F. McGrath at Butler University, retrieved November 3, 2021.</note> <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="34" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.34">This word could also be a transliteration from another language.</note>was proclaiming in the desert-like<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="35" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.35">Gebhardt-Klein, Joseph. The Travancore-Cochin, India Manuscript of Ezekiel Raḥabi’s Rabbinical-Hebrew Matthew Text and Translation, pp. vi. Gebhardt-Klein stated, “Note the prevalent mistaking of the Syriac conjunction/adverb (ܢܝܕ) cognate to the Biblical Aramaic אדין but later used in Syriac to emulate the Greek (δε) for the JPA 3rd person masculine singular pronoun דין , leading to many superfluous instances of זה . Other examples come from the redactor clumsily adapting Syriac words into Hebrew: e.g. Mt 3:1 reads that John preached “in the ruins” ( בחרבות ) of the Judeans, attempting to preserve the Syriac “in the desert” (ܐܒܪܘܚܒ).” Yet, in Gesenius' Hebrew-Chaldee Lexicon the word חרב (cherev) identifies that it also has the meaning of, “drought.” See Deuteronomy 28:22. While it is true that the word חרב (cherev) could be a Syriac word that means, “wild, or desert-like.” It doesn’t appear that the author was attempting to adapt the Syriac language in this case, but was using Hebrew to reference the desert where the Yehudim were located. See website: https://cal.huc.edu/oneentry.php?lemma=xrb%20A&amp;cits=all.</note> region of the Yehudim<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="36" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.36">Gesenius' Hebrew-Chaldee Lexicon ( יְהּודִי ), “In the later Hebrew, after the carrying away of the ten tribes, it was applied to any Israelite.” See also Jer 32:12, 38:19, 40:11, 43:9, esp 34:9; Neh 1:2, 3:33, 4:6; Est 2:5, 3:4, 5:13.</note> (Jews) saying,<verse eID="Matt.3.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2" n="2"/>“Return! For the kingdom of heaven is near!,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="38" osisRef="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2!note.38">Malachi 3:1.</note><note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="39" osisRef="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2!note.39">Yeshua delivers the same message in Matthew 4:17.</note><verse eID="Matt.3.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3" n="3"/>Behold! For this is who was mentioned by Isaiah the prophet, ‘A voice calling in the desert: ‘Turn to the path of <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="40" osisRef="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3!note.40">Literally, “Face the path of…”</note>Yehovah, make it straight in the desert.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="41" osisRef="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3!note.41">This is a direct quote from Isaiah 40:3, קול קורא במדבר פנו דרך ה֞ ישרו בערבה . “A voice calling in the desert: ‘Turn to the path of Yehovah, make straight in the desert.’’</note><verse eID="Matt.3.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.4" osisID="Matt.3.4" n="4"/>This John had clothing of the skin that was of a camel, and a leather belt upon his waist, and his food was grasshoppers and honey of the forest.<verse eID="Matt.3.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.5" osisID="Matt.3.5" n="5"/>And then he went out of Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) and all the Yehudim (“Jews,” i.e., kingdom of Judah), and all the area surrounding the Yarden (Jordan),<verse eID="Matt.3.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.6" osisID="Matt.3.6" n="6"/>they were baptized (all of them, those who were converted) from the river Yarden (Jordan), and they confessed their sin.<verse eID="Matt.3.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.7" osisID="Matt.3.7" n="7"/>As soon as they saw this, many of the sages and the Sadducees came to the one who does the conversions. He said to them, “Offspring of serpents, who told you to flee from the rage that will come?”<verse eID="Matt.3.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.8" osisID="Matt.3.8" n="8"/>“Do this like a meal of fruit which is worthy of goodwill.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="43" osisRef="Matt.3.8" osisID="Matt.3.8!note.43">Literal, “good desire.”</note><verse eID="Matt.3.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.9" osisID="Matt.3.9" n="9"/>And do not think and say within yourselves, ‘We have a father, Abraham.’ I, I say to you here it is possible for Elohim to raise up (establish) sons of Abraham from these stones.<verse eID="Matt.3.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.10" osisID="Matt.3.10" n="10"/>This is the scraping tool that is on the root of the tree: every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down and given over in the fire.<verse eID="Matt.3.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.11" osisID="Matt.3.11" n="11"/>I am he, cleansing you by water to my desire. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="46" osisRef="Matt.3.11" osisID="Matt.3.11!note.46">Or intent.</note>But he that comes after me, He is stronger than me, and I am not equal to bear his sandals. And He purifies you by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) and by fire.<verse eID="Matt.3.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.12" osisID="Matt.3.12" n="12"/>He has the fan <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="47" osisRef="Matt.3.12" osisID="Matt.3.12!note.47">The fan is waved over the fire to burn up the chaff.</note>in his hand and purifies his place, and the grain he brings into his storehouses, and the straw he burns with fire that is not extinguished.<verse eID="Matt.3.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.13" osisID="Matt.3.13" n="13"/>And in that hour, Yeshua came from Gailil (Galilee) to Yarden (Jordan) near Yochanan (John) to purify him.<verse eID="Matt.3.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.14" osisID="Matt.3.14" n="14"/>But Yochanan (John) ceased and said, “I need to be purified by you, and you come to me?”<verse eID="Matt.3.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.15" osisID="Matt.3.15" n="15"/>And Yeshua responded and answered and said to him, “Permit this now, for the purpose of this is well for us to establish all judgments,” and he permitted him in his sign of that hour.<verse eID="Matt.3.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.16" osisID="Matt.3.16" n="16"/>When Yeshua was purified, He hurriedly arose from the water, and the gate of the heavens opened for Him, and He saw the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) come down like a dove, and it came upon Him.<verse eID="Matt.3.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.17" osisID="Matt.3.17" n="17"/>Then he heard one voice of God (bat kol) from the heavens that said, “This is my beloved Son, and in Him, I desired.”<verse eID="Matt.3.17"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.3"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.4" osisID="Matt.4" n="4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.1" osisID="Matt.4.1" n="1"/>Then Yeshua was brought down to the wilderness by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) - indeed, that He would be tested by the Satan (adversary).<verse eID="Matt.4.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.2" osisID="Matt.4.2" n="2"/>And He fasted forty days, and forty nights; then He became hungry.<verse eID="Matt.4.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.3" osisID="Matt.4.3" n="3"/>Then Satan approached Him and said to him, “If you are the son of Elohim (God), tell the stones to become bread.”<verse eID="Matt.4.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.4" osisID="Matt.4.4" n="4"/>And He responded and said, “It is written, ‘Not by bread alone will man live, for upon all that comes from the mouth of Yehovah, he will live.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="50" osisRef="Matt.4.4" osisID="Matt.4.4!note.50">Deuteronomy 8:3.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.5" osisID="Matt.4.5" n="5"/>And then Satan led Him into the holy city and stood on top of the rooftop of the house of the Sanctuary<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="53" osisRef="Matt.4.5" osisID="Matt.4.5!note.53">The Sanctuary; this is referencing the Temple.</note> and said to Him,<verse eID="Matt.4.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.6" osisID="Matt.4.6" n="6"/>“If you are the Son of Elohim (God), throw yourself down to the earth below: for according to which is written, ‘For He commands His messengers concerning you... They will bear you up in their hands, lest you dash your foot against a stone.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="54" osisRef="Matt.4.6" osisID="Matt.4.6!note.54">Psalms 91:11-12.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.7" osisID="Matt.4.7" n="7"/>Yeshua said to him, “But indeed, it is written, ‘You shall not try Yehovah your Elohim (God)!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="55" osisRef="Matt.4.7" osisID="Matt.4.7!note.55">Deuteronomy 6:16, לֹא תְנַּסּו אֶת־יְהוָה אֱֹלהֵיכֶם . English translation, “Do not try Yehovah your Elohim...”</note><verse eID="Matt.4.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.8" osisID="Matt.4.8" n="8"/>And once more, Satan took Him and raised Him up to a very high mountain, and he showed Him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory. And he said to Him,<verse eID="Matt.4.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9" n="9"/>“All of this I will give you if you bow <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="57" osisRef="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9!note.57">This can mean, “bow your head in worship.”</note>and prostrate <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="58" osisRef="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9!note.58">Literally, “bow down,” or “worship.”</note>to me.”<verse eID="Matt.4.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10" n="10"/>And then He said to him (Yeshua to Satan), “Follow after Me: <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="59" osisRef="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10!note.59">Or can mean, “Get behind me.”</note>It is written, ‘Yehovah your Elohim (God), you shall fear; and Him, you shall serve.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="60" osisRef="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10!note.60">Deuteronomy 6:13a quote, “Fear Yehovah your Elohim and serve Him...”</note><verse eID="Matt.4.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.11" osisID="Matt.4.11" n="11"/>“Then Satan left, and lo, messengers approached and were ministering to Him.”<verse eID="Matt.4.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.12" osisID="Matt.4.12" n="12"/>And when Yeshua heard that Yochanan (John) was arrested in the house of prisoners, He returned to Galil (Galilee).<verse eID="Matt.4.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.13" osisID="Matt.4.13" n="13"/>And He left from Netzaret (Nazareth) and came to dwell in Kephar Nahum (Capernaum) beside the sea, that is in the borders of Zebulon and Naphtali,<verse eID="Matt.4.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.14" osisID="Matt.4.14" n="14"/>in order to establish the saying by the hand of the Prophet of Yeshayah (Isaiah), who said,<verse eID="Matt.4.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.15" osisID="Matt.4.15" n="15"/>“Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, the way of the sea across the Yarden (Jordan), Galil (Galilee) of the gentiles,”<verse eID="Matt.4.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16" n="16"/>“the people who sit <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="61" osisRef="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16!note.61">Can also mean, “who dwell.”</note>in darkness have seen the great light, and those sitting in the place of the shadow and death, a great light permeated them.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="62" osisRef="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16!note.62">Isaiah 9:1-2.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.17" osisID="Matt.4.17" n="17"/>Then He began to proclaim, saying, “Repent, for near to us is the kingdom of heaven!”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="63" osisRef="Matt.4.17" osisID="Matt.4.17!note.63">John the Immerser also proclaimed this same message in Matthew 3:2.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.18" osisID="Matt.4.18" n="18"/>And as Yeshua walked on the shore of the Sea of Galil (Galilee), He saw two brothers, Shim’on (Simon), named Kepha (Peter), and Andre (Andrew), his brother, casting nets into the sea, for they were fishermen.<verse eID="Matt.4.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.19" osisID="Matt.4.19" n="19"/>And He said to them, “Follow after Me. I will make you fishers of men.”<verse eID="Matt.4.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.20" osisID="Matt.4.20" n="20"/>And they left the net and went after Him.<verse eID="Matt.4.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.21" osisID="Matt.4.21" n="21"/>And when He passed from there, came two brothers: Ya’akov (Jacob/James) and Yochanan (John), his brother, in a ship with Zebdi (Zebedee), their father, improving their nets; and he called to them.<verse eID="Matt.4.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.22" osisID="Matt.4.22" n="22"/>They quickly abandoned the ship and their father and went after Him.<verse eID="Matt.4.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.23" osisID="Matt.4.23" n="23"/>And Yeshua went around all the Galileans teaching their assemblies and proclaiming the gospels of the kingdom and healing all their sick and all sicknesses in the people.<verse eID="Matt.4.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.24" osisID="Matt.4.24" n="24"/>And His report was heard in all Syria, and all approached Him who had iniquity and illness, weaknesses and pains, and even demon possession, and the deranged, and those with gout, and impeded speech, and wasted; and He healed them.<verse eID="Matt.4.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.25" osisID="Matt.4.25" n="25"/>Many went after Him and joined from Galil (Galilee), and from the ten cities, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="67" osisRef="Matt.4.25" osisID="Matt.4.25!note.67">“Dekapolis” in Greek.</note>and from Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), and from Yehudah (Judea), and from across the Yarden (Jordan).<verse eID="Matt.4.25"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.4"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.5" osisID="Matt.5" n="5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.1" osisID="Matt.5.1" n="1"/>When Yeshua saw a multitude of people, he ascended a mountain and sat down; the talmidim (students) came near.<verse eID="Matt.5.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.2" osisID="Matt.5.2" n="2"/>And he opened his mouth, taught them, and said,<verse eID="Matt.5.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.3" osisID="Matt.5.3" n="3"/>“Blessed are those who are poor; for their sake shall be the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.4" osisID="Matt.5.4" n="4"/>“Blessed are they who mourn, for they will be comforted.”<verse eID="Matt.5.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5" n="5"/>“Blessed are the humble, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="70" osisRef="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5!note.70">Literally, “the low.”</note>for they will inherit the world.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="71" osisRef="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5!note.71">Could also translate as, “for they will inherit the eternal.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.6" osisID="Matt.5.6" n="6"/>“Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, which they are satisfied.”<verse eID="Matt.5.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.7" osisID="Matt.5.7" n="7"/>“Blessed are those who love, for them I have compassion.”<verse eID="Matt.5.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.8" osisID="Matt.5.8" n="8"/>“Blessed are they of pure heart, for they will see Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.5.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.9" osisID="Matt.5.9" n="9"/>“Blessed are those who are makers of peace, for they shall be called the children of Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.5.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.10" osisID="Matt.5.10" n="10"/>“Blessed are those who labor in judgment, for they will inherit the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.11" osisID="Matt.5.11" n="11"/>“Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you, saying against you (leshon ha’ra)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="79" osisRef="Matt.5.11" osisID="Matt.5.11!note.79">Literally, “evil tongue” or “evil language.”</note> for the sake of my glory, to deceive.”<verse eID="Matt.5.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.12" osisID="Matt.5.12" n="12"/>“Rejoice and be happy, your reward is great in the heavens; because of me, many prophets were persecuted.”<verse eID="Matt.5.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13" n="13"/>“You are the salt of the earth, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="84" osisRef="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13!note.84">See Job 6:6. Salt adds flavor and makes food appealing. Salt is also of a covenant and sign of a covenant see 2 Chronicles 13:5. Salt was to be added to sacrifices and offerings to Elohim and represents being good; see Leviticus. 2:13, Ezekiel 43:24, Numbers 18:19, Mark 9:49- 50, Luke 14:34, Colossians 4:6.</note>but if the salt placed in a bag <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="85" osisRef="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13!note.85">See 1 Samuel 17:40b.</note>is destroyed, they throw it to the earth and isn’t advantageous, and the sons of man trample it.”<verse eID="Matt.5.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.14" osisID="Matt.5.14" n="14"/>“You are the light of the world, which is not able to hide in a high province built on a great mountain.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="86" osisRef="Matt.5.14" osisID="Matt.5.14!note.86">See Isaiah 49:6, “I will also give you as a light to the nations, that you should be my salvation to the ends of the earth.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.15" osisID="Matt.5.15" n="15"/>“And they do not light a lamp and place it under a curtain, but they light the menorah that illuminates for all the children of the house.”<verse eID="Matt.5.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.16" osisID="Matt.5.16" n="16"/>“Therefore, shine radiantly for all the eyes of man from good deeds and praising their Father in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.5.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17" n="17"/>“Do not think I came to abolish the Torah or the words of a prophet, but to establish.”,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="89" osisRef="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17!note.89">Can also mean, “uphold.”</note><note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="90" osisRef="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17!note.90">See Romans 3:31.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.18" osisID="Matt.5.18" n="18"/>“Although the heavens and the earth will cease, not even a letter or one dot from the Torah will cease, but be established.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="93" osisRef="Matt.5.18" osisID="Matt.5.18!note.93">Can also mean, “uphold.”</note>”<verse eID="Matt.5.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.19" osisID="Matt.5.19" n="19"/>“Everyone who abolishes <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="95" osisRef="Matt.5.19" osisID="Matt.5.19!note.95">The word ( יבטל ) is a Pi’el binyan with a yiqtol voice. The Pi’el is an intensifier binyan to bring emphasis. The yiqtol voice can be a simple future, a conditional future, language of the law (i.e. “you shall keep the commands of Yehovah your Elohim,”), which is a habitual action (i.e. “you shall keep”), or a past or future iterative action (Numbers 11:4-6). In this case, the yiqtol voice appears to be a conditional language of the law.</note>one small commandment that is in the Torah, and if he shall teach others in this way, he shall be called a little one in the kingdom of heaven; and everyone who establishes them, and if he teaches others, shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.20" osisID="Matt.5.20" n="20"/>“I say to you, that they will not add in judgment more than is in the scroll or scribes, but by this,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="96" osisRef="Matt.5.20" osisID="Matt.5.20!note.96">Thus, accordingly, by this standard set.</note> you shall be found worthy to ascend to the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.5.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21" n="21"/>“You heard it formerly <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="97" osisRef="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21!note.97">From ancient times.</note>said, “You shall not murder,” <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="98" osisRef="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21!note.98">Exodus 20:13; Deuteronomy 5:17.</note>and “whoever murders, commits a penalty of death by the Beit Din (house of judgment).”<verse eID="Matt.5.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.22" osisID="Matt.5.22" n="22"/>“I say to you, everyone who is angry regarding his brother and despises him, is guilty by the Beit Din, and everyone who says regarding his brother, ‘Worthless!’ or ‘Wicked!’ is condemned to the punishment of lashes by the Beit Din. Then whoever says, ‘Madman!’ to his friend, goes down to Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.23" osisID="Matt.5.23" n="23"/>“When you bring your offering, and there you remember that your brother has a matter of fault against you,”<verse eID="Matt.5.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.24" osisID="Matt.5.24" n="24"/>“then you leave your offering on the altar and go seek pardon from your brother, and afterwards, offer your offering.”<verse eID="Matt.5.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25" n="25"/>“You shall yearn to reconcile with your accuser <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="101" osisRef="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25!note.101">This word ( דינך ) is a legal term indicating a court of law and a legal case presented by an accuser and is usually seen in poetry. See Jeremiah 30:13.</note>while <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="102" osisRef="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25!note.102">Literally, “while he is with him.”</note>with him on the way, before he delivers you to the judge, and the judge delivers you to the officer, and you fall into the house of prisoners.”<verse eID="Matt.5.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.26" osisID="Matt.5.26" n="26"/>“Truly, I say to you, you will not be able to go out from there until you give even the last eighth.”<verse eID="Matt.5.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.27" osisID="Matt.5.27" n="27"/>“You have heard what was written, ‘You shall not commit adultery,’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="103" osisRef="Matt.5.27" osisID="Matt.5.27!note.103">Exodus 20:14, Deuteronomy 5:18.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.28" osisID="Matt.5.28" n="28"/>“I, I say to you, everyone who looked at a woman and lusted after her and is caught in adultery in his heart.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="104" osisRef="Matt.5.28" osisID="Matt.5.28!note.104">See Leviticus 19:17. The Torah commands were always commands of the heart.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.29" osisID="Matt.5.29" n="29"/>“If your right eye causes you to fail, remove it and throw it from you, for it is better that you lose one member from you, and not all your body in Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.30" osisID="Matt.5.30" n="30"/>“And if your right-hand stumbles you, cut and throw it away. For it is good for you to destroy one member of your body, and not all your body is to Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.31" osisID="Matt.5.31" n="31"/>“It is said, ‘whoever divorces his wife, will give her a Get of divorce.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="106" osisRef="Matt.5.31" osisID="Matt.5.31!note.106">See Deuteronomy 24:1–4.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.32" osisID="Matt.5.32" n="32"/>“I say to you, everyone who sends away his wife, except for fornication, is the maker for her of adultery, and also whoever marries her, the divorcee, commits adultery.”<verse eID="Matt.5.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33" n="33"/>“Again, you have heard those of old who said, ‘You shall not support <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="108" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.108">Can mean to “profess” or “support.”</note>a false oath, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="109" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.109">Compare to Leviticus 19:12; Numbers 30:2; Deuteronomy 23:21.</note>but you shall fulfill it to the owner of your oath.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="110" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.110">Leviticus 9:12, Numbers 30:2, Deuteronomy 23:21.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.34" osisID="Matt.5.34" n="34"/>“I say, do not swear by the heavens before the throne of Elohim (God),”<verse eID="Matt.5.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.35" osisID="Matt.5.35" n="35"/>“and not by the earth, because it is the footstool of His feet, and neither by Yerushalim (Jerusalem), because it is the land belonging to the King of the world.”<verse eID="Matt.5.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.36" osisID="Matt.5.36" n="36"/>“you should not swear by your head, for you are unable to make one hair either black or white.”<verse eID="Matt.5.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.37" osisID="Matt.5.37" n="37"/>“However, let your words be concerning, ‘Yes, yes,’ and concerning, ‘No, no;’ and if it passes from this, it is evil.”<verse eID="Matt.5.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.38" osisID="Matt.5.38" n="38"/>“You have heard what was said, ‘An eye in exchange for an eye, A tooth in exchange for a tooth.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="113" osisRef="Matt.5.38" osisID="Matt.5.38!note.113">Exodus 21:23–24; Leviticus 24:19–20; Deuteronomy 19:21.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.39" osisID="Matt.5.39" n="39"/>“I, I say to you, do not rise up to do evil, but whoever strikes upon one’s right cheek, shall also show him the left.”<verse eID="Matt.5.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.40" osisID="Matt.5.40" n="40"/>“And whoever wants to sentence you to take your turban, leave it to him, even your undershirt!”<verse eID="Matt.5.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.41" osisID="Matt.5.41" n="41"/>“Whoever wants you to walk a parasang, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="116" osisRef="Matt.5.41" osisID="Matt.5.41!note.116">“A parasang is a length or measurement of distance used in what is now Iran. It varied according to the region. The north-eastern parasang was about 15,000 paces; the north-western parasang was 18,000 paces, and the one of the southwest was merely 6,000 paces. The measurement called the “true parasang” was about 9,000 paces.” The Books of Enoch: The Angels, The Watchers and The Nephilim, pp. 225. See 3 Enoch 5: The Hebrew Book of Enoch for the use of the word, “parasang.” For 3 Enoch is believed to have been written around c. 100 CE.</note>walk with him two parasangs.<verse eID="Matt.5.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.42" osisID="Matt.5.42" n="42"/>“To whoever that asks a question, you must give to him; or whoever borrows from you, do not withhold it.”<verse eID="Matt.5.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.43" osisID="Matt.5.43" n="43"/>“You have heard that they said, ‘For you love your neighbor and hate your haters.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="119" osisRef="Matt.5.43" osisID="Matt.5.43!note.119">See Leviticus 19:18.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.44" osisID="Matt.5.44" n="44"/>“I say to you, ‘Have mercy on your enemies, and bless whoever curses you, and do good to those cursing you, and pray for those who memorialize to do evils and persecution;’”<verse eID="Matt.5.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.45" osisID="Matt.5.45" n="45"/>“and then you will be children of your Father in the heavens, who created the sun over good and over bad, and also brings down rain on good and bad, and the righteous and the wicked.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="120" osisRef="Matt.5.45" osisID="Matt.5.45!note.120">See Exodus 23:4-5 when Elohim (God) commanded His people to be merciful to their enemies.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.46" osisID="Matt.5.46" n="46"/>“And if you love those who love you, what reward do you have? For you are like the children of the market doing this!<verse eID="Matt.5.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.47" osisID="Matt.5.47" n="47"/>“And if they ask in peace of your brothers only, what good is there in you? Even the children of marketplaces do so!”<verse eID="Matt.5.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.48" osisID="Matt.5.48" n="48"/>“You, accordingly, will be complete, as your Father in heaven who is complete.<verse eID="Matt.5.48"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.5"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.6" osisID="Matt.6" n="6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.1" osisID="Matt.6.1" n="1"/>You shall see when you give you charity, to not give it before man, because if they see, there will not be a reward for you from your Father in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.6.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.2" osisID="Matt.6.2" n="2"/>When doing righteousness, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="121" osisRef="Matt.6.2" osisID="Matt.6.2!note.121">See Deuteronomy 6:25 and Psalms 119:172 for walking out the commands of Elohim. © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>do not announce in the streets and markets in order to be praised before the people, or you will lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.3" osisID="Matt.6.3" n="3"/>However, when you do righteousness, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="122" osisRef="Matt.6.3" osisID="Matt.6.3!note.122">See Deuteronomy 6:25. © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>it should be in secret as such: do not let even your left hand know the right hand is doing,<verse eID="Matt.6.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.4" osisID="Matt.6.4" n="4"/>so your righteousness is in secret. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="123" osisRef="Matt.6.4" osisID="Matt.6.4!note.123">See Romans 11:21-31 and 1 Corinthians 4:6-8 and proverbs 16:18. For in secret, pride and ego is prevented from being inflated in the public. © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>Then Elohim (God), who sees in secret, shall reward you by openly revealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.5" osisID="Matt.6.5" n="5"/>And when you pray, your prayer should not be short-term like those who are accustomed to stand and pray in the markets and streets before the eyes of the people, for you will lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.6" osisID="Matt.6.6" n="6"/>However, when you pray, enter your house and close your doors and pray to your hidden Elohim (God). And He who sees in secret, rewards you by openly revealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.7" osisID="Matt.6.7" n="7"/>And whoever is praying, do not speak like the hypocrites who speak much in order to be heard.<verse eID="Matt.6.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.8" osisID="Matt.6.8" n="8"/>Don’t imitate them, for your Father who is in heaven answers you before you ask Him.<verse eID="Matt.6.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.9" osisID="Matt.6.9" n="9"/>But, therefore, pray as so to your Father, and His name shall be sanctified.<verse eID="Matt.6.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.10" osisID="Matt.6.10" n="10"/>“May your kingdom be true, certain and approved, beneath the heavens and also on the earth.”<verse eID="Matt.6.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.11" osisID="Matt.6.11" n="11"/>“Give us food of the word <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="127" osisRef="Matt.6.11" osisID="Matt.6.11!note.127">Can also mean, “Give us food of the matter…” See Jeremiah 3:15; 15:16. © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>daily in its day.”<verse eID="Matt.6.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.12" osisID="Matt.6.12" n="12"/>“And forgive us of our debts, as we forgive our debtors.”<verse eID="Matt.6.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13" n="13"/>“Do not bring us to a trial <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="128" osisRef="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13!note.128">See Deuteronomy 6:16.</note>but rescue us from all evil. For it is your kingdom, and your power, and your glory forever and ever.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="129" osisRef="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13!note.129">See Psalms 145:1 for the phrase לעולם ועד - “forever and ever.” © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note><verse eID="Matt.6.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.14" osisID="Matt.6.14" n="14"/>If you forgive others, HaShem (Yehovah) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="130" osisRef="Matt.6.14" osisID="Matt.6.14!note.130">The Cochin Hebrew Matthew has the rabbinic phrase “HaShem” meaning, “the Name” (i.e. Yehovah) such as in Matthew 6:14, 6:15, 6:26, 7:11. The Cochin Hebrew Matthew also includes the hey gershyim (ה)֞ abbreviation also meaning “Yehovah” as shown in Matthew 3:3, 4:7, 4:10, or the abbreviation of ׳יי as is in Matthew 4:4 appears to be from different scribes. However, in the First Century, the name of Yehovah, but was banned sometime between 130-138 c. CE; Babylonian Talmud, Avodah Zarah 17b-18a and was spoken by the disciples. Therefore, these findings indicate different scribes with different traditions to replace the name of Yehovah. © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>will forgive you of your sins.”<verse eID="Matt.6.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.15" osisID="Matt.6.15" n="15"/>But if you do not forgive sons of man, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="131" osisRef="Matt.6.15" osisID="Matt.6.15!note.131">Also means, “human being.” © copyright 2025 Janice F. Baca</note>HaShem (Yehovah) will not forgive you of your sins.<verse eID="Matt.6.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.16" osisID="Matt.6.16" n="16"/>When you are afflicted, do not see yourselves as hungry, but change your faces in front of people to demonstrate being tortured, and lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.17" osisID="Matt.6.17" n="17"/>When you fast, your face and your head are washed,<verse eID="Matt.6.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.18" osisID="Matt.6.18" n="18"/>so that sons of man do not see you are in a fast, but your Father who is hidden and sees in secret, shall repay you.<verse eID="Matt.6.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.19" osisID="Matt.6.19" n="19"/>Do not set aside for yourselves hidden treasures on the earth, in a place of worms consuming and destroying, and as well as thieves sneaking in and stealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.20" osisID="Matt.6.20" n="20"/>But place your hidden treasures in the heavens where there are no worms to consume, and a place that thieves do not sneak in and steal.<verse eID="Matt.6.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.21" osisID="Matt.6.21" n="21"/>From the place which your treasures are, there will be your desire.<verse eID="Matt.6.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.22" osisID="Matt.6.22" n="22"/>The lamp of your body is the eye; and if your eye is confident, all your body will be a light.<verse eID="Matt.6.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.23" osisID="Matt.6.23" n="23"/>If your eye is wicked, all your body will be dark; therefore, if this light that is in you is dark, everything will be dark.<verse eID="Matt.6.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.24" osisID="Matt.6.24" n="24"/>No man will be able to serve two masters, for one he will hate and love another, or one he will value one or despise the other one; and you are not able to serve, likewise, Elohim (God) and mammon.<verse eID="Matt.6.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.25" osisID="Matt.6.25" n="25"/>And thus, I tell you to not loathe yourselves, saying, “What shall I eat, and what shall I drink?” nor for your body likewise, “What will you wear:” but is not the soul greater than mammon and your body than clothing?”<verse eID="Matt.6.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.26" osisID="Matt.6.26" n="26"/>And you will see the birds of the heavens: they do not sow, nor harvest, and do not have treasure in the storehouse; but HaShem (Yehovah) provides for them. Are you not abundantly above them?<verse eID="Matt.6.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.27" osisID="Matt.6.27" n="27"/>Who among you that by wearying are able to add onto his height one cubit?<verse eID="Matt.6.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.28" osisID="Matt.6.28" n="28"/>And if you loathe about clothing, you should look at the flowers of the wilderness, how they grow, which is not caused by fatigue.<verse eID="Matt.6.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.29" osisID="Matt.6.29" n="29"/>I tell you, even Shelomoh (Solomon), the king in all his splendor, was not clothed as one of them.<verse eID="Matt.6.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.30" osisID="Matt.6.30" n="30"/>If this grain of the field, that exists today and tomorrow, falls into the furnace, Elohim (God) sufficiently clothes. May not be the same for you, you of little faith?<verse eID="Matt.6.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.31" osisID="Matt.6.31" n="31"/>Do not fret by saying, “What will we eat?” or “What will we drink?” or “What will we be clothed?”<verse eID="Matt.6.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.32" osisID="Matt.6.32" n="32"/>All this the peoples of the lands seek. But your Father, who is in the heavens, knows you that you seek all this.<verse eID="Matt.6.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.33" osisID="Matt.6.33" n="33"/>You should request this from the Lord of the heavens, for it is his righteousness; and therefore, it will be added to you.<verse eID="Matt.6.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.34" osisID="Matt.6.34" n="34"/>You should not dread about the tomorrows, because tomorrow will have its own worry, and enough for a person is the evil of the day.”<verse eID="Matt.6.34"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.6"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.7" osisID="Matt.7" n="7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.1" osisID="Matt.7.1" n="1"/>You shall not judge, so you will not be judged.<verse eID="Matt.7.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.2" osisID="Matt.7.2" n="2"/>Just as you judge your friend, it will be judged to you, and with the measurement that you measure: with it, you will be corrected.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="144" osisRef="Matt.7.2" osisID="Matt.7.2!note.144">Or, “be set straight.”</note><verse eID="Matt.7.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.3" osisID="Matt.7.3" n="3"/>But you see the chip of wood in the eye of your friend, but the beam that is in your eye, you do not see?<verse eID="Matt.7.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.4" osisID="Matt.7.4" n="4"/>How can you say to your brother, “Take the chip of wood from between your tooth,” but here is a beam between your eye?<verse eID="Matt.7.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.5" osisID="Matt.7.5" n="5"/>You denier! Remove the beam from between your eyes beforehand, and later remove the chip of wood that is between the eyes of your brother.<verse eID="Matt.7.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.6" osisID="Matt.7.6" n="6"/>Do not throw a holy matter <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="149" osisRef="Matt.7.6" osisID="Matt.7.6!note.149">Can also translate to, “Do not throw a holy word before the dogs.”</note>before the dogs, and do not throw pearls before the pigs, for it may be that they trample them with their feet and in turn, injure you.<verse eID="Matt.7.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.7" osisID="Matt.7.7" n="7"/>Ask and I will give to you, seek and you will find it, and be strong <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="153" osisRef="Matt.7.7" osisID="Matt.7.7!note.153">“The root word קשה (Kashe) is an adjective that means, ‘hard’ or ‘difficult.’ It is often used to describe situations, tasks, or conditions that are challenging or tough to deal with.” See website for more details: https://talkpal.ai/vocabulary/%d7%a7%d7%a9%d7%94-kashe-vs- %d7%97%d7%96%d7%a7-chazak-hard-vs-strong-in-hebrew/.</note>at the entrance and I will open it for you.<verse eID="Matt.7.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8" n="8"/>So, everyone who asks, receives, and everyone who seeks, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="154" osisRef="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8!note.154">See 1 Chronicles 16:11.</note>finds. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="155" osisRef="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8!note.155">This literally translates to, “he/it was found.” This could have been meant to be an active participle מֹוצֵא (motze) meaning, he is found. This word could have been spelled as an active participle with or without the vav mater lectionis (consonants acting as vowel sounds).</note>And everyone who knocks, it is opened.<verse eID="Matt.7.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.9" osisID="Matt.7.9" n="9"/>What kind of man is he from among you, whose son asks him for bread, provides him a stone?<verse eID="Matt.7.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.10" osisID="Matt.7.10" n="10"/>And if he asks for a fish, would he provide him a snake?<verse eID="Matt.7.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.11" osisID="Matt.7.11" n="11"/>And even if you being evil know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more does HaShem (Yehovah)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="156" osisRef="Matt.7.11" osisID="Matt.7.11!note.156">Rabbinical term to replace the name of Yehovah after the ban of Yehovah’s name began around 130 CE.</note> give good gifts to His askers?<verse eID="Matt.7.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.12" osisID="Matt.7.12" n="12"/>Just as you desire that people do your will, thus you should do their desire; for the prophets commanded so.<verse eID="Matt.7.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.13" osisID="Matt.7.13" n="13"/>Enter into the narrow gate, to the wide opened door, and in the path is a spacious place for those who are lost go; for many men go there.<verse eID="Matt.7.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.14" osisID="Matt.7.14" n="14"/>How small is the gate and narrow path that leads to life, and few are they that are found there?<verse eID="Matt.7.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.15" osisID="Matt.7.15" n="15"/>Beware of the false prophets <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="157" osisRef="Matt.7.15" osisID="Matt.7.15!note.157">For information on false prophets, see Deuteronomy 13:1-5, 18:20-22, Jeremiah 23:16-32, and Ezekiel 13:1-9.</note>who come near to you in clothes of wool on the outside but inside are full of trickeries of war.<verse eID="Matt.7.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.16" osisID="Matt.7.16" n="16"/>From their fruits you will know them; for how can they gather grapes from thorns, and figs from thistles?<verse eID="Matt.7.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.17" osisID="Matt.7.17" n="17"/>Therefore, there is a good tree and its fruits are good; and a bad <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="158" osisRef="Matt.7.17" osisID="Matt.7.17!note.158">Can also mean, “wicked.”</note>tree, its fruits are bad.<verse eID="Matt.7.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.18" osisID="Matt.7.18" n="18"/>A good tree cannot be found having bad fruits, and neither can a bad tree have good fruits.<verse eID="Matt.7.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.19" osisID="Matt.7.19" n="19"/>Every tree that does not produce good fruit, they cut it down and throw into the fire.<verse eID="Matt.7.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.20" osisID="Matt.7.20" n="20"/>And from their fruit, you will know them.<verse eID="Matt.7.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.21" osisID="Matt.7.21" n="21"/>Not everyone <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="161" osisRef="Matt.7.21" osisID="Matt.7.21!note.161">Literally, “Not all who…”</note>who says to me, “My Master, my Master,” will ascend to the heavens, but it is whoever does the desire of my Father in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.7.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.22" osisID="Matt.7.22" n="22"/>Many will say on the day, “My Master, my Master — but did we not prophesy in your name, and in your name drive out demons, and in your name do many extraordinary things?”<verse eID="Matt.7.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.23" osisID="Matt.7.23" n="23"/>Then I will acknowledge them with an oath, saying, “I have not known you, but be removed from me, you workers of iniquity!”<verse eID="Matt.7.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.24" osisID="Matt.7.24" n="24"/>Everyone who hears these words from me and does accordingly, he is like a wise man and builds his house upon the hills.<verse eID="Matt.7.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.25" osisID="Matt.7.25" n="25"/>And its rain descended, and rivers came, and wind blew to destroy it (the house). But it did not fall because its foundation was marked upon the hills.<verse eID="Matt.7.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.26" osisID="Matt.7.26" n="26"/>And everyone who hears and does not do them is like a fool who built his house on sand.<verse eID="Matt.7.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.27" osisID="Matt.7.27" n="27"/>And rain descended, and a river came, then the wind blew and caused destruction, and the house fell, and it was a great fall.<verse eID="Matt.7.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.28" osisID="Matt.7.28" n="28"/>When the words of Yeshua were completed, all the people were astonished over the teaching.<verse eID="Matt.7.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.29" osisID="Matt.7.29" n="29"/>Because he taught them as an authority, and not like the scribes and sages.<verse eID="Matt.7.29"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.7"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.8" osisID="Matt.8" n="8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.1" osisID="Matt.8.1" n="1"/>When this one descended from the mountain, a vast people surrounded him. The scriptures: And when He came down from the mountain, large crowds followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.8.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.2" osisID="Matt.8.2" n="2"/>Then came one leper who came and bowed down and said, “My Teacher, if you desire, you are able to purify me. The scriptures: And see, a leper came, and bowed before Him, saying, “Master, if You desire, You are able to make me clean.”<verse eID="Matt.8.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.3" osisID="Matt.8.3" n="3"/>And he straightened out his hand and said, “I desire to purify you. Then within the hour, he was purified.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="165" osisRef="Matt.8.3" osisID="Matt.8.3!note.165">See Exodus 4:6 and also see the Syrian who came to the prophet Elisha for the healing of the skin issue in 2 Kings 5:15.</note> immediately his leprosy was cleansed.<verse eID="Matt.8.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.4" osisID="Matt.8.4" n="4"/>And Yeshua said to him, “See that you do not tell any man, but go to the priest and offer your sacrifice according to Moses in the Torah.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="166" osisRef="Matt.8.4" osisID="Matt.8.4!note.166">Leviticus 14:1-32.</note> the priest, and offer the gift that Mosheh commanded, as a witness to them.”<verse eID="Matt.8.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.5" osisID="Matt.8.5" n="5"/>After this, Yeshua went up to Kephar Nahum, and one official approached him and asked him and said,<verse eID="Matt.8.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.6" osisID="Matt.8.6" n="6"/>“My Lord, my boy has fallen in my house and became a madman and is laid down to die.” The scriptures: saying, “Master, my servant is lying in the house paralysed, grievously tortured.”<verse eID="Matt.8.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.7" osisID="Matt.8.7" n="7"/>Yeshua said to him, “I will come to heal him.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="167" osisRef="Matt.8.7" osisID="Matt.8.7!note.167">See Isaiah 35:6.</note><verse eID="Matt.8.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.8" osisID="Matt.8.8" n="8"/>The man replied to him and said, “My teacher, it is not my desire that you come under my rafter, but rather it is enough that you say one word and the boy will recover.” The scriptures: And the captain answering, said, “Master, I am not worthy that You should come under my roof. But only say a word, and my servant shall be healed.<verse eID="Matt.8.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.9" osisID="Matt.8.9" n="9"/>“Moreso, I that have mamona <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="168" osisRef="Matt.8.9" osisID="Matt.8.9!note.168">In Matthew 6:24 and 6:24, mammon, meaning, “money,” is spelled ממון (mammon). However, in Matthew 8:9 it is spelled as ממונה (mamona). Both words are Aramaic words but could be implying different meanings due to the spelling differences, or it could be the differences between writings of different scribes.</note>(money) from the kingdom, and in the shadow of my rafter, there are people. So, if I say to them, ‘You go,’ and they go. And to another I say, ‘Come,’ and he comes. And to my servant I say, ‘Do accordingly,’ as he does.” The scriptures: For I too am a man under authority, having soldiers under me. And I say to this one, ‘Go,’ and he goes, and to another, ‘Come,’ and he comes, and to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does it.<verse eID="Matt.8.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.10" osisID="Matt.8.10" n="10"/>When Yeshua heard, he was amazed and said to those with him, “Truth I say to you, that even with the children of Israel, there does not exist faith like this!” you, not even in Yisra’ĕl have I found such great belief! Now, when Eshu {Yeshua} heard this, He was amazed, and said unto those that were coming with Him, “Amiyn {Truly} I say unto you, that not even in IysraIyl {Israel} have I found Haymanutha {Faith} like this!<verse eID="Matt.8.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.11" osisID="Matt.8.11" n="11"/>“Yet, I say that many have come from the east and from the west to lean upon the table with Avraham (Abraham), and Yitzhaq (Isaac), Ya’akov (Jacob) in the kingdom of heaven.” The scriptures: “And I say to you that many shall come from east and west, and sit down with Abṟaham, and Yitsḥaq, and Ya‛aqoḇ in the reign of the heavens,<verse eID="Matt.8.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.12" osisID="Matt.8.12" n="12"/>Children of this kingdom will be expelled into darkness outside, and there they will be weeping and gnashing<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="170" osisRef="Matt.8.12" osisID="Matt.8.12!note.170">See Psalms 35:16; 37:12; 112:10, and Lamentations 2:16.</note> their teeth. The scriptures: but the sons of the reign shall be cast out into outer darkness – there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.8.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.13" osisID="Matt.8.13" n="13"/>And Yeshua said to the man, “You go; just as you believe, it will be for you.” And in that hour, the boy was healed. And his servant was healed that hour.<verse eID="Matt.8.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.14" osisID="Matt.8.14" n="14"/>And afterwards, Yeshua came to the house of Shim’on (Simeon), who is called Pedro (Peter). And he saw his mother-in-law hurled and seized with a fever. sick with inflammation.<verse eID="Matt.8.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.15" osisID="Matt.8.15" n="15"/>And he grasped her hand, and the fever went from her. Then she rose and served them. The scriptures: And He touched her hand, and the inflammation left her. And she arose and served them.<verse eID="Matt.8.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.16" osisID="Matt.8.16" n="16"/>When it was night, they drew near to him people who were epileptic by demons and expelled them by a whisper. And all of those who had something bad, he also healed, The scriptures: And when evening had come, they brought to Him many who were demon-possessed. And He cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick,<verse eID="Matt.8.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.17" osisID="Matt.8.17" n="17"/>so as to fulfill the saying of the prophet Yeshayah (Isaiah): “And he borne our pains and our sicknesses.” The scriptures: that it might be filled what was spoken by Yeshayahu the prophet, saying, “He Himself took our weaknesses and bore our sicknesses.”<verse eID="Matt.8.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.18" osisID="Matt.8.18" n="18"/>When Yeshua saw that many people gathered together around him, he commanded them to go to the other side. other side.<verse eID="Matt.8.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.19" osisID="Matt.8.19" n="19"/>Then one sage beside him and said to him, “Rabbi, wherever you go, I will follow you.” The scriptures: And a certain scribe, having come near, said to Him, “Teacher, I shall follow You wherever You go.”<verse eID="Matt.8.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.20" osisID="Matt.8.20" n="20"/>Yeshua said to him, “The foxes have holes, and the birds of the heavens have nests, but this Son of man has no place that he can rest his head.” Son of Ada̱m has nowhere to lay His head.”<verse eID="Matt.8.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.21" osisID="Matt.8.21" n="21"/>One of his talmidim (students) said to him, “My teacher, give me permission to go bury my father first.” The scriptures: And another of His taught ones said to Him, “Master, first let me go and bury my father.”<verse eID="Matt.8.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.22" osisID="Matt.8.22" n="22"/>In that hour, Yeshua said to him, “You are to come follow me and leave the dead, and the dead will bury him.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="178" osisRef="Matt.8.22" osisID="Matt.8.22!note.178">See Ephesian 2:1-2.</note><verse eID="Matt.8.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.23" osisID="Matt.8.23" n="23"/>And when Yeshua entered in the ship, his talmidim (students) were with him. The scriptures: And when He entered into a boat, His taught ones followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.8.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.24" osisID="Matt.8.24" n="24"/>And behold, the wind of a storm was in the sea until the ship was going to sink from the waves of the sea ‒ but Yeshua was asleep.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="179" osisRef="Matt.8.24" osisID="Matt.8.24!note.179">Also see Jonah 1:4-5.</note> The scriptures: And see, a great gale arose on the sea, so that the boat was covered by the waves. But He was sleeping.<verse eID="Matt.8.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.25" osisID="Matt.8.25" n="25"/>And his talmidim (students) woke him and said, “Our Lord, behold, we are perishing!” The scriptures: And His taught ones came to Him and woke Him up, saying, “Master, save us! We are perishing!” and His Disciples approached Him, woke Him, and said unto Him, “Maran {Our Lord}, deliver us, we are perishing!”<verse eID="Matt.8.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.26" osisID="Matt.8.26" n="26"/>Yeshua said to them, “Why are you afraid, who are of little faith?” Then he rose and rebuked the wind and the sea, and it calmed from its tempest. The scriptures: And He said to them, “Why are you afraid, O you of little belief?” Then, having risen, He rebuked the winds and the sea. And there was a great calm.<verse eID="Matt.8.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.27" osisID="Matt.8.27" n="27"/>These people were astonished and said, “Who is he that the wind and the sea listen to him?” The scriptures: And the men marvelled, saying, “What is this, that even the winds and the sea obey Him?”<verse eID="Matt.8.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.28" osisID="Matt.8.28" n="28"/>And after Yeshua came to cross over to that side, two sorcerers met him, who went out from among the tombs, so much evil that it was not possible for any people to pass that same path. The scriptures: And when He came to the other side, to the country of the Girgashites, two demon- possessed ones met Him, coming out of the tombs, very fierce, so that no one was able to pass that way.<verse eID="Matt.8.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.29" osisID="Matt.8.29" n="29"/>And they shouted and said, “What do we have to do with you, Yeshua, Son of Elohim (God)? You came here to irritate us before the appointed time?” Have You come here to torture us, before the appointed time?”<verse eID="Matt.8.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.30" osisID="Matt.8.30" n="30"/>And at that remote place from them, there was a stable of many pigs and also herdsmen. The scriptures: And at a distance from them there was a herd of many pigs feeding,<verse eID="Matt.8.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.31" osisID="Matt.8.31" n="31"/>And those demons begged him and said, “If you expel us from this place, we will go in the nearby pigs.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="181" osisRef="Matt.8.31" osisID="Matt.8.31!note.181">Pigs are considered unclean and an abomination in Torah. See Leviticus 7:21.</note> The scriptures: and the demons begged Him, saying, “If You cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs.”<verse eID="Matt.8.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.32" osisID="Matt.8.32" n="32"/>And Yeshua said to them, “You, go!” And after they left, they went in the nearby pigs to the stable, and the herdsmen ran to encompass them, but they fell in the sea and died in the water. The scriptures: And He said to them, “Go.” And they, coming out, went into the herd of pigs. And see, the entire herd of pigs rushed down the steep place into the sea, and died in the water.<verse eID="Matt.8.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.33" osisID="Matt.8.33" n="33"/>Those of the herdsmen that escaped and left for the province then told all that occurred and the matters of the adversaries (satans). The scriptures: And the herdsmen fled, and went away into the city and reported all this, and about those possessed by demons.<verse eID="Matt.8.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.34" osisID="Matt.8.34" n="34"/>And all the children of the city went out to Yeshua, and when they saw him, they asked of him that he would turn from their area. Him to leave their borders.<verse eID="Matt.8.34"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.8"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.9" osisID="Matt.9" n="9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.1" osisID="Matt.9.1" n="1"/>And he entered into the ship and passed over to its side, and he came to a province, and they brought to him one madman lying on a bed. The scriptures: And entering into a boat, He passed over, and came to His own city.<verse eID="Matt.9.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.2" osisID="Matt.9.2" n="2"/>And Yeshua saw their faith, which they believed in him and said to him with gout, “It is healed, my son, and he will forgive your sins.” belief, said to the paralytic, “Take courage, son, your sins have been forgiven.”<verse eID="Matt.9.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.3" osisID="Matt.9.3" n="3"/>Some people, they were thinking in their heart, “He blasphemes!” The scriptures: And see, some of the scribes said to themselves, “This One blasphemes!”<verse eID="Matt.9.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.4" osisID="Matt.9.4" n="4"/>Yeshua knew their thoughts and said to them, “Why are you thinking evil in your heart?” hearts?<verse eID="Matt.9.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.5" osisID="Matt.9.5" n="5"/>Which is more appropriate to say, “’Your sins have been forgiven you?’ or to say, ‘Rise, go’”? The scriptures: “For which is easier, to say, ‘Your sins have been forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Arise and walk’?<verse eID="Matt.9.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.6" osisID="Matt.9.6" n="6"/>And you will know that the Son of man has power on the earth to forgiven sins. Then he said to him who was sick, “Rise, take your bed and go to your house!” The scriptures: “But in order for you to know that the Son of Ada̱m possesses authority on earth to forgive sins...” He then said to the paralytic, “Rise, take up your bed, and go to your house.”<verse eID="Matt.9.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.7" osisID="Matt.9.7" n="7"/>Then he rose and ran to his house. The scriptures: And he rose and went to his house.<verse eID="Matt.9.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.8" osisID="Matt.9.8" n="8"/>When the community saw this, they were frightened and praised El (God) who gave such power to the son of man. The scriptures: And when the crowds saw it, they marvelled and praised Elohim who had given such authority to men.<verse eID="Matt.9.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.9" osisID="Matt.9.9" n="9"/>And when Yeshua went from there, he saw one man in the tax house, and his name was Mattai (Matthew); he said to him, “Follow me.” Then he rose and followed him. office. And He said to him, “Follow Me.” And he rose and followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.9.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.10" osisID="Matt.9.10" n="10"/>And when Yeshua was sitting at the table with his talmidim (students) in his house, people from the tax house came, and many sinners with them. Then they reclined at the table with Yeshua and his talmidim (students). and sinners came and sat down with Him and His taught ones.<verse eID="Matt.9.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.11" osisID="Matt.9.11" n="11"/>When the Pharisees saw, they said to the talmidim (students), “Why with evil people and sinners, does your rabbi eat?” The scriptures: And when the Pharisees saw it, they said to His taught ones, “Why does your Teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?”<verse eID="Matt.9.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.12" osisID="Matt.9.12" n="12"/>When Yeshua heard this, he said to them, “The healthy do not need healing, but those who are sick.” physician, but those who are sick.<verse eID="Matt.9.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.13" osisID="Matt.9.13" n="13"/>Go and learn from me that, “I desire their goodwill and not sacrifice and offering.” I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners.” The scriptures: But go and learn what this means, ‘I desire compassion and not offering.’ For I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but sinners.<verse eID="Matt.9.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.14" osisID="Matt.9.14" n="14"/>Afterwards, the talmidim (students) of Yochanan (John) drew near to him and said, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast, but your talmidim (students) do not fast?” The scriptures: Then the taught ones of Yoḥanan came to Him, saying, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but Your taught ones do not fast?”<verse eID="Matt.9.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.15" osisID="Matt.9.15" n="15"/>Yeshua said to them, “There are none of the sons of man in happiness with the bridegroom that are fasting, for the bridegroom is with them. However, when the bridegroom goes, then they are fasting.” bridegroom is with them? But the days shall come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they shall fast.<verse eID="Matt.9.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.16" osisID="Matt.9.16" n="16"/>There is no man who puts a new patch on old clothing, because the new patch is torn loose from the old garment. The scriptures: “And no one puts a piece of unshrunk cloth on an old garment, for the patch pulls away from the garment, and the tear is made worse.<verse eID="Matt.9.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.17" osisID="Matt.9.17" n="17"/>And no man puts wine into old skins, so that the skin does not tear, and the wine pours out. But they put new wine into a new skin, and by this the two of them are preserved.” The scriptures: “Neither do they put new wine into old wineskins, or else the wineskins burst, and the wine is spilled, and the wineskins are ruined. But they put new wine into fresh wineskins, and both are preserved.”<verse eID="Matt.9.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.18" osisID="Matt.9.18" n="18"/>And he was still speaking with them, and behold, an important man came and bowed down to him, and said, “Behold, my daughter is declining to death. Come to place your hand upon her so she will live.” The scriptures: While He was saying all this to them, see, a ruler came and bowed down to Him, saying, “My daughter is dead by now, but come and lay Your hand on her and she shall live.”<verse eID="Matt.9.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.19" osisID="Matt.9.19" n="19"/>Then Yeshua rose, and his talmidim (students) left following him.<verse eID="Matt.9.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.20" osisID="Matt.9.20" n="20"/>And behold, a woman who had blood going out of her for twelve years. Then, when he came, she came from behind him to touch his garment. The scriptures: And see, a woman who had a flow of blood for twelve years came from behind and touched the tzitzit of His garment.<verse eID="Matt.9.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.21" osisID="Matt.9.21" n="21"/>She,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="184" osisRef="Matt.9.21" osisID="Matt.9.21!note.184">An unusual added pronoun included in the sentence. Either this is for emphasis, or it was an error. However, the pronoun is not usually included as the pronoun is part of the verb morphology.</note> she thought in her heart, “If I only take hold of his garment, I will be healed.” The scriptures: For she said to herself, “If I only touch His garment, I shall be healed.”<verse eID="Matt.9.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.22" osisID="Matt.9.22" n="22"/>This Yeshua turned, and saw, and said to her, “My daughter, may you be fulfilled <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="186" osisRef="Matt.9.22" osisID="Matt.9.22!note.186">This appears to be used like a Jussive but is in second person. A jussive is like an imperative but in third person and begins with “let</note>for your emunah (faith) has brought you down here.” And the woman was healed in that hour. has healed you.” And the woman was healed from that hour.<verse eID="Matt.9.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.23" osisID="Matt.9.23" n="23"/>And Yeshua came to the house of that important man and saw them wailing with a sect of lamenters. making a noise,<verse eID="Matt.9.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.24" osisID="Matt.9.24" n="24"/>Then he said to them, “Get away from the virgin, for she has not died, but she is sleeping.” And they laughed at him. The scriptures: He said to them, “Go back, for the girl is not dead, but sleeping.” And they laughed at Him.<verse eID="Matt.9.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.25" osisID="Matt.9.25" n="25"/>And when he entered with them, he grabbed her by her hands and the daughter rose up. The scriptures: But when the crowd was put outside, He went in and took her by the hand, and the girl arose.<verse eID="Matt.9.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.26" osisID="Matt.9.26" n="26"/>And the rumor went all through the land. The scriptures: And this report went out into all that land.<verse eID="Matt.9.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.27" osisID="Matt.9.27" n="27"/>And when Yeshua came from there, two blind men chased after him, shouting and saying, “Have mercy, Son of David!” saying, “Son of Dawid,̱ have compassion on us!”<verse eID="Matt.9.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.28" osisID="Matt.9.28" n="28"/>And when Yeshua came from there to a house, those blind men approached him. He said to them, “Do you have faith that I am able to do this?” They said, “Yes, my Lord!” “Do you believe that I am able to do this?” They said to Him, “Yes, Master.”<verse eID="Matt.9.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.29" osisID="Matt.9.29" n="29"/>In that hour, he touched their eye and said, “According to your faith, so it will be to you.” The scriptures: Then He touched their eyes, saying, “According to your belief let it be to you.”<verse eID="Matt.9.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.30" osisID="Matt.9.30" n="30"/>And in that hour their eyes were opened, and so Yeshua warned them, “Do not report it to any man.” know.”<verse eID="Matt.9.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.31" osisID="Matt.9.31" n="31"/>After this, they went and announced his goodness all throughout the land. The scriptures: But when they went out, they made Him known in all that land.<verse eID="Matt.9.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.32" osisID="Matt.9.32" n="32"/>When Yeshua went out from there, they brought him to one mute who had a demon. The scriptures: And as they were going out, see, they brought to Him a man, dumb and demon- possessed.<verse eID="Matt.9.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.33" osisID="Matt.9.33" n="33"/>And when he expelled the demon from him, that mute spoke. And then all the people believed and said, “Never has there been found such this in Israel!” The scriptures: And when the demon was cast out, the dumb one spoke. And the crowds marvelled, saying, “It was never seen like this in Yisra’ĕl!”<verse eID="Matt.9.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.34" osisID="Matt.9.34" n="34"/>And the Pharisees said, “Whoever is first taken hold by a demon, expels the demon.” The scriptures: But the Pharisees said, “He casts out demons by the ruler of the demons.”<verse eID="Matt.9.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.35" osisID="Matt.9.35" n="35"/>And Yeshua was returning through all the provinces and villages and teaching in all assemblies,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="187" osisRef="Matt.9.35" osisID="Matt.9.35!note.187">The term "Knesset" ( כְּנֶסֶת ), meaning "assembly" or "gathering" in Hebrew, originated during the Second Temple period to denote the highest leadership body of that time, known as the Knesset HaGdola (the Great Assembly). This word first appears in the Mishnah, Pirkei Avot 1:1. Later, the name "Knesset" was officially adopted on February 16, 1949, during the inaugural session of the Constituent Assembly, which served as the foundational legislative body for the newly established State of Israel. See other uses in Matthew 11:7;</note> and declaring by the designs of heaven, and healing all sicknesses and disease. proclaiming the Good News of the reign, and healing every disease and every bodily weakness among the people.<verse eID="Matt.9.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.36" osisID="Matt.9.36" n="36"/>When he thus saw all the people of the land, he had mercy on them, lest they become worn out sitting like sheep without a shepherd. The scriptures: And having seen the crowds, He was moved with compassion for them, because they were weary and scattered, as sheep having no shepherd.<verse eID="Matt.9.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.37" osisID="Matt.9.37" n="37"/>And he said to his talmidim (students), “The harvest is great, but the workers are lesser.” The scriptures: Then He said to His taught ones, “The harvest truly is great, but the workers are few.<verse eID="Matt.9.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.38" osisID="Matt.9.38" n="38"/>“You are to pray of the owner of the harvest that he would bring out workers for his harvest.” The scriptures: Pray then that the Master of the harvest would send out workers to His harvest.”<verse eID="Matt.9.38"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.9"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.10" osisID="Matt.10" n="10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.1" osisID="Matt.10.1" n="1"/>And he called for his twelve talmidim (students) and vested them with power over all evil spirits, that they would be able to expel them, and taught them to heal all the sick and diseased.<verse eID="Matt.10.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.2" osisID="Matt.10.2" n="2"/>These are the names of his twelve talmidim (students) that came: the first one, Shimon (Simon) called Kepha (Peter), and Andre his brother, and Ya’akov (Jacob) son of (zebdi) Zebedee, and Yochanon (John) his brother,<verse eID="Matt.10.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.3" osisID="Matt.10.3" n="3"/>and Philippos (Philip), and Bartolmiyyahu (Bartholomew), and Toma (Thomas), Mateus (Matthew) the one who preaches and Ya’akov, the son of Halphai (Alphæus), and Lebohiyyahu (Lebbæus) who is called Taddodiyyahu (Thaddæus),<verse eID="Matt.10.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.4" osisID="Matt.10.4" n="4"/>and Shim’on (Simon) the Canaanite, Yehudah (Judah), Iscariota (Iscariot), <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="188" osisRef="Matt.10.4" osisID="Matt.10.4!note.188">From Etymonline.com, the name “Iscariot” is the surname of Judas, betrayer of Yeshua, in the New Testament, from Latin Iscariota, from</note>who condemned him.<verse eID="Matt.10.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.5" osisID="Matt.10.5" n="5"/>And to these twelve, Yeshua said and commanded them, “Do not walk by the way of pagans, and do not go by the region of the Samaritans,”<verse eID="Matt.10.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.6" osisID="Matt.10.6" n="6"/>“go you first to the lost sheep from the house of Yisrael (Israel).”<verse eID="Matt.10.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.7" osisID="Matt.10.7" n="7"/>“And in the hour that you go, you announce and say, ‘The time of the kingdom of heaven has arrived!’”<verse eID="Matt.10.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.8" osisID="Matt.10.8" n="8"/>“Heal <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="189" osisRef="Matt.10.8" osisID="Matt.10.8!note.189">The root of the word רפה (rafa) has several etymological roots that mean to heal, to mend, stitch together, let loose, or to slacken.</note>the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, expel the demons, and whoever redeems kindness to you, reward him with kindness.”<verse eID="Matt.10.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.9" osisID="Matt.10.9" n="9"/>“Do not buy gold, silver, nor copper to put in your pocket,”<verse eID="Matt.10.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.10" osisID="Matt.10.10" n="10"/>“nor shall you make a pocket for bread for the road, nor take two shirts, nor sandals, nor shirts, nor sandals, nor a staff, that they will not say of you, 'It is your false intentions.’”<verse eID="Matt.10.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.11" osisID="Matt.10.11" n="11"/>“In whichever district or village that you enter, ask who is faithful there, and dwell there until you go out from there.”<verse eID="Matt.10.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.12" osisID="Matt.10.12" n="12"/>“And when you who enter, ask for the peace of the men of the house.”<verse eID="Matt.10.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.13" osisID="Matt.10.13" n="13"/>“And if the head of the house is one who initially seeks your peace; your peace will be lost to you.”<verse eID="Matt.10.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.14" osisID="Matt.10.14" n="14"/>“And if they do not receive or listen to your speaking, when you go out of the house or the city, you are to shake the dust from your foot.”<verse eID="Matt.10.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.15" osisID="Matt.10.15" n="15"/>“In truth, I tell you, like Sodom and Amorah (Gomorrah) it will be appointed for them on the day of judgment for those sons of that province.”<verse eID="Matt.10.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.16" osisID="Matt.10.16" n="16"/>“Behold, I send you as a lamb among wolves; therefore, you will be wise as creatures and innocent as sons of a dove.”<verse eID="Matt.10.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.17" osisID="Matt.10.17" n="17"/>“Beware of the sons of man, who are seen as loving, that they remove you from Elohim (God) to their house of judgment (Beit Din).”<verse eID="Matt.10.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.18" osisID="Matt.10.18" n="18"/>“And deliver before the prince and king, driving you for my sake, to establish their<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="190" osisRef="Matt.10.18" osisID="Matt.10.18!note.190">This could be a spelling error with the incorrect pronominal suffix.</note> testimony and the testimony of the people.”<verse eID="Matt.10.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.19" osisID="Matt.10.19" n="19"/>“In the hour that they grievously chastise you, you are not to think about what you will respond to or what will contend against them, for in that hour, you will find what you are to speak,”<verse eID="Matt.10.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.20" osisID="Matt.10.20" n="20"/>“that it is not you who is speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking in you.”<verse eID="Matt.10.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.21" osisID="Matt.10.21" n="21"/>“A brother will send his brother over to the judge for death, and a father his son, and the children will rise against their parents and cause them to die.”<verse eID="Matt.10.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.22" osisID="Matt.10.22" n="22"/>“And you will be despised by all the sons of man because of My name; yet, he of mankind, who waits until the end of the matter, he will live.”<verse eID="Matt.10.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.23" osisID="Matt.10.23" n="23"/>“But those that pursue you in one province, you escape in another province; I will say saying to you that ‘You will not complete all the provinces until the Son of man comes!’”<verse eID="Matt.10.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.24" osisID="Matt.10.24" n="24"/>“There is no talmid (student) more informed than his Rabbi, and no servant than his lord.”<verse eID="Matt.10.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.25" osisID="Matt.10.25" n="25"/>“It’s enough for the talmidim (students) to be like his Rabbi, and a servant like his lord; so, if they called the head of the house Ba’al Zebub (Beelzebub), how much more to the children of the house?”<verse eID="Matt.10.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.26" osisID="Matt.10.26" n="26"/>“Therefore, don’t fear them for anything concealed; there is not a thing concealed that will not be revealed, nor hidden that will not be made known.”<verse eID="Matt.10.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.27" osisID="Matt.10.27" n="27"/>“And what I say to you in darkness, you are to say openly. And what your ears hear, you are to proclaim on the rooftops!”<verse eID="Matt.10.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.28" osisID="Matt.10.28" n="28"/>“And you will not fear those who kill the body but are not able to kill the soul. But be afraid of who is able to kill the body and soul in Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.10.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.29" osisID="Matt.10.29" n="29"/>“Are not two birds sold for one small coin? And not one of them falls to the earth except by the decree of the heavens,”<verse eID="Matt.10.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.30" osisID="Matt.10.30" n="30"/>“for even the hair on your head is numbered one by one.”<verse eID="Matt.10.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.31" osisID="Matt.10.31" n="31"/>“You shall not be very fearful, because you are considered greater than the birds.”<verse eID="Matt.10.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.32" osisID="Matt.10.32" n="32"/>“Every man who acknowledges me before the sons of man, I will acknowledge him before my Father, who is in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.10.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.33" osisID="Matt.10.33" n="33"/>“All who deny me before the sons of man, even I, I will deny him before my Father, who is in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.10.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.34" osisID="Matt.10.34" n="34"/>“Do not think that I came to allow peace on earth; I have not come except for a sword,”<verse eID="Matt.10.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.35" osisID="Matt.10.35" n="35"/>“I came but to leave division between a father and his son, between a daughter and her mother, and between a daughter-in-law and her mother-in-law,”<verse eID="Matt.10.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.36" osisID="Matt.10.36" n="36"/>“and the enemies of a man are men of his house.”<verse eID="Matt.10.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.37" osisID="Matt.10.37" n="37"/>“All who want his father or his mother more than me, he is not of me. And all who love his son or daughter more than me, he is not of me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.38" osisID="Matt.10.38" n="38"/>“And all who do not take up his cross and follow me, he is not of me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.39" osisID="Matt.10.39" n="39"/>“Whoever loves his soul will lose it. And whoever destroys his soul because of me, I will find him.”<verse eID="Matt.10.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.40" osisID="Matt.10.40" n="40"/>“Whoever receives you, receives me. And whoever receives me, receives Him who sent me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.41" osisID="Matt.10.41" n="41"/>“Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet, takes a reward of a prophet. And whoever receives the righteous in the name of a righteous, takes a reward of the righteous.”<verse eID="Matt.10.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.42" osisID="Matt.10.42" n="42"/>“And all who give drink to one of the little ones, a glass of cold water only, in the name of the talmidim (students). Truth, I say to you that he will not lose his reward.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="193" osisRef="Matt.10.42" osisID="Matt.10.42!note.193">See Hebrews 6:10.</note><verse eID="Matt.10.42"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.10"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.11" osisID="Matt.11" n="11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.1" osisID="Matt.11.1" n="1"/>And it came to be after Yeshua completed commanding his twelve talmidim (students), He changed his location from there to teach and proclaim in their provinces. from there to teach and to proclaim in their cities.<verse eID="Matt.11.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.2" osisID="Matt.11.2" n="2"/>When Yochanan (John) heard in the house of prisoners about the works of the Messiah, he sent by the hand of his talmidim (students) and said to him, The scriptures: And when Yoḥanan had heard in the prison of the works of Messiah, he sent two of his taught ones,<verse eID="Matt.11.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.3" osisID="Matt.11.3" n="3"/>“You are he, whom we are expecting, or do we wait for another and expect him?” The scriptures: and said to Him, “Are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?”<verse eID="Matt.11.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.4" osisID="Matt.11.4" n="4"/>Yeshua said, “Go to Yochanan (John) and say, “Such as that you heard, you will see.”<verse eID="Matt.11.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.5" osisID="Matt.11.5" n="5"/>“The blind see, and the lame walk, the leper is healed, the dead rise, and the poor are enriched.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="194" osisRef="Matt.11.5" osisID="Matt.11.5!note.194">See Isaiah 26:19, 29:18, 35:5-6, 61:1.</note> The scriptures: “Blind receive sight and lame walk, lepers are cleansed and deaf hear, dead are raised up and poor are brought the Good News.<verse eID="Matt.11.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.6" osisID="Matt.11.6" n="6"/>“Happy is the man who does not stumble in me.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="195" osisRef="Matt.11.6" osisID="Matt.11.6!note.195">See Proverbs 3:13.</note> The scriptures: “And blessed is he who does not stumble in Me.”<verse eID="Matt.11.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.7" osisID="Matt.11.7" n="7"/>When they went, Yeshua began to say to the Knesset (Assembly) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="196" osisRef="Matt.11.7" osisID="Matt.11.7!note.196">See footnote Matthew 9:35.</note>for Yochanan (John), “Why did you go out to the dry land<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="197" osisRef="Matt.11.7" osisID="Matt.11.7!note.197">Meaning, “desert.”</note> to see broken reeds that are shocked by the wind?” did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?<verse eID="Matt.11.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.8" osisID="Matt.11.8" n="8"/>“And for what did you go out to see? As one clothed in mediocre clothes like this, or one of pristine? You will see it even in a house of royalty.” The scriptures: “But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft garments? Look, those wearing soft garments are in the houses of sovereigns.<verse eID="Matt.11.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.9" osisID="Matt.11.9" n="9"/>“Again, you went out to see a prophet? Truth I, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="198" osisRef="Matt.11.9" osisID="Matt.11.9!note.198">See Cochin Hebrew Matthew 10:7 for same occurrence. This pronoun is not needed as the pronoun is included in the verb. This author</note>say to you, that more than a prophet, is he.” The scriptures: “But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I say to you, and more than a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.11.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.10" osisID="Matt.11.10" n="10"/>“Indeed, this is he, whom it is written of him, ‘Behold, I send a messenger before you to make straight the way before you.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="199" osisRef="Matt.11.10" osisID="Matt.11.10!note.199">See Malachi 3:1.</note> The scriptures: “For this is he of whom it was written, ‘See, I send My messenger before Your face, who shall prepare Your way before You.’<verse eID="Matt.11.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.11" osisID="Matt.11.11" n="11"/>“Truth, I, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="201" osisRef="Matt.11.11" osisID="Matt.11.11!note.201">See Cochin Hebrew Matthew 10:7 for same occurrence. This pronoun is not needed as the pronoun is included in the verb. This author often adds pronouns where they are not required. This could be for emphasis, patterns of speech, or error.</note>say to you, ‘There has not risen among the sons of man a rabbi, like Yochanan (John) the Purifier. And he, the smallest in heaven, is greater than him!’” The scriptures: “Truly, I say to you, among those born of women there has not risen one greater than Yoḥanan the Immerser, yet the least one in the reign of the heavens is greater than he.<verse eID="Matt.11.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.12" osisID="Matt.11.12" n="12"/>“From the birth of Yochanan (John) the Purifier and until now, the kingdom of heaven is strengthened, and all the accusers will cease and be silenced;” The scriptures: “And from the days of Yoḥanan the Immerser till now the reign of the heavens is violated, and the violent seize it.<verse eID="Matt.11.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.13" osisID="Matt.11.13" n="13"/>“according to every prophet and the Torah until the appointed time of Yochanan (John) that they prophesied. The scriptures: “For all the prophets and the Torah prophesied till Yoḥanan.<verse eID="Matt.11.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.14" osisID="Matt.11.14" n="14"/>“And if you want to receive it, he is Elyyahu (Elijah), who is intending to come.” The scriptures: “And if you wish to accept it, he is Ěliyahu who was about to come.<verse eID="Matt.11.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.15" osisID="Matt.11.15" n="15"/>“Whoever of him has ears to hear, let him hear.” The scriptures: “He who has ears to hear, let him hear!<verse eID="Matt.11.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.16" osisID="Matt.11.16" n="16"/>“To whom will I compare these kindred? This resembles little ones sitting in the market and calling out to their friends,” The scriptures: “And to what shall I compare this generation? It is like children sitting in the market- places and calling to their companions,<verse eID="Matt.11.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.17" osisID="Matt.11.17" n="17"/>“and they say, 'We sing for you and you are not dancing. And we lament for you, but you do not weep.’” The scriptures: and saying, ‘We played the flute for you, and you did not dance; we lamented to you, and you did not beat the breast.’<verse eID="Matt.11.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.18" osisID="Matt.11.18" n="18"/>“Because of this, Yochanan (John) saw and came not for eating and not drinking, and they said, ‘A demon possessed him.’” The scriptures: “For Yoḥanan came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, ‘He has a demon.’<verse eID="Matt.11.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.19" osisID="Matt.11.19" n="19"/>“The Son of man came, ate, and drank, and he says, ‘You will see this man is gluttonous, and a drunkard, and he loves lawlessness and sinners.’ And the wisdom<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="203" osisRef="Matt.11.19" osisID="Matt.11.19!note.203">See Job 28:28; Proverbs 3:15; 4:6; Sirach 1:1-10.</note> of his righteousness is by works.” The scriptures: “The Son of Ada̱m came eating and drinking, and they say, ‘See, a man, a glutton and a winedrinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ And wisdom was declared right by her works.”<verse eID="Matt.11.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.20" osisID="Matt.11.20" n="20"/>Then Yeshua began to rebuke those provinces which had done many mighty things, and they did not do repentance. The scriptures: Then He began to reproach the cities in which most of His miracles had been done, because they did not repent:<verse eID="Matt.11.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.21" osisID="Matt.11.21" n="21"/>And he h̶e said, “Woe to you, Korazin (Chorazin)! Woe to you, Bet Tzaidah (Bethsaida)! And if Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon) began doing marvelous things, they would have cried, and outside of this, they would have done repentance in sack and ashes!” The scriptures: “Woe to you, Korazin! Woe to you, Bĕyth Tsaida̱! Because if the miracles which were done in you had started this in Tsor and Tsido̱n, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.<verse eID="Matt.11.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.22" osisID="Matt.11.22" n="22"/>“In truth, I I̶ say to you, for Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon) there will be a restful spirit on the day of judgement, but not for you!” The scriptures: “But I say to you, it shall be more bearable for Tsor and Tsido̱n in the day of judgment than for you.<verse eID="Matt.11.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.23" osisID="Matt.11.23" n="23"/>“And you Kephar Nahum (Capernaum), ‘from the heavens would you be cast? For down to Sheol, you will be lowered. For which in Sodom they had seen as these marvelous things, as were among them, as before.” The scriptures: “And you, Kephar Naḥum, who were exalted to the heaven, shall be brought down to She’ol! Because if the miracles which were done in you had been done in Sedo̱m, it would have remained until this day.<verse eID="Matt.11.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.24" osisID="Matt.11.24" n="24"/>“In truth, I say to you, for Sodom there will be a restful spirit on the day of judgment, but not for you.” The scriptures: “But I say to you that it shall be more bearable for the land of Sedo̱m in the day of judgment than for you.”<verse eID="Matt.11.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.25" osisID="Matt.11.25" n="25"/>Then Yeshua said in that hour, saying, “I give thanks before You, my Father, who is in the heavens, and earth. For You have concealed these words from the wise and intelligent, and likened them to children.” earth, because You have hidden these matters from clever and learned ones and have revealed them to babes.<verse eID="Matt.11.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.26" osisID="Matt.11.26" n="26"/>“If so, my Father, Your desire be before You.” The scriptures: “Yes, Father, because so it was well-pleasing in Your sight.<verse eID="Matt.11.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.27" osisID="Matt.11.27" n="27"/>“All what is delivered to me of my Father, and no man knows His Son, only his Father; also no man knows the Father only His Son, and he to whom the Son wants to rebel.” The scriptures: “All have been handed over to Me by My Father, and no one knows the Son except the Father. Nor does anyone know the Father except the Son, and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal Him.<verse eID="Matt.11.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.28" osisID="Matt.11.28" n="28"/>“Come near to me, all of you in yokes of hardship, and with yokes of burdens, for I will give you rest.” The scriptures: “Come to Me, all you who labour and are burdened, and I shall give you rest.<verse eID="Matt.11.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.29" osisID="Matt.11.29" n="29"/>“You are to carry my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am stricken of spirit and young of the heart, and you will find rest for your spirit and souls.” The scriptures: “Take My yoke upon you and learn from Me, for I am meek and humble in heart, and you shall find rest for your beings<verse eID="Matt.11.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.30" osisID="Matt.11.30" n="30"/>“For my yoke is from the inside, and my burden is light.” The scriptures: “For My yoke is gentle and My burden is light.’”<verse eID="Matt.11.30"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.11"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.12" osisID="Matt.12" n="12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.1" osisID="Matt.12.1" n="1"/>At that time, Yeshua walked on the Sabbath in the seed house,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="207" osisRef="Matt.12.1" osisID="Matt.12.1!note.207">Unlike the Greek version, which has them gathering in the fields as permitted by Deuteronomy 23:25, the Cochin version describes them entering the grain storage area in the fields. This aligns with the comparison to David entering the Temple when hungry, as noted in Matthew 12:4.</note> and his talmidim (students) were hungry and began collecting ears of grain and eating.<verse eID="Matt.12.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.2" osisID="Matt.12.2" n="2"/>The Pharisees, when they saw this, said to him, “Behold! Your talmidim (students) are doing something that is forbidden to do on the Sabbath.”<verse eID="Matt.12.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.3" osisID="Matt.12.3" n="3"/>Then he replied to them, “Have you not read what David did, when he was hungry, and the men that were with him?”<verse eID="Matt.12.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.4" osisID="Matt.12.4" n="4"/>“How he entered into the house of Elohim (God) and ate the bread of the presence (table of showbread) which was not appropriate for him to eat nor for the men who were with him, but rather for the priests alone.”<verse eID="Matt.12.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.5" osisID="Matt.12.5" n="5"/>“And again, have you not read in the Torah that the priests in the holy place profane on the Sabbath, and they were not afraid or sinning from this?”<verse eID="Matt.12.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.6" osisID="Matt.12.6" n="6"/>“I ‒ I say to you that one greater than the holy place is here.”<verse eID="Matt.12.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.7" osisID="Matt.12.7" n="7"/>“The following matter you are to know; whoever my father pardons, I do not want sacrifice or peace offerings. He (Yehovah) will not demonstrate love for those who do not fear Him.”<verse eID="Matt.12.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.8" osisID="Matt.12.8" n="8"/>“Lord of the Sabbath is he, the Son of man.”<verse eID="Matt.12.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.9" osisID="Matt.12.9" n="9"/>And Yeshua departed from there and came in the midst of their community.<verse eID="Matt.12.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.10" osisID="Matt.12.10" n="10"/>And there was a man whose hand withered, and they asked him if he is able to heal on the Sabbath so that he would be destroyed by it.<verse eID="Matt.12.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.11" osisID="Matt.12.11" n="11"/>This one responded and said, “Who among you is a man that has a sheep that has fallen into a pit on the Sabbath, would not raise it out?”<verse eID="Matt.12.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.12" osisID="Matt.12.12" n="12"/>“How much more is a son of man than a sheep! By this judgement, it is appropriate on the Sabbath to do good.”<verse eID="Matt.12.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.13" osisID="Matt.12.13" n="13"/>And hastily he said to the man, “Stretch out your hand.” And he stretched out, and it became well like its accompanying hand.<verse eID="Matt.12.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.14" osisID="Matt.12.14" n="14"/>And the Pharisees and the king went out, and they arose against him to destroy him.<verse eID="Matt.12.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.15" osisID="Matt.12.15" n="15"/>Then Yeshua, when he knew this, went after the crowds and healed all of them.<verse eID="Matt.12.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.16" osisID="Matt.12.16" n="16"/>And he would remind them not to reveal him,<verse eID="Matt.12.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.17" osisID="Matt.12.17" n="17"/>to carry out what was said through <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="208" osisRef="Matt.12.17" osisID="Matt.12.17!note.208">Literally, “by the hand of.”</note>the prophet Yeshayah (Isaiah),<verse eID="Matt.12.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.18" osisID="Matt.12.18" n="18"/>“Behold, my servant whom I support, my chosen one whom my soul delights. I have given my Spirit upon him, and judgment to the nations, he will bring out.”<verse eID="Matt.12.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.19" osisID="Matt.12.19" n="19"/>“He will not shout, or lift up, or cause his voice to be heard on the outside.”<verse eID="Matt.12.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.20" osisID="Matt.12.20" n="20"/>“A crushed reed, he will not break, and a darkened wick he will not extinguish, until deliver judgment unto truth.”<verse eID="Matt.12.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.21" osisID="Matt.12.21" n="21"/>“And in his name, they, the people, were hoping.”<verse eID="Matt.12.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.22" osisID="Matt.12.22" n="22"/>After this, they brought him one madman, who was also mute and blind, and he healed him, so the mute and blind spoke and saw.<verse eID="Matt.12.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.23" osisID="Matt.12.23" n="23"/>And all the community was amazed. And they said, “Perhaps this is the son of David?”<verse eID="Matt.12.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.24" osisID="Matt.12.24" n="24"/>When the Pharisees heard, they said, “Certainly he does not expel demons except in the place of Ba’al Zebub (Be’elzubul), the chief of the demons.”<verse eID="Matt.12.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.25" osisID="Matt.12.25" n="25"/>When he understood their thoughts, he said to them, “Every kingdom that is divided against itself is destroyed, and every house and province that you divide against itself cannot exist.”<verse eID="Matt.12.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.26" osisID="Matt.12.26" n="26"/>“And if Satan brings an accusation against himself and makes division; for however can his kingdom stand?”<verse eID="Matt.12.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.27" osisID="Matt.12.27" n="27"/>“And if I expel by Ba’al Zebub (Beelzebub), the demons, why would your children expel him in haste? So therefore, they will be defendants against you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.28" osisID="Matt.12.28" n="28"/>“And if I expel the demons by the power of the Ruach (Spirit) of Elohim (God) of demons, then the kingdom of heaven has come near upon you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.29" osisID="Matt.12.29" n="29"/>“How can a common man enter into the house of one man, a mighty one, and steal his mammon (money), but first needing to bind the mighty one, and then his house will be stolen?”<verse eID="Matt.12.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.30" osisID="Matt.12.30" n="30"/>“Whoever is not with me, I become jealous, and whoever does not assemble with me is surely scattered.”<verse eID="Matt.12.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.31" osisID="Matt.12.31" n="31"/>“Therefore, I say to you that all sins and contemptible things can be forgiven for the sons of man. However, contemptible things that are against the Spirit will not be forgiven.”<verse eID="Matt.12.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.32" osisID="Matt.12.32" n="32"/>“And everyone who says a word about the Son of man, it will be forgiven of him. But whoever says about the Holy Spirit, it is not forgiven of him; not in this world or in the coming world.”<verse eID="Matt.12.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.33" osisID="Matt.12.33" n="33"/>“Either you make a tree good and its fruit good, or you make the tree bad and its fruit bad; from its fruit you shall know the tree.”<verse eID="Matt.12.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.34" osisID="Matt.12.34" n="34"/>“Children of serpents! How are you able to speak good, but you are evil? From the intentions of your heart, your mouth speaks.”<verse eID="Matt.12.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.35" osisID="Matt.12.35" n="35"/>“A good man, from his good thinking, brings out good. And an evil man, from his evil thinking, brings evil.”<verse eID="Matt.12.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.36" osisID="Matt.12.36" n="36"/>“I say to you that all idle words that they, the sons of mankind, are hanged by their words on the day of judgment.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="218" osisRef="Matt.12.36" osisID="Matt.12.36!note.218">See Ecclesiastes 12:13-14.</note><verse eID="Matt.12.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.37" osisID="Matt.12.37" n="37"/>“From your words, you will be justified, and by your words, you will be condemned.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="222" osisRef="Matt.12.37" osisID="Matt.12.37!note.222">Literally, “indebted to.”</note>”<verse eID="Matt.12.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.38" osisID="Matt.12.38" n="38"/>Then the people from the scribes and Pharisees answered, “Our Rabbi, we ‒ we need to see some sign from you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.39" osisID="Matt.12.39" n="39"/>Then he answered and said, “A wicked and adulterous kindred seek signs, but the sign will not be given, except a sign of Yonah (Jonah), the prophet.”<verse eID="Matt.12.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.40" osisID="Matt.12.40" n="40"/>“As Yonah (Jonah) who was in the inward parts of the fish, three days and three nights, in this way, the Son of man will be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.”<verse eID="Matt.12.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.41" osisID="Matt.12.41" n="41"/>“My people will rise up with this kindred and charge them, for they repented with the preaching<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="225" osisRef="Matt.12.41" osisID="Matt.12.41!note.225">Literally, “proclamation” or “declaration.”</note> of Yonah (Jonah); and here is one greater than Yonah (Yonah).”<verse eID="Matt.12.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.42" osisID="Matt.12.42" n="42"/>“The kingdom of the South will rise in the judgement with this kinsmen and charge it, for she came from a distant land to hear the wisdom of Shelomoh (Solomon), and here is one greater than Shelomoh (Solomon).”<verse eID="Matt.12.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.43" osisID="Matt.12.43" n="43"/>“Whenever the impure spirit goes out from the sons of man, it goes to a restful place to rest and does not find it.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="226" osisRef="Matt.12.43" osisID="Matt.12.43!note.226">In this verse, the demons are referred to as “he,” and in the following two verses, they are referenced as “she.”</note><verse eID="Matt.12.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.44" osisID="Matt.12.44" n="44"/>Then she says, “I will overthrow my house from which I went out.” And she comes and finds the house empty and turns upside down.”<verse eID="Matt.12.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45" n="45"/>“Then they go and take with it seven other spirits more evil <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="227" osisRef="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45!note.227">Can also translate to “eviler.”</note>than itself, and they come and dwell in the same house, and it became worse for that person than at the beginning; and thus will it be for these evil kindred.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="228" osisRef="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45!note.228">This can also be translated as “and so will it be for this evil generation,” as this is speaking of a different group of family members at a</note><verse eID="Matt.12.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.46" osisID="Matt.12.46" n="46"/>When he was speaking this, his mother and brothers came and stood outside and wanted to speak with him.<verse eID="Matt.12.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.47" osisID="Matt.12.47" n="47"/>One man said to him, “Behold, your mother and brothers stand outside and want to speak with you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.48" osisID="Matt.12.48" n="48"/>He, this one, responded to whoever told him, “Who is it, my mother, and who are they, my brothers?”<verse eID="Matt.12.48"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.49" osisID="Matt.12.49" n="49"/>And he sent his hand toward his talmidim (students) and said, “These ‒ they are my mother and they are my brothers!”<verse eID="Matt.12.49"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.50" osisID="Matt.12.50" n="50"/>“Whoever does the desire of my Father, he is my brother, and my sisters, and my mother.”<verse eID="Matt.12.50"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.12"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.13" osisID="Matt.13" n="13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.1" osisID="Matt.13.1" n="1"/>On the same day that Yeshua went out from his house, he sat upon the riverbank of the sea.<verse eID="Matt.13.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.2" osisID="Matt.13.2" n="2"/>Many people assembled and drew near him. When he went out, he sat in the ship, and all the people stood on the riverbank of the sea.<verse eID="Matt.13.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.3" osisID="Matt.13.3" n="3"/>And he spoke many words with them in segments and in Proverbs. Then he said this, “The sower went to sow,”<verse eID="Matt.13.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.4" osisID="Matt.13.4" n="4"/>“and when he sowed this, a few fell on the road, birds came and ate them.”<verse eID="Matt.13.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.5" osisID="Matt.13.5" n="5"/>“And one portion fell on the hills in a place that without much dirt on it. And in that hour, it grew because there was no dirt for its root.”<verse eID="Matt.13.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.6" osisID="Matt.13.6" n="6"/>“And when the sun shone on it and it became heated, because it had no root for it, it withered.”<verse eID="Matt.13.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.7" osisID="Matt.13.7" n="7"/>“And one portion fell in the place of the thorns, and when it grew, the thorns choked it.”<verse eID="Matt.13.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.8" osisID="Matt.13.8" n="8"/>“And one portion fell on good land and produced fruit; some had given a hundred, some sixty, and some thirty.”<verse eID="Matt.13.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.9" osisID="Matt.13.9" n="9"/>“Whoever has ears to hear, he will hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.10" osisID="Matt.13.10" n="10"/>And his talmidim (students) approached him, “Why do you speak parables with them?”<verse eID="Matt.13.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.11" osisID="Matt.13.11" n="11"/>“Again, he answered and said, “To you is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of heaven, but for those it is not given.”<verse eID="Matt.13.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.12" osisID="Matt.13.12" n="12"/>“To whoever has, they give to him, and it increases more so. But whoever does not have, even what he has, they take from him.”<verse eID="Matt.13.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.13" osisID="Matt.13.13" n="13"/>“Because of this, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="231" osisRef="Matt.13.13" osisID="Matt.13.13!note.231">The double pronoun could be intentional to express inflection, or it could be that the author is using the active participle as a verb without</note>speak with them in a parable, for they see, and do not see. And hearing they do not hear; and as well, they do not believe.”<verse eID="Matt.13.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.14" osisID="Matt.13.14" n="14"/>“And by them, the prophecy of Yeshayah (Isaiah) is validated, that said, ‘Hearing, you hear and you don’t understand, and seeing you see and you don’t perceive,<verse eID="Matt.13.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15" n="15"/>“The heart of this people is fat, their ears are heavy, and their eyes shut, lest they will see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and they return<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="233" osisRef="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15!note.233">Return, meaning to repent.</note> and are healed.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="234" osisRef="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15!note.234">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note><verse eID="Matt.13.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.16" osisID="Matt.13.16" n="16"/>“On you is the goodness for your eyes see and your ears hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.17" osisID="Matt.13.17" n="17"/>“In truth, for I say to you that many prophets and righteous ones longed to see what you see, but did not see, and to hear what you hear, but did not hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.18" osisID="Matt.13.18" n="18"/>“You - you will understand the proverb of the sower.”<verse eID="Matt.13.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.19" osisID="Matt.13.19" n="19"/>“All who hear the words of the kingdom and do not discern it, and had sown it without desire. This is he, who sows upon the road.”<verse eID="Matt.13.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.20" osisID="Matt.13.20" n="20"/>“He who sows upon a mountain, is he who hears the word, and receives it with joy, in that same hour.”<verse eID="Matt.13.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.21" osisID="Matt.13.21" n="21"/>“He has no essence of himself, except for a time; and when there is sickness or some weakness, quickly he stumbles.”<verse eID="Matt.13.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.22" osisID="Matt.13.22" n="22"/>“That which is sown in the place of the thorns is he; he whom hears the word, but thought about it, in the pondering of this world, and wealth misled him and strangled the word, and it did not produce fruit.”<verse eID="Matt.13.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.23" osisID="Matt.13.23" n="23"/>And whoever sows on good earth, it is he who hears and understands, and bears fruit to a hundred or sixty, or thirty.”<verse eID="Matt.13.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.24" osisID="Matt.13.24" n="24"/>Another proverb, he compared to them, “The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his city.”<verse eID="Matt.13.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.25" osisID="Matt.13.25" n="25"/>“And when they were sleeping, enemies came and sowed a weed between the wheat, and left.”<verse eID="Matt.13.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.26" osisID="Matt.13.26" n="26"/>“And when the herbs grew beautifully and they produced fruit, in that hour, appeared the weed.”<verse eID="Matt.13.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.27" osisID="Matt.13.27" n="27"/>“And the servants approached the Lord and said to him, ‘Our Lord, you sow good seed in your field. Where has the weed come from?’”<verse eID="Matt.13.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.28" osisID="Matt.13.28" n="28"/>“The Lord said to him, ‘My enemies did this.’ And his servants said, ‘Do you want to quickly remove them?’”<verse eID="Matt.13.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.29" osisID="Matt.13.29" n="29"/>“He said to them, ‘Perhaps when you remove the weed, you will uproot the wheat likewise.’”<verse eID="Matt.13.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.30" osisID="Matt.13.30" n="30"/>“But let them remain growing together, until the season of the harvest. In the hour of the harvest, I say to the reaper, ‘Cleanse first the weed, and bind them to burn. But the wheat, bring into the treasure.’<verse eID="Matt.13.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.31" osisID="Matt.13.31" n="31"/>“Another proverb I will liken for you; the kingdom of heaven resembles seeds of mustard grains that a man took and sowed in his field,”<verse eID="Matt.13.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.32" osisID="Matt.13.32" n="32"/>“and it is the smallest seed of all the seeds, but it grows from all the green herbs and becomes like a tree until the birds of the sky come to gather in its shade.”<verse eID="Matt.13.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.33" osisID="Matt.13.33" n="33"/>“Another parable I say to you, ‘The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and planted in three measures of flour, until it leavened all of it.’”<verse eID="Matt.13.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.34" osisID="Matt.13.34" n="34"/>“All of this was in parables for the communities, and whatever was without a parable and allegory, he did not speak with them.”<verse eID="Matt.13.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.35" osisID="Matt.13.35" n="35"/>“As it was said by the hand of the prophet, ‘I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter dark sayings of old.’”<verse eID="Matt.13.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.36" osisID="Matt.13.36" n="36"/>“Then Yeshua left the community and came to a house, and his talmidim (students) approached and said, “Solve the matter for us regarding the weed and the earth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.37" osisID="Matt.13.37" n="37"/>Yeshua answered and said, “The one who sowed good seed, they are sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.13.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.38" osisID="Matt.13.38" n="38"/>“And the earth is the world, the good seed are children, the provision is that which has children of evil in it.”<verse eID="Matt.13.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.39" osisID="Matt.13.39" n="39"/>“And the enemies who sowed them are demons, his harvest is repentance and good works of the world, and the harvesters are the messengers.”<verse eID="Matt.13.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.40" osisID="Matt.13.40" n="40"/>“As they grasp the nourishment and burn with fire, so will be the fulfillment of this world.”<verse eID="Matt.13.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.41" osisID="Matt.13.41" n="41"/>“He sends out the sons of man with his messengers to select from every kingdom, the stumbling blocks and all those who do unrighteousness.”<verse eID="Matt.13.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.42" osisID="Matt.13.42" n="42"/>“And they will throw them into the furnace of fire, and there they will be weeping and gnashing their teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.43" osisID="Matt.13.43" n="43"/>“Then the righteous will shine like the brightness of the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, he will hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.44" osisID="Matt.13.44" n="44"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is as a hidden treasure in the earth, and when a man found it, he was joyful, and went out and hid it in his place, and from joy, he went and sold everything of what he had, and bought this land.”<verse eID="Matt.13.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.45" osisID="Matt.13.45" n="45"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant who seeks a good pearl.”<verse eID="Matt.13.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.46" osisID="Matt.13.46" n="46"/>“And when he found one pearl, he went and sold all he had, and bought it.”<verse eID="Matt.13.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.47" osisID="Matt.13.47" n="47"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net that falls into the water and all of what fell into it, gathering fish.”<verse eID="Matt.13.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.48" osisID="Matt.13.48" n="48"/>“So, therefore, he raised them to dry land, full of fish. And he sat over it to sort out, and the good he threw into containers, and the different bad ones he left out(side).”<verse eID="Matt.13.48"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.49" osisID="Matt.13.49" n="49"/>“So will it be at the end of the world; the messengers will go out and divide the evil from the righteous.”<verse eID="Matt.13.49"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.50" osisID="Matt.13.50" n="50"/>“And he will throw them into the furnace into the fire; and they will be weeping and gnashing their teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.50"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.51" osisID="Matt.13.51" n="51"/>Yeshua said to them, “Do you understand all this?” And they said to him, “Yes, our teacher.”<verse eID="Matt.13.51"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.52" osisID="Matt.13.52" n="52"/>He said to them, “Because of this, all scribes that are taught in the kingdom of heaven resemble a man, the owner of a house, who brings out from his treasure chest, new and old.”<verse eID="Matt.13.52"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.53" osisID="Matt.13.53" n="53"/>Then, when Yeshua completed these parables, he changed his location from there.<verse eID="Matt.13.53"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.54" osisID="Matt.13.54" n="54"/>And he came to a district and taught them in their community. How they were amazed, saying, “So where does he have this wisdom and wonders?”<verse eID="Matt.13.54"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.55" osisID="Matt.13.55" n="55"/>“Is not this the son of a carpenter of wood? Is not his mother called Miryam, and his brothers Ya’akov (Jacob), Yoseph (Joseph), Shim’on (Simon), and Yehudah (Judah)?”<verse eID="Matt.13.55"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.56" osisID="Matt.13.56" n="56"/>“And also, his sisters, are they not with them? From where come to him all this?”<verse eID="Matt.13.56"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.57" osisID="Matt.13.57" n="57"/>And they were amazed by him. So, Yeshua said to them, “There is no prophet without honor, only in his city and in his house.”<verse eID="Matt.13.57"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.58" osisID="Matt.13.58" n="58"/>Then Yeshua went out and did not do great marvelous wonders there because they did not believe in him.<verse eID="Matt.13.58"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.13"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.14" osisID="Matt.14" n="14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.1" osisID="Matt.14.1" n="1"/>In that very hour, Horodes (Herodes) the tetrarch <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="237" osisRef="Matt.14.1" osisID="Matt.14.1!note.237">The governor or ruler of one of four divisions of a country or province. Rome divided the land into four regions after Herod the Great</note>(including Horodos (Herodes) the fourth) heard of Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.2" osisID="Matt.14.2" n="2"/>And he said to his servants, “Yochanan (John) the Purifier has risen from the dead, because of this, he does these marvelous things.”<verse eID="Matt.14.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.3" osisID="Matt.14.3" n="3"/>This Horodes (Herodes) seized Yochanan, and bound him, and threw him into the house of prisoners, because of Herodiah (Herodias), the wife of Philippos (Philip), his brother.<verse eID="Matt.14.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.4" osisID="Matt.14.4" n="4"/>Then he - indeed, Yochanan (John) said to him that he is not allowed to take her to be his wife.<verse eID="Matt.14.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.5" osisID="Matt.14.5" n="5"/>And he wanted to kill him, but because he feared the crowds of people, he didn’t kill him from among them, for they were thinking him to be a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.14.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.6" osisID="Matt.14.6" n="6"/>When it was the feast of the birthday of Horodos (Herodes), the daughter of Herodiah (Herodias) danced before him and Horodos (Herodes) desired her.<verse eID="Matt.14.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.7" osisID="Matt.14.7" n="7"/>Because of this, with an oath he said to her, he would give her all <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="238" osisRef="Matt.14.7" osisID="Matt.14.7!note.238">Mark 6:23 says “up to half my kingdom.” And Esther 5:3 has the phrase “up to half the kingdom.” This is an ancient idiom for a generous</note>of what she asked.<verse eID="Matt.14.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.8" osisID="Matt.14.8" n="8"/>And she, because of numerous urges of her mother, said, “Give me now on one dish, the head belonging to Yochanan (John) the Purifier!”<verse eID="Matt.14.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.9" osisID="Matt.14.9" n="9"/>The king sighed because of her — because of the oath he made at the table, he commanded that it be given to her.<verse eID="Matt.14.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.10" osisID="Matt.14.10" n="10"/>Then he sent for and murdered Yochanan (John) in the house of prisoners.<verse eID="Matt.14.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.11" osisID="Matt.14.11" n="11"/>And he brought his head on a dish and gave it to the girl, and she brought it to her mother.<verse eID="Matt.14.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.12" osisID="Matt.14.12" n="12"/>And his talmidim (students) came and took his body and buried it, and they came and told Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.13" osisID="Matt.14.13" n="13"/>When Yeshua had heard it, he changed his location in a ship for a desert place to be alone. But then the congregation walked after him on dry ground from the land.<verse eID="Matt.14.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.14" osisID="Matt.14.14" n="14"/>And Yeshua went out with the community and had mercy on them. So, he healed their pains.<verse eID="Matt.14.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.15" osisID="Matt.14.15" n="15"/>When it was evening, his talmidim (students) drew near him and they said to him, “This is a dry place and the time of eating has passed. You could send the community so they can go to the city and buy themselves food.”<verse eID="Matt.14.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.16" osisID="Matt.14.16" n="16"/>He, he said to them, “They do not need to go. You are to give them food.”<verse eID="Matt.14.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.17" osisID="Matt.14.17" n="17"/>They said, “There is nothing here, but rather five loaves and two fish.”<verse eID="Matt.14.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.18" osisID="Matt.14.18" n="18"/>He said to them, “You bring them here to me.”<verse eID="Matt.14.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.19" osisID="Matt.14.19" n="19"/>And he commanded the community to sit on the earth. Then he took the five loaves and two fishes, and set it before them, and looked into the heavens, and blessed, and divided it to his talmidim (students). Then his talmidim (students) gave to the community,<verse eID="Matt.14.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.20" osisID="Matt.14.20" n="20"/>and all of them ate and were satisfied and they (the talmidim) took from the remaining pieces, twelve full baskets.<verse eID="Matt.14.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.21" osisID="Matt.14.21" n="21"/>This people who ate were five thousand, besides women and children.<verse eID="Matt.14.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.22" osisID="Matt.14.22" n="22"/>Then quickly he commanded his talmidim (students) to ascend the ship and go before him to cross over the river, and sent away the communities.<verse eID="Matt.14.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.23" osisID="Matt.14.23" n="23"/>But when he sent away the community, he ascended the mountain by himself to pray.<verse eID="Matt.14.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.24" osisID="Matt.14.24" n="24"/>So, he was alone, and the ship was far off from the land, and the sea was greatly raging from the waves, and the wind was against them.<verse eID="Matt.14.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.25" osisID="Matt.14.25" n="25"/>And at the fourth guard of the night, Yeshua came near them, strolling upon the sea.<verse eID="Matt.14.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.26" osisID="Matt.14.26" n="26"/>And his talmidim saw <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="239" osisRef="Matt.14.26" osisID="Matt.14.26!note.239">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note>one walking on the sea. So, they were amazed and said, “It is miraculous!” and from fear, they shouted.<verse eID="Matt.14.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.27" osisID="Matt.14.27" n="27"/>Then Yeshua said to them, “Harden your heart, it is I; you are to not fear!”<verse eID="Matt.14.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.28" osisID="Matt.14.28" n="28"/>Then Pedro (Peter) answered and said, “My Master, if it is you, command me to come to you<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="240" osisRef="Matt.14.28" osisID="Matt.14.28!note.240">Literally, “If it is you, command that I, he will come…” Thus,</note> to you on the water.”<verse eID="Matt.14.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.29" osisID="Matt.14.29" n="29"/>And Yeshua said to him, “Come!” And Pedro (Peter) descended from the ship and walked upon the sea and came beside Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.30" osisID="Matt.14.30" n="30"/>And when a hard wind came, he feared and began to sink. Then he raised his voice and shouted, “My Lord, save me!”<verse eID="Matt.14.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.31" osisID="Matt.14.31" n="31"/>And in that hour, he extended his hand and said, “Because of what little faith ‒ you were with doubts!”<verse eID="Matt.14.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.32" osisID="Matt.14.32" n="32"/>But when they ascended the ship, the wind ceased.<verse eID="Matt.14.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.33" osisID="Matt.14.33" n="33"/>So, those in the ship came and bowed down to him and said, “In truth, you are the Son of Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.14.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.34" osisID="Matt.14.34" n="34"/>Then they descended and came to the land of Gennosar (Gennesaret).<verse eID="Matt.14.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35" n="35"/>When the men <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="241" osisRef="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35!note.241">Can have a common plural meaning, “people.”</note>of that place knew him, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="242" osisRef="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35!note.242">Or “recognized him.”</note>they sent out to the city and its surroundings, and approached him with all those who were doing iniquity.<verse eID="Matt.14.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.36" osisID="Matt.14.36" n="36"/>So they requested him that they may offer up <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="243" osisRef="Matt.14.36" osisID="Matt.14.36!note.243">Literally, “sacrifice their hand.”</note>their hand for the corners of his wings of his clothing. Then all who touched were healed.<verse eID="Matt.14.36"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.14"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.15" osisID="Matt.15" n="15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.1" osisID="Matt.15.1" n="1"/>Then the Pharisees and scribes from Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) approached Yeshua and said,<verse eID="Matt.15.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.2" osisID="Matt.15.2" n="2"/>“Why do your talmidim (students) transgress against the religious law <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="244" osisRef="Matt.15.2" osisID="Matt.15.2!note.244">The Cochin Hebrew Matthew verse is clearer than the tradition of the elders in the Greek translation (i.e., The Scriptures 2009). This verse describes the Oral Torah of the Pharisees and Sages. Furthermore, the verse is about the practice of saying a blessing while pouring</note>of the sages, and not wash hands when they eat bread?”<verse eID="Matt.15.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.3" osisID="Matt.15.3" n="3"/>Yeshua answered and said before them, “For what reason do you transgress against the commandments of Yehovah for your pleasure!”<verse eID="Matt.15.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4" n="4"/>Yehovah said, “Honor your father and your mother.” <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="245" osisRef="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4!note.245">This is a direct quote of Exodus 20:12a, and Deuteronomy 5:16a, with the correction of אִָמֶּ֔ך (i.e., “your mother”) replacing the second repeated word ( אָבִ֨יִך )ָ֙ “your father.” See Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16.</note>And whoever curses them, shall surely die.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="246" osisRef="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4!note.246">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.15.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.5" osisID="Matt.15.5" n="5"/>You, what do you say? “All who say to their father and mother, ‘You drew near me to plot against me.’”<verse eID="Matt.15.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.6" osisID="Matt.15.6" n="6"/>And they do not honor their father and mother because of your enjoyment.<verse eID="Matt.15.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.7" osisID="Matt.15.7" n="7"/>Unbelievers! Plentily did Yeshayah (Isaiah) prophesy about you and said,<verse eID="Matt.15.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.8" osisID="Matt.15.8" n="8"/>“This people honor me with its mouth and lips, but its heart is distanced from me.”<verse eID="Matt.15.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.9" osisID="Matt.15.9" n="9"/>“And their fearing me when they study, even after this, is a commandment of men that is taught.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="250" osisRef="Matt.15.9" osisID="Matt.15.9!note.250">See Isaiah 29:13.</note><verse eID="Matt.15.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.10" osisID="Matt.15.10" n="10"/>Then he called the community and said to them, “You are to hear and understand.”<verse eID="Matt.15.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.11" osisID="Matt.15.11" n="11"/>It is not all that enters into the mouth that defiles the sons of man, but all that goes out from the mouth that defiles the sons of man.<verse eID="Matt.15.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.12" osisID="Matt.15.12" n="12"/>Then the talmidim (students) approached him and said to him, “You should know that the Pharisees who heard this word became discontented.”<verse eID="Matt.15.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.13" osisID="Matt.15.13" n="13"/>He answered and said to them, “All plantings in a place that is not planted of my Father in heaven, will be uprooted.<verse eID="Matt.15.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.14" osisID="Matt.15.14" n="14"/>You are to leave them: they are blind and leading the blind, and the blind leaders of the blind, by necessity in a place of walking, for two of them fall into the pit.”<verse eID="Matt.15.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.15" osisID="Matt.15.15" n="15"/>Then, Pedro (Peter) answered and said, “My Master, interpret this word for us.”<verse eID="Matt.15.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.16" osisID="Matt.15.16" n="16"/>And then he said, “Yet likewise, you haven’t become bearers of intelligence?”<verse eID="Matt.15.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.17" osisID="Matt.15.17" n="17"/>“Do you know that all of what goes to the mouth to the belly, it goes. And from there it is purified, and it goes outside?”<verse eID="Matt.15.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.18" osisID="Matt.15.18" n="18"/>“And all that goes out from the mouth, goes out from the heart, and it defiles sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.15.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.19" osisID="Matt.15.19" n="19"/>“From the heart go out evil thoughts, and killing, and fornication, theft, adultery, lying witness, and defaming.”<verse eID="Matt.15.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.20" osisID="Matt.15.20" n="20"/>“These are those that defile sons of man, but whoever eats without handwashing is not defiled.”<verse eID="Matt.15.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.21" osisID="Matt.15.21" n="21"/>Then Yeshua went out from there and came to the region of Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon).<verse eID="Matt.15.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.22" osisID="Matt.15.22" n="22"/>Then came a Canaanite woman from that region, shouting and crying, saying, “Have mercy on me, my Lord, son of David; my daughter is seized by a demon!”<verse eID="Matt.15.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.23" osisID="Matt.15.23" n="23"/>He did not respond any word, and his talmidim (students) approached and sought of him, and said, “Leave her, for she weeps after us!”<verse eID="Matt.15.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.24" osisID="Matt.15.24" n="24"/>He ‒ he answered and said to her,” I was not sent except to the lost sheep from the house of Yisrael (Israel).”<verse eID="Matt.15.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.25" osisID="Matt.15.25" n="25"/>In the hour she came, she wept, “My Master, help me!”<verse eID="Matt.15.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.26" osisID="Matt.15.26" n="26"/>Yeshua said to her, “It is not fitting for you to take a morsel of bread belonging to the children and give it to the dogs.”<verse eID="Matt.15.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.27" osisID="Matt.15.27" n="27"/>She ‒ she replied, “So, the dogs eat of the crumbs of bread that falls from the table of our masters, and they live.”<verse eID="Matt.15.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.28" osisID="Matt.15.28" n="28"/>Then Yeshua answered, “Woman, great is your faith; may it be done for you as you desired.” And her daughter was healed from that hour.<verse eID="Matt.15.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.29" osisID="Matt.15.29" n="29"/>And he left there and came upon the southern sea of Galil (Galilee), and he ascended a mountain and sat there.<verse eID="Matt.15.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.30" osisID="Matt.15.30" n="30"/>Then approached him, all the communities, many who had with them, cripples, blind, deaf, and sickness of gout of the feet, and many others who fell at the feet of Yeshua, and he healed them.<verse eID="Matt.15.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.31" osisID="Matt.15.31" n="31"/>How astonished were the communities who saw a mute speaking, and one possessed of gout, healed, and the lame walking and the blind seeing. So, they praised the God of Yisrael (Israel)!<verse eID="Matt.15.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.32" osisID="Matt.15.32" n="32"/>Then he approached his talmidim (students) and said to them, “I have compassion on this community which these three days dwelled with me, and in case they did not have food, and I don’t want to send them away fasting, lest they become wearied on the way.<verse eID="Matt.15.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.33" osisID="Matt.15.33" n="33"/>His talmidim (students) said, "Where will bread come to us in this dry place to satisfy all this community?”<verse eID="Matt.15.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.34" osisID="Matt.15.34" n="34"/>He said to them, “How much bread do you have?” They said, “Seven loaves and a few small fish.”<verse eID="Matt.15.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.35" osisID="Matt.15.35" n="35"/>Then he commanded the community to sit on the earth.<verse eID="Matt.15.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.36" osisID="Matt.15.36" n="36"/>Then he took the seven bread and fish and blessed. Then he cut it for his disciples, and his disciples gave to the community.<verse eID="Matt.15.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.37" osisID="Matt.15.37" n="37"/>And all of them ate and were satisfied. And there remained of the crumbles, seven baskets.<verse eID="Matt.15.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.38" osisID="Matt.15.38" n="38"/>This who ate, there were four thousand men, apart from the little ones, and women.<verse eID="Matt.15.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.39" osisID="Matt.15.39" n="39"/>And when the community went away, he ascended the ship and came to the area of Megiddo.<verse eID="Matt.15.39"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.15"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.16" osisID="Matt.16" n="16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.1" osisID="Matt.16.1" n="1"/>And the Pharisees and Sadducees approached to test him and ask him for a sign from the heavens, shown to them.<verse eID="Matt.16.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.2" osisID="Matt.16.2" n="2"/>Then he answered, and he said to them, “When it is evening time, you say, ‘It is a good hour, because the heavens are red.’”<verse eID="Matt.16.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.3" osisID="Matt.16.3" n="3"/>“And in the morning you say, ‘It is a cold day, and the heavens were red in color.’ Unbelievers! Do you know he said to examine the signs of the heavens, but even a mark of time, you do not know how to differentiate?”<verse eID="Matt.16.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.4" osisID="Matt.16.4" n="4"/>An evil and stubborn tribe! You seek a sign, but a sign is not given, but of Yonah (Jonah), the Prophet.” And he left them and went away.<verse eID="Matt.16.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.5" osisID="Matt.16.5" n="5"/>And when he came with his disciples to pass over that side, they forgot to take bread with them.<verse eID="Matt.16.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.6" osisID="Matt.16.6" n="6"/>He said this to them, “Beware of the tricks of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”<verse eID="Matt.16.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.7" osisID="Matt.16.7" n="7"/>And they were comforted in their heart and said, “Why is all this, since we did not take bread?”<verse eID="Matt.16.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.8" osisID="Matt.16.8" n="8"/>And Yeshua, when he understood, said to them, “Why are you thinking in your heart, who are little of faith, regarding the bread you did not bring?”<verse eID="Matt.16.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.9" osisID="Matt.16.9" n="9"/>“Until now, you did not understand, nor know those five breads for the five thousand, and how many baskets you took away?”<verse eID="Matt.16.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.10" osisID="Matt.16.10" n="10"/>“And even those seven bread for the four thousand, and how many baskets you took away?”<verse eID="Matt.16.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.11" osisID="Matt.16.11" n="11"/>“Truth, you did not understand that I told you about the bread? Rather, you should beware of the tricks<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="255" osisRef="Matt.16.11" osisID="Matt.16.11!note.255">In Matthew 13:33, the word שאור (lse’or) meaning “leaven” was used. Yet, in this verse, leaven is not recorded, but a word that means,</note> of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”<verse eID="Matt.16.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.12" osisID="Matt.16.12" n="12"/>After this, they understood that he did not say to beware of the bread, but of the Talmud (teaching) of the Pharisees and Sadducees.<verse eID="Matt.16.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.13" osisID="Matt.16.13" n="13"/>When Yeshua came to the land of Caesar of Phillippos (Philip), he asked his talmidim and said, “What do the sons of adam say about me, if I am a son of a man?”<verse eID="Matt.16.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.14" osisID="Matt.16.14" n="14"/>They replied to him and said, “There are some saying he is Yochanan (John) the Converter, and some say Eliyyah (Elijah), and others say Yirmeyah (Jeremiah), or one of the prophets.<verse eID="Matt.16.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.15" osisID="Matt.16.15" n="15"/>He said to them, “What do you say about me? Who am I?”<verse eID="Matt.16.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.16" osisID="Matt.16.16" n="16"/>Pedro (Peter) replied and said to him, “You are the Messiah, Son of the Living Elohim!”<verse eID="Matt.16.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.17" osisID="Matt.16.17" n="17"/>Yeshua answered and said, “Good for you, Pedro (Peter), son of Yonah (Jonah), for flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father in the heavens!”<verse eID="Matt.16.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.18" osisID="Matt.16.18" n="18"/>I also say to you that you are Pedro (Peter), for on this stone, I build an assembly (knesset); and the gates of Gehinnom will not inherit it.<verse eID="Matt.16.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19" n="19"/>To you <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="256" osisRef="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19!note.256">This is a masculine singular second person. This was spoken only to Peter and not to the other disciples. This is also true in the Greek, as well.</note>I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit in the world will be prohibited in heaven, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.16.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.20" osisID="Matt.16.20" n="20"/>After this, he commanded his talmidim (students) that they shall not say the son of man that he is the Messiah.<verse eID="Matt.16.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.21" osisID="Matt.16.21" n="21"/>And from that hour, Yeshua began to hint to his talmidim (students) that he would get ready to go up to Jerusalem and greatly quarrel with the elders, the priests, and the scribes, and that they would kill him, but on the third day he would arise.<verse eID="Matt.16.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.22" osisID="Matt.16.22" n="22"/>And Pedro (Peter) spoke and said, “God forbid that this evil be to you, my Master.”<verse eID="Matt.16.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.23" osisID="Matt.16.23" n="23"/>Then he responded and said to Pedro (Peter), “Go you! Get behind me, evil inclination (Yetzer ha-Ra)! Already, you do not have words like children of Elohim (God), but like sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.16.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.24" osisID="Matt.16.24" n="24"/>Then Yeshua said to his disciples, “All who want to come after me will give up his soul to death and come after me.”<verse eID="Matt.16.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.25" osisID="Matt.16.25" n="25"/>For all who want a restful spirit for his soul, even he will lose it on my account, and all who destroy his own soul for me will rest.<verse eID="Matt.16.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.26" osisID="Matt.16.26" n="26"/>“All who purchase one man, it is as if he bought all the world, but while reducing his own soul in death ‒ or will give him a son of man as atonement for his soul?”<verse eID="Matt.16.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.27" osisID="Matt.16.27" n="27"/>The Son of man is about to come in the excellencies of his Father with the holy angels, and then he will repay all of man and woman according to their works.<verse eID="Matt.16.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.28" osisID="Matt.16.28" n="28"/>“Truth I say to you, that there are people sitting here who will not taste death until they see the Son of Man about to go in his kingdom.”<verse eID="Matt.16.28"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.16"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.17" osisID="Matt.17" n="17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.1" osisID="Matt.17.1" n="1"/>And after six days Yeshua took Pedro (Peter) and Ya’akov (Jacob), and Yochanan (John), his brother, and caused them to ascend alone.<verse eID="Matt.17.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.2" osisID="Matt.17.2" n="2"/>And Yeshua changed his appearance and his face shone like the sun, and his clothing was white like radiance.<verse eID="Matt.17.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.3" osisID="Matt.17.3" n="3"/>And it resembled to them there, the faces of Moses and Elijah, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="257" osisRef="Matt.17.3" osisID="Matt.17.3!note.257">It is understood by scholars that Moses represents the Torah, and Elijah represents the Prophets. Therefore, for Moses and Elijah to be</note>who were speaking with him.<verse eID="Matt.17.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.4" osisID="Matt.17.4" n="4"/>Pedro (Peter) answered and said to Yeshua, “My Lord, that we should sit here, and if it is acceptable in your eyes, let us make three places: One for you and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”<verse eID="Matt.17.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.5" osisID="Matt.17.5" n="5"/>And when he was speaking, there was a bright cloud and a shadow over them, and a voice came out from the cloud, “Behold my beloved son, and in him I am satisfied, and to him, you will listen.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="258" osisRef="Matt.17.5" osisID="Matt.17.5!note.258">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note><verse eID="Matt.17.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.6" osisID="Matt.17.6" n="6"/>And when the talmidim (students) heard, they fell upon their faces and greatly feared.<verse eID="Matt.17.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.7" osisID="Matt.17.7" n="7"/>And Yeshua approached them and said to them, “Arise, and do not fear!”<verse eID="Matt.17.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.8" osisID="Matt.17.8" n="8"/>And they opened their eyes and did not see any man except for Yeshua alone.<verse eID="Matt.17.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.9" osisID="Matt.17.9" n="9"/>And when they descended from the mountain, Yeshua ordered and said to them, “Regarding my aspects of the Son of man's vision until the Son of man is risen from the dead.”<verse eID="Matt.17.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.10" osisID="Matt.17.10" n="10"/>And his talmidim (students) asked and said to him, “The scribes say that Eliyyah (Elijah) will come first.”<verse eID="Matt.17.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.11" osisID="Matt.17.11" n="11"/>Yeshua answered and said to them, “Elyyah (Elijah) will come first to whoever acknowledges in him, he will be found.”<verse eID="Matt.17.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.12" osisID="Matt.17.12" n="12"/>I say to you, Eliyyah (Elijah) came and you did not know him, and they did with him all that they wanted: likewise, the Son of man is about to be struck by them.”<verse eID="Matt.17.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.13" osisID="Matt.17.13" n="13"/>And then the talmidim (students) understood that he was speaking to them about Yochanan (John) the purifier.<verse eID="Matt.17.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.14" osisID="Matt.17.14" n="14"/>Then, when they had come to the house of their community, and one man drew near to him and bowed down upon his knees and said to him,<verse eID="Matt.17.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.15" osisID="Matt.17.15" n="15"/>“My Lord have mercy upon my son; he has become greatly mad, because sometimes he falls in the water and other times in the fire.”<verse eID="Matt.17.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.16" osisID="Matt.17.16" n="16"/>“So I brought him to your talmidim (students), but they were not able to heal him.”<verse eID="Matt.17.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.17" osisID="Matt.17.17" n="17"/>Yeshua answered and said, “I am toward <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="260" osisRef="Matt.17.17" osisID="Matt.17.17!note.260">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note>you, lowly generation, who does not believe. Until when do you stray? And until when must you be taught by me? Bring him here!”<verse eID="Matt.17.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.18" osisID="Matt.17.18" n="18"/>And Yeshua rebuked and expelled it to come out of him. And the child was healed in that hour.<verse eID="Matt.17.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.19" osisID="Matt.17.19" n="19"/>In that hour, his talmidim (students) drew near Yeshua and said to him, “Why were we not able to heal him?”<verse eID="Matt.17.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.20" osisID="Matt.17.20" n="20"/>Yeshua said to them, “Because you did not believe well. Truth I say to you, that if there was faith in you as a lone mustard, you could even say to the mountain that it will change its place, and it would be changed. And additionally, they will not lack of anything.<verse eID="Matt.17.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.21" osisID="Matt.17.21" n="21"/>Behold, this generation will not go out but if by prayer and in fasting.<verse eID="Matt.17.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.22" osisID="Matt.17.22" n="22"/>When they were turning into Galil (Galilee), Yeshua said to them, “The Son of man is about to fall into the hand of a son of a man,”<verse eID="Matt.17.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.23" osisID="Matt.17.23" n="23" subType="x-alt-17.22b"/>and they will kill him, and on the third day, he will rise.” And they were greatly grieved.<verse eID="Matt.17.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.24" osisID="Matt.17.24" n="24" subType="x-alt-17.23"/>And when he came to Kephar Nahum (Capernaum), the tax collectors, who take two by two zuzin-coins<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="264" osisRef="Matt.17.24" osisID="Matt.17.24!note.264">Biblical silver coin, ¼ of a shekel. Historically, the zuz and dinar were used interchangeably.</note> for no sum,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="265" osisRef="Matt.17.24" osisID="Matt.17.24!note.265">Or may also mean, “for no debt.”</note> came before Pedro (Peter) and said to him, “Does not your Lord not pay the choicest money, his two zuzim-coins?”<verse eID="Matt.17.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.25" osisID="Matt.17.25" n="25"/>He said to them, “Good.” And when he (Peter) came near Yeshua to the house, Yeshua said to him, “What do you think, Pedro (Peter)? In the kingdom of this earth, from those who collect the tax, do they first collect from their children or from strangers?”<verse eID="Matt.17.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.26" osisID="Matt.17.26" n="26"/>Pedro (Peter) said to him, “From strangers, they first collect.” Yeshua said to him, “If so, the children will be children of freedom.”<verse eID="Matt.17.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.27" osisID="Matt.17.27" n="27"/>But so not to cause them to stumble, go to the sea and cast your fork into the water, and the fish that comes out first, you will open its mouth and find one stater-coin,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="266" osisRef="Matt.17.27" osisID="Matt.17.27!note.266">The Stater coin was a Greek coin used from 8th century BC to 50 AD. This coin played a significant role in trade.</note> and take it and give on behalf of me and for you.<verse eID="Matt.17.27"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.17"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.18" osisID="Matt.18" n="18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.1" osisID="Matt.18.1" n="1"/>In that hour, the talmidim (students) approached Yeshua, and said, “Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”<verse eID="Matt.18.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.2" osisID="Matt.18.2" n="2"/>Then Yeshua called for the boy, and he stood in their midst.<verse eID="Matt.18.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.3" osisID="Matt.18.3" n="3"/>And He said, “Truth I say that if you do not repent and make yourselves like children, you will not enter into the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.4" osisID="Matt.18.4" n="4"/>“He who humbles himself like this boy, he will be great in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.5" osisID="Matt.18.5" n="5"/>“And whoever receives the boy in my name, he receives Me,”<verse eID="Matt.18.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.6" osisID="Matt.18.6" n="6"/>“and all who become stumbling blocks for children who believe in me, it is better for him to be given millstones that are hung by his neck and drown himself in the sea.”<verse eID="Matt.18.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.7" osisID="Matt.18.7" n="7"/>“Woe to the world because of those attacks! For there is about to come to him a stumbling block. Woe to the man for whose sake the attacks will come!”<verse eID="Matt.18.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.8" osisID="Matt.18.8" n="8"/>“If your hand or your foot snares you, cut it off and throw it from you, for it is better for you to come to life lame or left-handed and not with two feet and two hands into the fire and into Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.18.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.9" osisID="Matt.18.9" n="9"/>“And if your eye causes you to fail, you should pluck it out and throw it out; it is better to enter with one eye into life than two eyes into Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.18.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.10" osisID="Matt.18.10" n="10"/>“And you will see not to despise even one of the little ones: I say for the angels are seen all the time before my Father in heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.11" osisID="Matt.18.11" n="11"/>“Because I, the Son of man, redeem all those who are lost.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="269" osisRef="Matt.18.11" osisID="Matt.18.11!note.269">This verse is absent from early Greek manuscripts such as Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus. This verse is similar to Luke 19:10 and may have been inserted at some point to harmonize the Gospels.</note><verse eID="Matt.18.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.12" osisID="Matt.18.12" n="12"/>And from this, you will see: if there are sons of man that have a hundred sheep, and one who was lost, would they not leave remaining the ninety-nine, and intensely seek for the one that was lost?<verse eID="Matt.18.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.13" osisID="Matt.18.13" n="13"/>“And if it is found, truth, I say to you, there will be with this one, more joy than from the ninety-nine that were not lost.”<verse eID="Matt.18.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.14" osisID="Matt.18.14" n="14"/>“Is there not this same desire with your Father <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="271" osisRef="Matt.18.14" osisID="Matt.18.14!note.271">Early manuscripts as well as the Byzantine collection include “your Father” in this verse. Whereas later manuscripts as well as the</note>in the heavens on losing of one of these little ones?”<verse eID="Matt.18.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.15" osisID="Matt.18.15" n="15"/>“If one of your brothers sinned against you, go and make peace between you and him, and if he has heard you, you have gained your brother.”<verse eID="Matt.18.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.16" osisID="Matt.18.16" n="16"/>“And if he will not hear, take two or three witnesses so that on the mouth of two witnesses, or by the mouth of three witnesses, every word will be established.”<verse eID="Matt.18.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.17" osisID="Matt.18.17" n="17"/>“And if this one does not hear, even to them, you are to tell the assembly, and if to the assembly, likewise, he does not hear, then he is to you as vile and despised.”<verse eID="Matt.18.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.18" osisID="Matt.18.18" n="18"/>“And truth I say to you, all what is bound on earth, will be bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="272" osisRef="Matt.18.18" osisID="Matt.18.18!note.272">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note><verse eID="Matt.18.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.19" osisID="Matt.18.19" n="19"/>“Again, I say to you, ‘If two of you desire one thing on this earth, regarding all you desire that you (intensely) ask, it will be for you by my Father who is in the heavens.’”<verse eID="Matt.18.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.20" osisID="Matt.18.20" n="20"/>“And in the place that two or three are assembled together for my name, I will be amongst them.”<verse eID="Matt.18.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.21" osisID="Matt.18.21" n="21"/>Then Pedro (Peter) approached him and said to him, “My Master, how many times if my brother sins against me, am I to forgive him? Up to seven times?”<verse eID="Matt.18.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.22" osisID="Matt.18.22" n="22"/>Yeshua said to him, “I have not said seven times, but seventy times seven, seven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.23" osisID="Matt.18.23" n="23"/>“Because the kingdom of heaven is likened to one king who wanted to take account of his servants,”<verse eID="Matt.18.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.24" osisID="Matt.18.24" n="24"/>“and when taking account, there was one debtor who was in debt of ten thousand talents.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="274" osisRef="Matt.18.24" osisID="Matt.18.24!note.274">See Esther 3:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.18.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.25" osisID="Matt.18.25" n="25"/>“And when he was not able to pay it off, the master ordered him that he would sell his wife and children, and whatever he had entirely, and to pay.”<verse eID="Matt.18.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.26" osisID="Matt.18.26" n="26"/>Then he fell and bowed down and said, “My Master, be long-suffering (of your anger) and I will pay all of that there is to you.<verse eID="Matt.18.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.27" osisID="Matt.18.27" n="27"/>“And his master pitied him and released him and even forgave his debt.”<verse eID="Matt.18.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.28" osisID="Matt.18.28" n="28"/>This servant went out and found one man who was a debtor to him a hundred, and he seized him and strangled him and said, “You will pay me my debt!”<verse eID="Matt.18.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.29" osisID="Matt.18.29" n="29"/>And the debtor <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="275" osisRef="Matt.18.29" osisID="Matt.18.29!note.275">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note>fell at his foot and begged, “You should be patient and I will pay it off.”<verse eID="Matt.18.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.30" osisID="Matt.18.30" n="30"/>And this one did not want to, but he went and left him in the house of prisoners until he repaid the debt.<verse eID="Matt.18.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.31" osisID="Matt.18.31" n="31"/>When he saw this, their friends were greatly furious and came and informed their Lord about what had happened.<verse eID="Matt.18.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.32" osisID="Matt.18.32" n="32"/>The master called to him and said to him, “Wicked servant, how of all the debts I did forgive you because of your request!”<verse eID="Matt.18.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.33" osisID="Matt.18.33" n="33"/>“And should you not have mercy upon your friend like I had mercy over you?”<verse eID="Matt.18.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.34" osisID="Matt.18.34" n="34"/>And the master was angry and condemned him to beatings until he would pay off all his debt.<verse eID="Matt.18.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.35" osisID="Matt.18.35" n="35"/>“Thus does my Father do to you, if you do not forgive and pardon every man, their brother, from a willing heart.”<verse eID="Matt.18.35"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.18"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.19" osisID="Matt.19" n="19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.1" osisID="Matt.19.1" n="1"/>And when these words of Yeshua’s were completed, he journeyed from Galil (Galillee) and came to the area of Yehudim (Jews) across the Yarden (Jordan).<verse eID="Matt.19.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.2" osisID="Matt.19.2" n="2"/>Then a great community went after him, and he healed them there.<verse eID="Matt.19.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.3" osisID="Matt.19.3" n="3"/>And the Pharisees approached him and were testing him and saying to him, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every accusation?”<verse eID="Matt.19.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4" n="4"/>He <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="276" osisRef="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4!note.276">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>answers and says to them, “Did you not read that He who made them, 'created them,' in the beginning?<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="277" osisRef="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4!note.277">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.5" osisID="Matt.19.5" n="5"/>And he said, “Because of this, leave his father and his mother, and cleave to his wife, and they will become one flesh.”<verse eID="Matt.19.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.6" osisID="Matt.19.6" n="6"/>“From this, it is seen, it is not two, but one body: Because thus, Elohim (God) made a pair of human beings, that he would not divorce."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="278" osisRef="Matt.19.6" osisID="Matt.19.6!note.278">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.7" osisID="Matt.19.7" n="7"/>He said to him, “Why did Moses do it this way? That he commanded to give a writing of divorce and release.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="279" osisRef="Matt.19.7" osisID="Matt.19.7!note.279">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.8" osisID="Matt.19.8" n="8"/>And he said to them, “Mosheh (Moses), for heaviness of heart, that you have, allowed for you to divorce your wives, but from the beginning, it was not so.”<verse eID="Matt.19.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9" n="9"/>“I say to you, whoever sends away <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="281" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.281">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>his wife without her being a fornicator <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="282" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.282">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>and takes another wife, commits fornication: and whoever that marries her (i.e., the first wife),<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="283" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.283">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note> commits fornication.”<verse eID="Matt.19.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.10" osisID="Matt.19.10" n="10"/>His talmidim (students) said to him, “If such is the case between husbands with a wife, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="284" osisRef="Matt.19.10" osisID="Matt.19.10!note.284">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note>then he will not be able to marry a wife.”<verse eID="Matt.19.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.11" osisID="Matt.19.11" n="11"/>He said to them, “Not every man is able to learn this, except whoever it is given to.”<verse eID="Matt.19.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.12" osisID="Matt.19.12" n="12"/>There is whomever that has faith from the womb of his mother, and is born, there is whomever that has faith, it is only his faith by man. And there are some who have faith who did by their own will in faith for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. And whoever wills to succeed, is able to succeed.”<verse eID="Matt.19.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.13" osisID="Matt.19.13" n="13"/>After this, they brought children to him, so that he may place his hand upon them to bless them, but his talmidim (students) were not willing.<verse eID="Matt.19.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.14" osisID="Matt.19.14" n="14"/>Then Yeshua said, “Allow the children to come to me, and do not withhold them, for they are little ones, but they will be in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.19.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.15" osisID="Matt.19.15" n="15"/>And he placed his hand upon them, and went from there.<verse eID="Matt.19.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.16" osisID="Matt.19.16" n="16"/>And one approached him and said to him, “Good Teacher, what good thing should I do that I may have eternal life?”<verse eID="Matt.19.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.17" osisID="Matt.19.17" n="17"/>And he said to him, “Why do you call me good? There is none good except One. And if you want to live forever, keep His commandments.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="285" osisRef="Matt.19.17" osisID="Matt.19.17!note.285">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note><verse eID="Matt.19.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.18" osisID="Matt.19.18" n="18"/>And he said to him. Behold! These are the commandments that Yeshua said, “You shall not murder, nor shall you commit adultery, nor shall you steal, nor shall you bear false witness against your neighbor,”<verse eID="Matt.19.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.19" osisID="Matt.19.19" n="19"/>“honor your father and mother, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself.”<verse eID="Matt.19.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.20" osisID="Matt.19.20" n="20"/>He said to him, “Since I was a young man in my adolescence, I kept all these. Even so, what am I lacking the most?<verse eID="Matt.19.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.21" osisID="Matt.19.21" n="21"/>Yeshua said to them, “If you want to be complete, sell your inheritance and give it to the poor, and it will be there for you in heaven; then come follow me.”<verse eID="Matt.19.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.22" osisID="Matt.19.22" n="22"/>And when this young man heard these words, he went away in sorrow, for he had much property.<verse eID="Matt.19.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.23" osisID="Matt.19.23" n="23"/>Yeshua said this to his students (talmidim), “Truth I say to you, the rich are yet to ascend to the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.19.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.24" osisID="Matt.19.24" n="24"/>I, I say, as it will be much easier for that camel to enter the small hole of a needle than for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.19.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.25" osisID="Matt.19.25" n="25"/>The talmidim (students), when they heard, were much amazed and said with amazement of heart, “Who is able to be delivered accordingly for eternal life?”<verse eID="Matt.19.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.26" osisID="Matt.19.26" n="26"/>Yeshua shouted at them and said to them in anger, “It is not possible for the sons of man: with Elohim (God), all what they seek, they will find.”<verse eID="Matt.19.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.27" osisID="Matt.19.27" n="27"/>Then Pedro (Peter) answered, and said to him,” Behold, we left all we had and went after you. Why will it not be so for us?”<verse eID="Matt.19.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.28" osisID="Matt.19.28" n="28"/>Yeshua said to them, “In truth, I say to you that you, who follow me in this world, that like this we sit on the throne of the kingdom, therefore you will also sit upon twelve thrones and judge over the twelve tribes of Israel.”<verse eID="Matt.19.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.29" osisID="Matt.19.29" n="29"/>“And everyone who leaves his household, or his brothers, or his sister, or his father, or his mother, or his wife, or his children, or relatives for my name, in truth, they will receive their reward and live for eternal life.”<verse eID="Matt.19.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.30" osisID="Matt.19.30" n="30"/>Many of those who were first <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="286" osisRef="Matt.19.30" osisID="Matt.19.30!note.286">The word קדמונים is the Hebrew equivalent of the Aramaic ܐܝܡܕܩ (qaddimāya), meaning the “first ones” from the ancient world. While accurately</note>became last, and the last became first.<verse eID="Matt.19.30"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.19"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.20" osisID="Matt.20" n="20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.1" osisID="Matt.20.1" n="1"/>This kingdom of heaven resembles a man, the owner of the house, who went outside in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.<verse eID="Matt.20.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.2" osisID="Matt.20.2" n="2"/>And he resolved with his workers for one dinar <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="287" osisRef="Matt.20.2" osisID="Matt.20.2!note.287">Dinar was a silver coin of ancient Rome, minted around 211 BC.</note>for the day, and sent them to the vineyard.<verse eID="Matt.20.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.3" osisID="Matt.20.3" n="3"/>And he went in three hours and saw others standing in the market, and unemployed.<verse eID="Matt.20.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.4" osisID="Matt.20.4" n="4"/>And he said to them, “Go also you to the vineyard, and whatever is appropriate I will give to you.”<verse eID="Matt.20.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.5" osisID="Matt.20.5" n="5"/>And they went. Then he went out again in six hours and in nine hours, and he did just so.<verse eID="Matt.20.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.6" osisID="Matt.20.6" n="6"/>And before eleven hours, he went out and found others who were standing, and unemployed, and he said to them, “Why are you standing all day long unemployed?”<verse eID="Matt.20.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.7" osisID="Matt.20.7" n="7"/>They said to him, “There is no man to hire us.” He said to them, “You are also to go to the vineyard, and whatever is appropriate I will give you.”<verse eID="Matt.20.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.8" osisID="Matt.20.8" n="8"/>As soon as it was night, the master of the vineyard said to his attendant, “Call for the workers and give to them their wages and begin from the last to the first!”<verse eID="Matt.20.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.9" osisID="Matt.20.9" n="9"/>“And those came for eleven hours, and they took a dinar coin each.”<verse eID="Matt.20.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.10" osisID="Matt.20.10" n="10"/>“And when the first came, they thought that he would acquire more for them. And they did not take, but rather a dinar coin each.”<verse eID="Matt.20.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.11" osisID="Matt.20.11" n="11"/>“And when they took it, thus they murmured about the owner of the house, and they said,”<verse eID="Matt.20.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.12" osisID="Matt.20.12" n="12"/>“‘These last ones who did not work one hour, you paid them the same as us, for we did the heavy work in the heat of the day.’”<verse eID="Matt.20.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.13" osisID="Matt.20.13" n="13"/>He responded and said to them, “My friend, I have not wronged you. Did I not make a bargain with you for a dinar-coin?”<verse eID="Matt.20.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.14" osisID="Matt.20.14" n="14"/>“Take what belongs to you and go! But I want to give to those following the same as you.”<verse eID="Matt.20.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.15" osisID="Matt.20.15" n="15"/>“There is no one who will rule over my decision, doing what I desire! So, I will not be darkened<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="290" osisRef="Matt.20.15" osisID="Matt.20.15!note.290">This example illustrates a Hebrew idiom contrasting an “eye of light” with an “eye of darkness.” The former signifies generosity, while the latter</note> because of your evil eye, and I am a good person.”<verse eID="Matt.20.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.16" osisID="Matt.20.16" n="16"/>“Therefore, the last will be first, and the first, last; many will be called, but few will be chosen.”<verse eID="Matt.20.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.17" osisID="Matt.20.17" n="17"/>And Yeshua passed on to Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) and he took his twelve talmidim (students) with him, and on the way he said to them,<verse eID="Matt.20.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.18" osisID="Matt.20.18" n="18"/>“Behold! We go up to Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), and the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and to the scribes, and he will be condemned<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="292" osisRef="Matt.20.18" osisID="Matt.20.18!note.292">This word יתחייב (yitchayev) is a Hit’pael verb binyan that has a reflexive or reciprocal voice. In this situation, it appears to be reflexive, and he</note> to death.”<verse eID="Matt.20.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.19" osisID="Matt.20.19" n="19"/>“And they will deliver him to the people,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="293" osisRef="Matt.20.19" osisID="Matt.20.19!note.293">Or can translate to “nation” (singular).</note> and beat him, and curse him, and impale <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="294" osisRef="Matt.20.19" osisID="Matt.20.19!note.294">This is also the same word for “crucify.”</note>him, and on the third day he will rise.”<verse eID="Matt.20.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.20" osisID="Matt.20.20" n="20"/>Then there approached him the sons of Zebdi (Zebedee) and their mother; she and her sons. Then bowed down to him, and she asked him a word,<verse eID="Matt.20.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.21" osisID="Matt.20.21" n="21"/>And this one said to her, “What do you want from me?” She said to him, “That these two sons of mine will sit, one on your right and one on your left, in the kingdom.”<verse eID="Matt.20.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.22" osisID="Matt.20.22" n="22"/>Yeshua answered and said, “You do not know what you ask. Are you able to drink the cup which I am about to drink also, and be purified with the purification which purified me?” They said to him, “We are able.”<verse eID="Matt.20.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.23" osisID="Matt.20.23" n="23"/>He said to them, “In truth, you will drink of my cup, and my purification you will be purified, but to sit by my right or my left, I cannot, but it is for those of whom my Father prepared.”<verse eID="Matt.20.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.24" osisID="Matt.20.24" n="24"/>When they heard this, those ten became angry at these two brothers.<verse eID="Matt.20.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.25" osisID="Matt.20.25" n="25"/>Then Yeshua called them and said, “You know as well that the leaders of the people will have dominion over them, and the chiefs of the people rule over the leaders.”<verse eID="Matt.20.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.26" osisID="Matt.20.26" n="26"/>“Not so should it be between you, but whoever wants to be great, let him be your servants.”<verse eID="Matt.20.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.27" osisID="Matt.20.27" n="27"/>“And whoever from you wants to be first, let him be your servants.”<verse eID="Matt.20.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.28" osisID="Matt.20.28" n="28"/>Thus, this Son of man did not come for others to serve him, but he came to serve and deliver himself over to save many people.<verse eID="Matt.20.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.29" osisID="Matt.20.29" n="29"/>And when Yeshua went from Yeriho (Jericho), then a great community followed him.<verse eID="Matt.20.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.30" osisID="Matt.20.30" n="30"/>And behold, two blind ones sat on the mouth of the road, and when they heard Yeshua passing, they gave a shout and said, “Have mercy on us, our Lord, son of David!”<verse eID="Matt.20.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.31" osisID="Matt.20.31" n="31"/>And the community was rebuking them to be silent, and they were raising their voice and saying, “Have mercy on us, son of David!”<verse eID="Matt.20.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.32" osisID="Matt.20.32" n="32"/>And Yeshua rose and called them and said, “What do you want me to do for you?”<verse eID="Matt.20.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.33" osisID="Matt.20.33" n="33"/>He said to him, “Our Lord, that you open our eyes.”<verse eID="Matt.20.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.20.34" osisID="Matt.20.34" n="34"/>And he had compassion on them and touched their eyes, and in that hour, their eyes were opened, and they followed him.<verse eID="Matt.20.34"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.20"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.21" osisID="Matt.21" n="21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.1" osisID="Matt.21.1" n="1"/>When he approached Yershalayim (Jerusalem) and Bet Pageh (Bethphage), on the south of the Mount of Olives, Yeshua sent two of his talmidim (students) and said to them,<verse eID="Matt.21.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.2" osisID="Matt.21.2" n="2"/>Go to the city that is before you, and quickly you will find one donkey tied, and a female camel with her. Then loosen her and bring them to me.<verse eID="Matt.21.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.3" osisID="Matt.21.3" n="3"/>And if anyone says anything to you, you are to say, “The Master wants this.” Then he will send them quickly.”<verse eID="Matt.21.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.4" osisID="Matt.21.4" n="4"/>This is like what was spoken by the prophet:<verse eID="Matt.21.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.5" osisID="Matt.21.5" n="5"/>“Say to the daughter of Tsion (Zion), ‘Behold, your king comes humbled, and riding on a donkey and on a foal, the offspring of a she-donkey.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="297" osisRef="Matt.21.5" osisID="Matt.21.5!note.297">Isaiah 62:11, Zechariah 9:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.21.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.6" osisID="Matt.21.6" n="6"/>And his talmidim (students) left and did as Yeshua commanded them.<verse eID="Matt.21.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.7" osisID="Matt.21.7" n="7"/>And they brought the donkey and her offspring, and put on her offspring their clothes, and Yeshua rode on it.<verse eID="Matt.21.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.8" osisID="Matt.21.8" n="8"/>And the assemblies were being prepared and spreading their clothes on the road, and others cut greenery from a tree and threw them on the earth.<verse eID="Matt.21.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.9" osisID="Matt.21.9" n="9"/>This assembly of this one, who went before and after him, shouted and said, “Hoshana, and blessed is he who comes of Master Yehovah!<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="299" osisRef="Matt.21.9" osisID="Matt.21.9!note.299">MarYah מריה is the Aramaic equivalent of the Hebrew אדני יהוה (Adonai Yehovah).</note> Hoshanah from on high!”<verse eID="Matt.21.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.10" osisID="Matt.21.10" n="10"/>And when he was in Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), every province trembled, and they said, “Who is this?”<verse eID="Matt.21.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.11" osisID="Matt.21.11" n="11"/>This assembly said, “This is Yeshua, the prophet from Netzaret (Nazareth) of Galil (Galilee).”<verse eID="Matt.21.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.12" osisID="Matt.21.12" n="12"/>And Yeshua came to the Holy Place of Elohim and expelled everyone from there, buying and selling in the Holy Place, and he seized the table of the moneychanger and also the chair of those selling doves.<verse eID="Matt.21.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.13" osisID="Matt.21.13" n="13"/>And he said to them, “It is written: ‘My house will be called a house of prayer,’ <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="300" osisRef="Matt.21.13" osisID="Matt.21.13!note.300">Isaiah 56:7.</note>you have made this a pit of thieves.’”<verse eID="Matt.21.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.14" osisID="Matt.21.14" n="14"/>And the blind and lame approached him, and he healed them.<verse eID="Matt.21.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.15" osisID="Matt.21.15" n="15"/>And when the greatest of the priests and scribes saw this, they were astonished over this. And the youths in the Holy Place were saying, “Hoshanah to the son of David!” They were angry at them.<verse eID="Matt.21.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.16" osisID="Matt.21.16" n="16"/>And they said to him, “Has he heard what these say?” Yeshua said to them, “In truth and by oath, have you not heard? And ‘from the mouth of youths and sucklings, you have prepared praises?’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="301" osisRef="Matt.21.16" osisID="Matt.21.16!note.301">This phrase is a quotation from Psalm 8:3 in the Septuagint translation: “From the mouth of babies and nursing infants, you have established praise</note><verse eID="Matt.21.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.17" osisID="Matt.21.17" n="17"/>Then having left them, and went outside of the province to Beit Aniyyah (Bethany) and he lodged there.<verse eID="Matt.21.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.18" osisID="Matt.21.18" n="18"/>And in the morning, when he returned to the province, he was hungry.<verse eID="Matt.21.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.19" osisID="Matt.21.19" n="19"/>Then he saw one fig tree by the road and came near it and did not find anything on it except leaves only. And he said to it, “Let there not be more fruit on you, forever.” And quickly, the fig tree dried up.<verse eID="Matt.21.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.20" osisID="Matt.21.20" n="20"/>And his talmidim (students) were amazed and said, “How did the fig tree dry up in an hour?”<verse eID="Matt.21.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.21" osisID="Matt.21.21" n="21"/>Yeshua answered and said, “Truth I say to you: it will happen by your faith (emunah)—doubt not. It is possible not only for this fig tree, but if you say to this mountain that it will be uprooted, then it will fall into the sea. So, it will happen.<verse eID="Matt.21.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.22" osisID="Matt.21.22" n="22"/>“And everything that you seek in prayer, for it can be found in faith (emunah).”<verse eID="Matt.21.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.23" osisID="Matt.21.23" n="23"/>And when Yeshua came to the Holy Place, the priests and Pharisees approached him, for the people were saying, “By what authority do you do this, and who gave you this authority?”<verse eID="Matt.21.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.24" osisID="Matt.21.24" n="24"/>Yeshua answered and said to them, “Therefore, I will tell you one thing, and if they will tell me, I will also tell you who gave me this authority.”<verse eID="Matt.21.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.25" osisID="Matt.21.25" n="25"/>“Where was Yochanan (John), the purifier from, whether from the heavens or from the earth?” And they thought, “If we say, ‘from the heavens,’ he will say, ‘If so, why did you not believe in him?’”<verse eID="Matt.21.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.26" osisID="Matt.21.26" n="26"/>“And if we say, ‘From the sons of man,’ we are afraid because of the armies,” for they took him as a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.21.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.27" osisID="Matt.21.27" n="27"/>And they answered and said, “We do not know.” Yeshua said to them, “Even I also will not say by what power I do this.”<verse eID="Matt.21.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.28" osisID="Matt.21.28" n="28"/>“What do you think? One man had two sons, and he called for the first near him and said to him, ‘Go and till today in the vineyard!’”<verse eID="Matt.21.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.29" osisID="Matt.21.29" n="29"/>And he answered and said, “I do not want to do so.” But nevertheless, he went in repentance to<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="304" osisRef="Matt.21.29" osisID="Matt.21.29!note.304">This word is not the same word as the Hebrew word repented (i.e., תשובה teshuvah) but can also mean “repented” or “sorrowful.”</note> the vineyard.”<verse eID="Matt.21.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.30" osisID="Matt.21.30" n="30"/>And he approached the second, saying likewise to him: and he answered and said, “I will go, my father,” and he did not go.<verse eID="Matt.21.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.31" osisID="Matt.21.31" n="31"/>Who, of the two of them, did the desire of their father?” And they said, “The first.” Yeshua said to them, “In truth, I say to you, the people of the earth and children of whores will precede you in the Kingdom of Heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.21.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.32" osisID="Matt.21.32" n="32"/>“For Yochanan (John) came near to you for righteousness, and you did not believe. But the people of the land and the children of whores believed in him. You who saw even this, were likewise, did not repent to believe in him.”<verse eID="Matt.21.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.33" osisID="Matt.21.33" n="33"/>“Hear another parable: One man, the owner of the house, planted a vineyard and the surrounding area with a perimeter and fence, and dug an area in it and built a tower,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="305" osisRef="Matt.21.33" osisID="Matt.21.33!note.305">Compare to Isaiah 5:2.</note> and he retained them to one tenant and went out to the city.”<verse eID="Matt.21.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.34" osisID="Matt.21.34" n="34"/>“When the season of fruits drew near, he sent for his servants to the tenant so that he would send of its fruits to him.”<verse eID="Matt.21.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.35" osisID="Matt.21.35" n="35"/>“And the tenants seized his servants and beat one, and stoned one, and killed one.”<verse eID="Matt.21.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.36" osisID="Matt.21.36" n="36"/>“And again, he dispatched other servants, more than previously, and again, they did so.”<verse eID="Matt.21.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.37" osisID="Matt.21.37" n="37"/>“And lastly, he sent his son and said, ‘Perhaps there will be honor for my son.’”<verse eID="Matt.21.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.38" osisID="Matt.21.38" n="38"/>“And when the tenant saw his son, they thought, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="307" osisRef="Matt.21.38" osisID="Matt.21.38!note.307">Interesting note: one tenant saw his son, but then “they thought” is a unique expression. This could be intentional, but this could also mean that the</note>‘This is his successor. Let us kill him, and we will become his successors.’”<verse eID="Matt.21.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.39" osisID="Matt.21.39" n="39"/>And he seized his son outside of the vineyard, and they killed him.<verse eID="Matt.21.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.40" osisID="Matt.21.40" n="40"/>“When the owner of the vineyard comes, what will he do to the tenant?”<verse eID="Matt.21.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.41" osisID="Matt.21.41" n="41"/>They said to him, “He will kill the evil ones with evil, and give the vineyard to other tenants who will give his fruits in its time.”<verse eID="Matt.21.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.42" osisID="Matt.21.42" n="42"/>Yeshua said to them, “In truth, have you not read in the scripture, ‘The stone that the builders built into the corner of the place is from our Master, and it was marvelous in our eyes.’”<verse eID="Matt.21.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.43" osisID="Matt.21.43" n="43"/>“Because of this, I say to you that he will take from you the Kingdom of the Heavens and give it to people who produce fruits.”<verse eID="Matt.21.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.44" osisID="Matt.21.44" n="44"/>“And whoever that falls on this stone, they will be broken. And all whom it falls on, this stone crushes.”<verse eID="Matt.21.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.45" osisID="Matt.21.45" n="45"/>And when the great priests and Pharisees heard these parables, they knew that he was speaking about them.<verse eID="Matt.21.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.21.46" osisID="Matt.21.46" n="46"/>And they sought to apprehend him, but they feared the community because they took him to be like a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.21.46"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.21"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.22" osisID="Matt.22" n="22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.1" osisID="Matt.22.1" n="1"/>Yeshua answered again in a parable and said,<verse eID="Matt.22.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.2" osisID="Matt.22.2" n="2"/>“The Kingdom of Heaven resembles a man who makes a chuppah <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="308" osisRef="Matt.22.2" osisID="Matt.22.2!note.308">A chuppah (Hebrew: חֻפָּה , "covering" or "canopy") is a ceremonial canopy under which a Jewish or Messianic couple stands during their wedding, representing the home they will establish together. It generally consists of a cloth supported by four poles and embodies hospitality, divine presence,</note>for his son,” The scriptures: “The reign of the heavens is like a man, a sovereign, who made a wedding feast for his son,<verse eID="Matt.22.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.3" osisID="Matt.22.3" n="3"/>“and he sent his servants to call people to the meal, but they did not want to come.” The scriptures: and sent out his servants to call those who were invited to the wedding feast. But they would not come.<verse eID="Matt.22.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.4" osisID="Matt.22.4" n="4"/>“Once again, he sent other servants to tell all the called guests, 'That the food was prepared, and the rows and fatlings were slaughtered, and everything is good for the banquet.” The scriptures: Again he sent out other servants, saying, ‘Say to those who are invited, “See, I have prepared my dinner. My oxen and fattened cattle are slaughtered, and all is ready. Come to the wedding feast.”’<verse eID="Matt.22.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.5" osisID="Matt.22.5" n="5"/>“And they did not think, and all left, everyone to his forest, and to his merchandise.” The scriptures: But they disregarded it and went their way – this one to his field, that one to his trade.<verse eID="Matt.22.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.6" osisID="Matt.22.6" n="6"/>“Then others took these servants and scorned, and killed them.” The scriptures: And the rest, having seized his servants, insulted and killed them.<verse eID="Matt.22.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.7" osisID="Matt.22.7" n="7"/>“When the king heard, he became angry and dispatched his soldiers to destroy and to kill them, and their province with fire.” The scriptures: But when the sovereign heard, he was wroth, and sent out his soldiers, destroyed those murderers, and set their city on fire.<verse eID="Matt.22.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.8" osisID="Matt.22.8" n="8"/>Then he said again to his servants, “This meal is good, and they are not worthy to eat this food.” The scriptures: Then he said to his servants, ‘The wedding feast, indeed, is ready, but those who were invited were not worthy.<verse eID="Matt.22.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.9" osisID="Matt.22.9" n="9"/>“You go outside on the road and all whom you find, them you will call to the meal.” The scriptures: Therefore go into the street corners, and as many as you find, invite to the wedding feast.’<verse eID="Matt.22.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.10" osisID="Matt.22.10" n="10"/>“And the servants exited out on the road and gathered all whom they found, bad and good: and filled all the banquet house and reclined.” The scriptures: And those servants went out into the street corners and gathered all whom they found, both wicked and good. And the wedding hall was filled with guests.<verse eID="Matt.22.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.11" osisID="Matt.22.11" n="11"/>“And the king came and saw those leaning, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="309" osisRef="Matt.22.11" osisID="Matt.22.11!note.309">Literally, “those leaning,” meaning "reclining" or "seated around" (a table), specifically referring to reclining in comfort during a festive meal, such</note>and he saw one man who was not dressed for the meal.” The scriptures: And when the sovereign came in to view the guests, he saw there a man who had not put on a wedding garment, Aramaic And The King entered to see the smiyke {the guests}, and saw a gabra {a man} there who wasn’t wearing the bushe d’Meshthutha {the clothing of The Banquet}.<verse eID="Matt.22.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.12" osisID="Matt.22.12" n="12"/>“And he asked him, 'My friend, why did you come down to the meal, but with no attire for the meal?’” The scriptures: and he said to him, ‘Friend, how did you come in here not having a wedding garment?’ And he was speechless.<verse eID="Matt.22.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.13" osisID="Matt.22.13" n="13"/>“Then he said to his servants, ‘Bind him by the hands and feet, and cast him to the outer darkness, and there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth!’” The scriptures: Then the sovereign said to the servants, ‘Bind him hand and foot, take him away, and throw him out into the outer darkness – there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’<verse eID="Matt.22.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.14" osisID="Matt.22.14" n="14"/>‘“Many of them are called, but few are chosen.’” The scriptures: For many are called, but few are chosen.”<verse eID="Matt.22.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.15" osisID="Matt.22.15" n="15"/>Then the Pharisees went and took counsel how he would be trapped by words. The scriptures: Then the Pharisees went and plotted how to trap Him in His words.<verse eID="Matt.22.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.16" osisID="Matt.22.16" n="16"/>And they sent him their talmidim (students) with the house of Horodos (Herodes), and said to him, “Rabbi, we know that you are upright, and you teach the way of Elohim, and you have not opened your mouth to man.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="312" osisRef="Matt.22.16" osisID="Matt.22.16!note.312">This is an idiomatic expression to praise them for their integrity and patience, not intimidated by human authority.</note> And how you do not take long-suffering of man?”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="313" osisRef="Matt.22.16" osisID="Matt.22.16!note.313">This is a classical Hebrew term denoting patience or a slow temper. The particular phrasing presents a unique Rabbinic interpretation, emphasizing impartiality and the absence of favoritism toward individuals.</note> The scriptures: And they sent to Him their taught ones with the Herodians, saying, “Teacher, we know that You are true, and teach the way of Elohim in truth, and it does not concern You about anyone, for You are not partial to any man.<verse eID="Matt.22.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.17" osisID="Matt.22.17" n="17"/>And he asked him, “What do you think? By force should one give tax to Caesar, or not?” The scriptures: Then say to us, what do You think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?”<verse eID="Matt.22.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.18" osisID="Matt.22.18" n="18"/>But Yeshua knew their intention and said, “And why do you tempt me, you hypocrites?”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="314" osisRef="Matt.22.18" osisID="Matt.22.18!note.314">The Hebrew idiom כנושא פנים literally translates to "face lifter" and refers to individuals who are insincere and biased, masking their true intentions.</note><verse eID="Matt.22.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.19" osisID="Matt.22.19" n="19"/>“Show me the dinar-coin of the tax.” They offered him one dinar coin. The scriptures: Show Me the coin of the tax.” And they brought Him a denarius.<verse eID="Matt.22.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.20" osisID="Matt.22.20" n="20"/>Then Yeshua said to them, “Whose image, and signature writing is this writing?” The scriptures: And He said to them, “Whose likeness and inscription is this?”<verse eID="Matt.22.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.21" osisID="Matt.22.21" n="21"/>And they said, “Caesar’s.” He said to them, “So then, give what is Caesar’s to Caesar and what is Elohim’s give to Elohim.” The scriptures: They said to Him, “Caesar’s.” And He said to them, “Then give to Caesar what is Caesar’s, and to Elohim what is Elohim’s.”<verse eID="Matt.22.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.22" osisID="Matt.22.22" n="22"/>And when they heard this, they were amazed, then left and went away. The scriptures: And having heard, they marvelled, and left Him and went away.<verse eID="Matt.22.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.23" osisID="Matt.22.23" n="23"/>On this day, the Sadducees approached him and said, “There is no reviving <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="315" osisRef="Matt.22.23" osisID="Matt.22.23!note.315">The word תחית (tchiyat) signifies “revival.” Unlike the Greek term ἀνάστασιν (anastasin), which simply means "resurrection." The Hebrew equivalent of the Greek word is קם (kam). Therefore, תחית (tchiyat) conveys the idea of being revived by God's power, signifying renewal through</note>of the dead.” They asked and additionally said to him, The scriptures: On that day Sadducees, who say there is no resurrection, came to Him and asked Him,<verse eID="Matt.22.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.24" osisID="Matt.22.24" n="24"/>“Teacher, Mosheh (Moses) said to us, ‘If one man dies and has no sons, his brother will marry the woman and raise offspring for his brother.’” The scriptures: saying, “Teacher, Mosheh said that if anyone should die, having no children, his brother shall marry his wife and raise offspring for his brother.<verse eID="Matt.22.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.25" osisID="Matt.22.25" n="25"/>“And if there were seven brothers, the first married a wife and dies without having sons, and left his wife to his brother.” The scriptures: And there were with us seven brothers, and the first died after he had married, and having no children, left his wife to his brother.<verse eID="Matt.22.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.26" osisID="Matt.22.26" n="26"/>“And so it was in the case of the second, and in addition, the third, until the seventh.” The scriptures: In the same way the second also, and the third, unto the seventh.<verse eID="Matt.22.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.27" osisID="Matt.22.27" n="27"/>“And in the aftermath of everyone, the woman also died.” The scriptures: And last of all the woman died too.<verse eID="Matt.22.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.28" osisID="Matt.22.28" n="28"/>“In the resurrection of the dead, to whom of the seven will be a wife? Because she was married to all of them.” The scriptures: At the resurrection, then, whose wife of the seven shall she be – for all had her?” In The Resurrection, therefore, unto whom from these seven will she be an anttha {a wife}? For, they all took {married} her?”<verse eID="Matt.22.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.29" osisID="Matt.22.29" n="29"/>Yeshua answered, saying to them, “You err and have not known what the writing is, nor the power of Yehovah!” of Elohim.<verse eID="Matt.22.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.30" osisID="Matt.22.30" n="30"/>“And in the reviving of the dead, they do not marry wives, nor wives of husbands, but they are like messengers of Yehovah in the reviving of the dead.” The scriptures: For in the resurrection they do not marry, nor are they given in marriage, but are as messengers of Elohim in heaven.<verse eID="Matt.22.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.31" osisID="Matt.22.31" n="31"/>And about the reviving of the dead, have you not read what was said to you from Yehovah, who said, The scriptures: And concerning the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was spoken to you by Elohim, saying,<verse eID="Matt.22.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.32" osisID="Matt.22.32" n="32"/>“I am He, Elohim of Avraham (Abraham), and Elohim of Yitszaq (Isaac), and Elohim of Ya’acob (Jacob)?” And He is not the Elohim of the dead, but the living.” The scriptures: ‘I am the Elohim of Abṟaham, and the Elohim of Yitsḥaq, and the Elohim of Ya‛aqob’̱? Elohim is not the Elohim of the dead, but of the living.”<verse eID="Matt.22.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.33" osisID="Matt.22.33" n="33"/>And when the assembly heard, they were astonished by his teaching. The scriptures: And when the crowds heard, they were astonished at His teaching.<verse eID="Matt.22.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.34" osisID="Matt.22.34" n="34"/>When the Pharisees heard that he silenced the Sadducees, they were joined as one. The scriptures: But the Pharisees, having heard that He had silenced the Sadducees, were gathered together,<verse eID="Matt.22.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.35" osisID="Matt.22.35" n="35"/>And one of them who knew the decree of Torah asked, as if testing him. The scriptures: and one of them, one learned in the Torah, did question, trying Him, and saying,<verse eID="Matt.22.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.36" osisID="Matt.22.36" n="36"/>“Teacher, which is the greatest commandment in the Torah?” The scriptures: “Teacher, which is the great command in the Torah?”<verse eID="Matt.22.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.37" osisID="Matt.22.37" n="37"/>Yeshua said to him, “And you shall love Yehovah your Elohim with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your might, and with all your thoughts.” being, and with all your mind.’<verse eID="Matt.22.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.38" osisID="Matt.22.38" n="38"/>“This commandment is the greatest and first.” The scriptures: This is the first and great command.<verse eID="Matt.22.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.39" osisID="Matt.22.39" n="39"/>“And the second is like it, ‘And love your neighbor as yourself.’” The scriptures: And the second is like it, ‘You shall love your neighbor as yourself.’<verse eID="Matt.22.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.40" osisID="Matt.22.40" n="40"/>“On these two commandments, hang the Torah and Prophet(s).” The scriptures: On these two commands hang all the Torah and the Prophets.”<verse eID="Matt.22.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.41" osisID="Matt.22.41" n="41"/>When the Pharisees were gathered together, Yeshua asked and said,<verse eID="Matt.22.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.42" osisID="Matt.22.42" n="42"/>“What do you say about the Mashiach (Messiah)? Whose son is he?” They said, “The son of David.” The scriptures: saying, “What do you think concerning the Messiah? Whose Son is He?” They said to Him, “The Son of Dawid.̱”<verse eID="Matt.22.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.43" osisID="Matt.22.43" n="43"/>He said to them, “How did David, by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), call his Master and say thus,” The scriptures: He said to them, “Then how does Dawiḏ in the Spirit call Him ‘Master,’ saying,<verse eID="Matt.22.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.44" osisID="Matt.22.44" n="44"/>‘The Master’s declaration to my Master: ‘Sit yourself at my right-hand until I place your enemies as the footstool for your feet.’” feet’?<verse eID="Matt.22.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.45" osisID="Matt.22.45" n="45"/>“Then how does David call his Master thus? And how now is he his son?” The scriptures: If then Dawiḏ calls Him ‘Master,’ how is He his Son?”<verse eID="Matt.22.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.22.46" osisID="Matt.22.46" n="46"/>And there was no man again from that day on to question him. The scriptures: And no one was able to answer Him a word, and from that day on no one was bold enough to ask Him any more questions.<verse eID="Matt.22.46"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.22"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.23" osisID="Matt.23" n="23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.1" osisID="Matt.23.1" n="1"/>Yeshua then spoke with the community and his talmidim (students), and he said to them,<verse eID="Matt.23.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.2" osisID="Matt.23.2" n="2"/>“On the chair of Mosheh (Moses) sit scribes and Pharisees.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="316" osisRef="Matt.23.2" osisID="Matt.23.2!note.316">The Chair or Seat of Moses was a formal seat of authority within the synagogues. During the recitation of the Tanakh, the reader would stand. After the scroll was read, the reader would sit in the seat to deliver the interpretation and explanation of the text to the congregation. The speaker, seated in</note><verse eID="Matt.23.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.3" osisID="Matt.23.3" n="3"/>“All what they say <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="317" osisRef="Matt.23.3" osisID="Matt.23.3!note.317">In Shem Tov’s collection of The Hebrew Gospel of Matthew, some manuscripts use the term “yomar,” meaning “he said” (referring to Moses). This indicates that in this verse, the reference points back to Moses, emphasizing that one should follow what Moses instructed. For further details, consult George Howard’s edition of The Gospel of Matthew, page 112. Note that the Hebrew text should be referenced directly, as the English translation may contain inaccuracies, such as the incorrect use of “they.”</note>to you to do, you are to do. And whatever they keep, you are to keep. However, this you are not to do which they say to do, but they do not do.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="318" osisRef="Matt.23.3" osisID="Matt.23.3!note.318">This explanation highlights that they are imposing man-made laws on the public that are not found in the Tanakh. It also criticizes the Pharisees and</note><verse eID="Matt.23.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.4" osisID="Matt.23.4" n="4"/>“They tie a heavy burden and bear it on the shoulders of the sons of man, but by themselves, they do not want to touch them, even with one of their fingers.<verse eID="Matt.23.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.5" osisID="Matt.23.5" n="5"/>“And all their requirements they do to demonstrate to the sons of man, for they enlarge their tefillin (phylacteries)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="319" osisRef="Matt.23.5" osisID="Matt.23.5!note.319">Tefillin were first utilized in the first century CE, with both textual and archaeological evidence indicating that this practice may date back to the period of the Second Temple. Notably, references to phylactery appear in the Dead Sea Scrolls, including the “Qumran Phylactery” scrolls (Mur 4QPhyl), and several Qumran tefillin cases have been discovered. Additionally, Philo of Alexandria’s “De Specialibus Legibus” (especially IV.137–</note> and they hang the blue cord in their clothes.”<verse eID="Matt.23.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.6" osisID="Matt.23.6" n="6"/>“And they love to be the leader at the dining <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="320" osisRef="Matt.23.6" osisID="Matt.23.6!note.320">Literally, “Leaning.”</note>feast, and the leader sitting in the assembly,”<verse eID="Matt.23.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.7" osisID="Matt.23.7" n="7"/>“and to offer their ‘Peace’ in the market and be called, ‘Rabbi, Rabbi!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="321" osisRef="Matt.23.7" osisID="Matt.23.7!note.321">In the First Century, the term "rabbi" broadly denoted both "great one" and "master," reflecting the close conceptual connection between these</note><verse eID="Matt.23.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.8" osisID="Matt.23.8" n="8"/>“You are not to be called ‘Rabbi: This one, Yeshua, he is your Rabbi, and all of you are as brothers.”<verse eID="Matt.23.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.9" osisID="Matt.23.9" n="9"/>“And you are not to call any one on your earth, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="322" osisRef="Matt.23.9" osisID="Matt.23.9!note.322">Literally, “your land,” or “your earth.”</note>‘Father,’ <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="323" osisRef="Matt.23.9" osisID="Matt.23.9!note.323">Initially, it may seem appropriate to translate this as “my Father.” However, in this context, it is used grammatically as a standalone title within the</note>according to the expression of man; for One, He is your Father in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.23.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.10" osisID="Matt.23.10" n="10"/>“And you are not to be called ‘Leader,’ for one leads you; who is Mashiach (Messiah).”<verse eID="Matt.23.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.11" osisID="Matt.23.11" n="11"/>“And this one who is the greatest among you, and serves you.”<verse eID="Matt.23.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.12" osisID="Matt.23.12" n="12"/>“And whoever of him that exalts himself, he will be humbled. And whoever humbles his soul, will be exalted.”<verse eID="Matt.23.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.13" osisID="Matt.23.13" n="13"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees! Because you devour the house of widows and lengthen your prayers, and because of this, you will receive a greater judgment!”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="324" osisRef="Matt.23.13" osisID="Matt.23.13!note.324">It appears the Cochin Matthew verses 13 and 14 are in a different order than the Greek translation versification.</note><verse eID="Matt.23.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.14" osisID="Matt.23.14" n="14"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, infidels! Because you shut the kingdom of heaven before people: you do not enter, and one who wants to, is unable to enter.”<verse eID="Matt.23.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.15" osisID="Matt.23.15" n="15"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees! For you that go around sea and dry land to make one proselyte, and after being made, you make them children of Gehinnom and double against them in the judgment.”<verse eID="Matt.23.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.16" osisID="Matt.23.16" n="16"/>Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, deniers! Because you guide the blind, saying, “You who swear by the Holy Place,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="325" osisRef="Matt.23.16" osisID="Matt.23.16!note.325">In the first century, individuals would swear by the Temple, the land, the sea, and other significant objects. The appropriate method of swearing involved invoking God’s Holy Name. This act allowed them to affirm falsehoods as long as God’s name was not used. Although the Law of Moses permitted swearing by God's name to establish the truth (Deuteronomy 6:13), religious leaders such as scribes and Pharisees developed a sophisticated system of oaths. This system distinguished between binding oaths—such as swearing by the gold in the Temple—and non-binding</note> have no worry. But whoever swears over the gold needs to collect taxes for debt.”<verse eID="Matt.23.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.17" osisID="Matt.23.17" n="17"/>“Foolish and blind ones! What is greater, the Holy Place or the gold? But the Holy Place sanctifies the gold!”<verse eID="Matt.23.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.18" osisID="Matt.23.18" n="18"/>“And whoever swears over the altar has no worry, but whoever swears over the offering is made like a debtor!”<verse eID="Matt.23.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.19" osisID="Matt.23.19" n="19"/>“Foolish and blind ones! What is greater, the altar or the offering? Or does the altar sanctify the offering?”<verse eID="Matt.23.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.20" osisID="Matt.23.20" n="20"/>“And whoever swears by the altar, swears by it, and what all that there is on it.”<verse eID="Matt.23.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.21" osisID="Matt.23.21" n="21"/>“And whoever swears by the Holy Place, swears by it, and whatever that is in it.”<verse eID="Matt.23.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.22" osisID="Matt.23.22" n="22"/>“And whoever swears by the heavens, is like swearing by the throne of Yehovah, and by who sits on it.”<verse eID="Matt.23.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.23" osisID="Matt.23.23" n="23"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, deniers! For you tithe herbs, and anise, and cumin, and forsake the weightier edicts: clemency, and mercy, and faith. This they do not remember to do, and by means of this, not to be idle.<verse eID="Matt.23.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.24" osisID="Matt.23.24" n="24"/>“Associations of the blind, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="327" osisRef="Matt.23.24" osisID="Matt.23.24!note.327">This phrase is another way of saying that they are also comparable to blind individuals.</note>who pray because of a mosquito, and swallow camels!”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="328" osisRef="Matt.23.24" osisID="Matt.23.24!note.328">Camels are unclean animals and are forbidden to be eaten. It is also forbidden according to Torah to touch the carcass of a dead camel. See</note><verse eID="Matt.23.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.25" osisID="Matt.23.25" n="25"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, deniers! Why do you clean the cup from the outside, but its inside is full of filth and injustice?”<verse eID="Matt.23.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.26" osisID="Matt.23.26" n="26"/>“Associations of the blind! Cleanse first inside the cup and dish, therefore, for the outside to also be clean.”<verse eID="Matt.23.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.27" osisID="Matt.23.27" n="27"/>“Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, deniers! For you are like a tomb cast from the outside beautifully, but its inside is filled with bones of the dead and all defilement!”<verse eID="Matt.23.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.28" osisID="Matt.23.28" n="28"/>“So also you from the outside appear to people as righteous, but your inside is filled with extortion and deceptions.”<verse eID="Matt.23.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.29" osisID="Matt.23.29" n="29"/>“Woe to you, Pharisees and scribes, deniers! For you construct the gravesite of the prophet, and the tombstone of the righteous!”<verse eID="Matt.23.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.30" osisID="Matt.23.30" n="30"/>“And you say, ‘If we were in the days of our fathers, we would not have been in cooperation with them in the blood of the prophet.’”<verse eID="Matt.23.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.31" osisID="Matt.23.31" n="31"/>“And you are confessors, testifying against yourselves, that you are children of those who killed the prophet.”<verse eID="Matt.23.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.32" osisID="Matt.23.32" n="32"/>“And though you filled those attributes of your fathers.”<verse eID="Matt.23.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.33" osisID="Matt.23.33" n="33"/>“Snakes and offspring of vipers! How can you escape the judgment of Gehinnom?”<verse eID="Matt.23.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.34" osisID="Matt.23.34" n="34"/>“Because of this, I send prophets, and sages, and scribes.” From them they will kill and crucify, and from them they beat in your communities and also persecute them from province to province,”<verse eID="Matt.23.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.35" osisID="Matt.23.35" n="35"/>“but that there comes upon you, all the blood that you shed of the righteous on the earth, from the blood of righteous Hebel (Abel) and until the blood of Zekharyah (Zechariah), the son of Berehyah (Barachiah), whom you killed between the Holy Place and the altar.”<verse eID="Matt.23.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.36" osisID="Matt.23.36" n="36"/>“Truth, I say to you: This all will come upon this family.”<verse eID="Matt.23.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.37" osisID="Matt.23.37" n="37"/>“Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), who kills the prophet and stones those sent to you. How many times have I desired to collect your children like the hen collects her chicks under her wings,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="330" osisRef="Matt.23.37" osisID="Matt.23.37!note.330">God protects His people with the description of wings or bird. See Psalm 91:4, Psalm 46:1, Psalm 57:1, Psalm 61:4, and Psalm 36:7.</note> and you were unwilling?”<verse eID="Matt.23.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.38" osisID="Matt.23.38" n="38"/>This is desolation for you, and your house is laid waste.”<verse eID="Matt.23.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.23.39" osisID="Matt.23.39" n="39"/>“I say to you that you will not see me entirely until you say, ‘Blessed is he who came in the Name of Yehovah!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="332" osisRef="Matt.23.39" osisID="Matt.23.39!note.332">Due to the ban on using God’s Holy Name by the Pharisees in the second century, this phrase cannot be spoken by Jewish communities in Israel.</note><verse eID="Matt.23.39"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.23"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.24" osisID="Matt.24" n="24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.1" osisID="Matt.24.1" n="1"/>And Yeshua went out of the Holy Place to go, and his talmidim (students) approached and showed him the building of the Holy Place.<verse eID="Matt.24.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.2" osisID="Matt.24.2" n="2"/>He said to them, “Do you not see all of this? In this truth, I say to you, it will not be much longer until there will not be left one stone upon another, that will not be destroyed.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="335" osisRef="Matt.24.2" osisID="Matt.24.2!note.335">Derived from the root ר-ת-ס (S-T-R), it connotes tearing down, demolishing, or destroying a structure. This root is frequently used in Rabbinic Hebrew, including the Mishnah and Talmud, as well as in Aramaic. Given the rabbinic context of the manuscript, it is interpreted to mean “was destroyed.”</note><verse eID="Matt.24.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.3" osisID="Matt.24.3" n="3"/>Then, when Yeshua sat on the Mount of Olives, his talmidim (students) approached and said among themselves, “Are you not going to tell us when this will be? What will be the sign of your coming and the retribution of the world?”<verse eID="Matt.24.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.4" osisID="Matt.24.4" n="4"/>Yeshua answered and said to them, “You are to see <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="339" osisRef="Matt.24.4" osisID="Matt.24.4!note.339">Idiomatic meaning, “See to it.”</note>that not any man deceives you.”<verse eID="Matt.24.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.5" osisID="Matt.24.5" n="5"/>“For many will come in My Name and say, ‘I am he, the Messiah,’ and they will mislead many.”<verse eID="Matt.24.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.6" osisID="Matt.24.6" n="6"/>“You are about to hear wars and the neighings of war, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="340" osisRef="Matt.24.6" osisID="Matt.24.6!note.340">ומצהלות מלחמה (u-mitzaholot milchama): This phrase, meaning "the shouts or neighing of war," uses the noun מצהלות (singular מִצְהָלָה ), derived from the biblical root ל-ה-צ. It can be translated as "battle cries" or "shouts of battle." In biblical Hebrew, the root ל-ה-צ often describes the high-pitched neighing of horses (as in Jeremiah 8:16, 50:11) or cries of joy and triumph (like in Isaiah 12:6, 24:14). By using the plural מצהלות מלחמה , the translator replaces the abstract idea of auditory "rumors" with a vivid image of battlefield noise — the shouting of soldiers or the neighing of warhorses charging into battle. This choice of words reflects a style typical of Rabbinic or medieval Hebrew, aimed at creating a poetic and vivid auditory impression.</note>but you will see and not fear from all this, even though it has not become the recompense of the world.”<verse eID="Matt.24.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.7" osisID="Matt.24.7" n="7"/>“For nation will rise against a nation, and kingdom against kingdom. And there will be famine, pestilence, and quaking of the earth in every place.”<verse eID="Matt.24.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.8" osisID="Matt.24.8" n="8"/>“All these are the beginning of the sorrows.”<verse eID="Matt.24.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.9" osisID="Matt.24.9" n="9"/>“Then he (they) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="341" osisRef="Matt.24.9" osisID="Matt.24.9!note.341">This may be an inadvertent omission of a suffix by the scribe. The addition of a final vav suffix transforming ימסר (yimsor) to ימסרו (yimseru) would change the meaning from “he delivered” to “they delivered." Consequently, the translation accounts for this potential error and offers a corrected version.</note>will deliver you, and will kill you, and all the nations will abhor you for the sake of My Name.”<verse eID="Matt.24.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.10" osisID="Matt.24.10" n="10"/>“Then many will stumble, and hate one another, and they will betray one another.”<verse eID="Matt.24.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.11" osisID="Matt.24.11" n="11"/>“And many false <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="342" osisRef="Matt.24.11" osisID="Matt.24.11!note.342">This word שקר (sheker) means, “falsehood” or “lie.” These are lying prophets.</note>prophets will arise and deceive many.”<verse eID="Matt.24.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.12" osisID="Matt.24.12" n="12"/>“And due to increasing iniquity, the goodness of many becomes cold.”<verse eID="Matt.24.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.13" osisID="Matt.24.13" n="13"/>“Whoever waits <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="344" osisRef="Matt.24.13" osisID="Matt.24.13!note.344">This word מייחל (meyachel) means “those who hope” or “expect” with the expectation of a good outcome.</note>until the end of the world will have freedom.”<verse eID="Matt.24.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.14" osisID="Matt.24.14" n="14"/>“And this gospel shall be proclaimed as a declaration in every kingdom of the world, to testify to all nations, so then peace will come.”<verse eID="Matt.24.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.15" osisID="Matt.24.15" n="15"/>“When <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="346" osisRef="Matt.24.15" osisID="Matt.24.15!note.346">This phrase directly translates to, “What is this you observe...".</note>you see this, the abomination of desolation told by Daniel the prophet, that is standing in the Holy Place,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="347" osisRef="Matt.24.15" osisID="Matt.24.15!note.347">Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11.</note> then whoever reads is astonished.”<verse eID="Matt.24.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.16" osisID="Matt.24.16" n="16"/>“Then those Yehudites (Judeans) should flee from their harm to the mountain.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="348" osisRef="Matt.24.16" osisID="Matt.24.16!note.348">During times of war, people would seek refuge on Mount Mariah or Mount Zion in the ancient city of Jerusalem. When enemies captured the city, inhabitants would make a final stand at the temple. The mention of a single mountain in this verse suggests that the people might be fleeing to a mountain associated with the temple, such as Mount Sinai in northwestern Saudi Arabia, or to another distinctive mountain.</note><verse eID="Matt.24.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.17" osisID="Matt.24.17" n="17"/>And whoever is on the roof, let them not go down to take from the house.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="350" osisRef="Matt.24.17" osisID="Matt.24.17!note.350">This word ( מבית - m’beit) means, “from the house.” However, if a vav suffix ( מביתו - m’beit’o) is added, then it is “from his house.” It is possible that the vav suffix was omitted by mistake.</note><verse eID="Matt.24.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.18" osisID="Matt.24.18" n="18"/>“And those who are in the field should not turn back to take their clothes.”<verse eID="Matt.24.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.19" osisID="Matt.24.19" n="19"/>“Woe to those pregnant <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="352" osisRef="Matt.24.19" osisID="Matt.24.19!note.352">Literally, “of the womb” and refers to, “those who are pregnant.”</note>and those suckling in that day.”<verse eID="Matt.24.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.20" osisID="Matt.24.20" n="20"/>“You are to pray that your escape is not on the day of the snow and not on the sabbath.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="353" osisRef="Matt.24.20" osisID="Matt.24.20!note.353">This can be interpreted either as "a Sabbath," referring to an unspecified Sabbath, or “the Sabbath," indicating a specific Sabbath, depending on vocalization. בַשַבָת (ba-shabbat) means "On the Sabbath" (note the vowel under the first letter), while בְשַבָת (b'shabbat) means "On a Sabbath” (note the two vertical dots under the first letter). Therefore, without knowledge of the original vocalization, the final translation remains uncertain.</note><verse eID="Matt.24.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.21" osisID="Matt.24.21" n="21"/>“Then there will be much sorrow, that has not been like this from the beginning of the world until now, nor even will there be like it.”<verse eID="Matt.24.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.22" osisID="Matt.24.22" n="22"/>“And unless those days be hasten, all flesh would not live, because the collector<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="354" osisRef="Matt.24.22" osisID="Matt.24.22!note.354">Since the collectors are rushing ( משום שגובים ממהרים ), the phrase literally means “because [the] collectors are rushing.” In this context, the active masculine plural participle גובים (from the root ה-ב-ג, meaning "to collect or demand payment”) functions as a noun, referring to "creditors,” “collectors,” or “divine enforcers." In rabbinic and apocalyptic texts, the term is used metaphorically to describe angels of destruction or agents of divine punishment sent to enforce retribution for human sins. This phrase exemplifies the dual role of the Hebrew participle: גובים serves as the subject, "the collectors," while ממהרים (from ר-ה-מ) functions as the active verb, "are rushing." Joseph Gebhardt-Kein, in his translation, “The Travancore-Cochin, India Manuscript of Ezekiel Rahabi’s Rabbinical-Hebrew Matthew: Text and Translation,” rendered this word as “elect,” probably to suggest a unique group implementing this plan.</note> hastens in that day.”<verse eID="Matt.24.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.23" osisID="Matt.24.23" n="23"/>“Then if a man says <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="355" osisRef="Matt.24.23" osisID="Matt.24.23!note.355">Literally, “If a man asks of you…”</note>to you, 'In here is he, the Messiah,’ or ‘There!’ you are not to believe.”<verse eID="Matt.24.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.24" osisID="Matt.24.24" n="24"/>“There will be established at that time, a lying messiah and a false prophet, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="356" osisRef="Matt.24.24" osisID="Matt.24.24!note.356">The verb יקומון (yekumon) signifies that "they” will arise, while the terms משיח שקר ונביא appear to refer to a single false prophet and a false messiah. This word appears in Daniel 7:17.</note>and he will give great signs, so that they may mislead<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="357" osisRef="Matt.24.24" osisID="Matt.24.24!note.357">This verb can be a simple future tense (yiqtol) meaning, “will mislead,” or it can be a jussive future meaning, “may mislead.”</note> also the elect.”<verse eID="Matt.24.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.25" osisID="Matt.24.25" n="25"/>“This, I said to you before.”<verse eID="Matt.24.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.26" osisID="Matt.24.26" n="26"/>“All this, they say to you, 'This one is in the desolate place,’ you should not go out; or ‘He is in the room,’<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="360" osisRef="Matt.24.26" osisID="Matt.24.26!note.360">It can also refer to an "inner chamber.”</note> you are not to believe.”<verse eID="Matt.24.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.27" osisID="Matt.24.27" n="27"/>“Like the lightning <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="361" osisRef="Matt.24.27" osisID="Matt.24.27!note.361">Literally, “lightnings.”</note>that go out from the east and are seen unto the west, so it will be for his coming of the Son of Man.”<verse eID="Matt.24.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.28" osisID="Matt.24.28" n="28"/>“This place is a spoiling decay; <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="364" osisRef="Matt.24.28" osisID="Matt.24.28!note.364">This statement implies the presence of carcasses.</note>there the vultures will gather.”<verse eID="Matt.24.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.29" osisID="Matt.24.29" n="29"/>“Quickly after this sorrow of that day, ‘The sun will become darkened, and the moon will not shine, and the stars will fall from the heavens,’<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="366" osisRef="Matt.24.29" osisID="Matt.24.29!note.366">Isaiah 13:10 and Ezekiel 32:7.</note> and the power of the heavens will be shaken.”<verse eID="Matt.24.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.30" osisID="Matt.24.30" n="30"/>“And then will appear the sign of the Son of Man in the heavens, and then all the families of the earth will skip about (mourn)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="367" osisRef="Matt.24.30" osisID="Matt.24.30!note.367">Joseph Gebhardt-Kein, in his translation, “The Travancore-Cochin, India Manuscript of Ezekiel Rahabi’s Rabbinical-Hebrew Matthew: Text and Translation,” translated this word as “mourn.” It is possible that the scribe intended to use the word יקדרון (yikderun) from the root kadar, meaning “they will mourn” or “grow dark,” perhaps misreading the dalet as a resh. Then, וָאז ירקדון (v’az yirkadun) means, “and then they will dance or skip about.” The verb includes a Paragogic Nun suffix ( ון -ּ / -un), an old form found in Biblical Hebrew poetry (like in Isaiah 26:19) and in Aramaic. This suffix adds a solemn, prophetic tone. The root ד-ק-ר (“to dance or leap”) is unusual if the passage is meant to express fear or dread. To clarify this, the translator might be imagining a vivid, unsettling scene of nature shaking or leaping under divine judgment, similar to Psalm 114:4 (“The mountains skipped like rams”).</note> and ‘see the Son of Man who comes upon the clouds of the heavens’<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="368" osisRef="Matt.24.30" osisID="Matt.24.30!note.368">Daniel 7:13.</note> with many forces<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="369" osisRef="Matt.24.30" osisID="Matt.24.30!note.369">Because the root ל-י-ח is heavily tied to the biblical concept of חַיִל (chayil - meaning military power, host, or substance), the word חֲיָלֹות (chayalot, with a soft 'ch' sound) was adopted in late biblical and rabbinic Hebrew as a collective, gender-neutral noun specifically meaning 'armies' or 'legions'. However, in Modern Hebrew, an entirely different word spelled the exact same way— חַיָלֹות (chayalot, with a hard, doubled 'y')—functions strictly as the plural noun for female soldiers.</note> and praises.”<verse eID="Matt.24.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.31" osisID="Matt.24.31" n="31"/>“And he will send his messenger with a great shofar, and be joined together, his proven ones from the four winds<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="370" osisRef="Matt.24.31" osisID="Matt.24.31!note.370">The Four winds term is also found in Daniel 7:2, Ezekiel 37:9, Jeremiah 49:36, and Revelation 7:1.</note> of the world, from the beginning of the heavens unto the other beginning of the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.24.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.32" osisID="Matt.24.32" n="32"/>“From the fig tree, you are to learn allegories: as quickly as its branches become green and buds;<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="371" osisRef="Matt.24.32" osisID="Matt.24.32!note.371">The phrase עליו (v’novlim alav) is understood as “and [its] leaves sprout or put forth.” However, the text contains a notable contradiction or possible scribal error. The active plural participle ( נובלים from root )ל means “wilting,” “fading,” or “falling נ-ב- off,” which conflicts with the passage's context—namely, the Synoptic Fig Tree Parable (see Matthew 24:32; Mark 13:28)— where the tree appears to flourish and come alive as a sign of summer. It is likely that a scribe or translator mistakenly confused the root ל with a similar-sounding or similar-meaning root in Rabbinic Hebrew -ל-ב-ל , נ-ב- ב (l-b-l-b), meaning “to sprout, bud, or blossom.” The original text probably intended to say ומלבלבים עליו (u-melavlevim alav), meaning “and its leaves are budding ”. This correction aligns with the botanical imagery and the theological message of the apocalyptic sign.</note> by this you will know that summer has arrived.”<verse eID="Matt.24.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.33" osisID="Matt.24.33" n="33"/>“So, therefore, when you see all this, know that these have drawn near to the gate.”<verse eID="Matt.24.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.34" osisID="Matt.24.34" n="34"/>“Truth I say to you: This family <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="372" osisRef="Matt.24.34" osisID="Matt.24.34!note.372">This word can also mean, “generation” or “kindred.”</note>will not pass away until all has come of this matter.”<verse eID="Matt.24.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.35" osisID="Matt.24.35" n="35"/>“The heavens and the earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="373" osisRef="Matt.24.35" osisID="Matt.24.35!note.373">The Word will not pass away, even if Heaven and Earth pass away. It is established forever. Reference Cochin Matthew 5:18.</note><verse eID="Matt.24.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.36" osisID="Matt.24.36" n="36"/>“Regarding that day and that hour, there is no man who knows, not even the messengers, but only my Father who is in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.24.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.37" osisID="Matt.24.37" n="37"/>“As the days of Noah were, so will it be in his coming <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="374" osisRef="Matt.24.37" osisID="Matt.24.37!note.374">Refer to the footnote in Matthew 24:3.</note>of the Son of Man.”<verse eID="Matt.24.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.38" osisID="Matt.24.38" n="38"/>“For as they were before the flood, eating and drinking, and taking wives and giving women husbands, until the day that Noah went up to the ark,”<verse eID="Matt.24.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.39" osisID="Matt.24.39" n="39"/>“and they did not know until the flood came and carried all of them away, so it will be the coming of the Son of Man.”<verse eID="Matt.24.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.40" osisID="Matt.24.40" n="40"/>“Then two will be in the wilderness, one will be taken, and one will be left.”<verse eID="Matt.24.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.41" osisID="Matt.24.41" n="41"/>“Two are grinding at one mill, one is taken, and one will be left.”<verse eID="Matt.24.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.42" osisID="Matt.24.42" n="42"/>“They should all be awake! <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="382" osisRef="Matt.24.42" osisID="Matt.24.42!note.382">This verb is a Hit’pael and in this situation, is reflexive, meaning to awaken themselves.</note>You do not know in which hour your Master comes.”<verse eID="Matt.24.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.43" osisID="Matt.24.43" n="43"/>“For know this: if the master of the house had known which watch <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="384" osisRef="Matt.24.43" osisID="Matt.24.43!note.384">Literally translates to "guard,” signifying the specific type of "watch.”</note>the thief comes, he would have been awake, and not allowed him to break into his house.”<verse eID="Matt.24.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.44" osisID="Matt.24.44" n="44"/>“Because of this, even this, you are to be prepared, because you do not know the hour that the Son of Man comes.”<verse eID="Matt.24.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.45" osisID="Matt.24.45" n="45"/>“Who is he that is a faithful and wise servant, whose master established him over the children of his house to give them their food at its time?”<verse eID="Matt.24.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.46" osisID="Matt.24.46" n="46"/>“Fortunate is that servant whose Master comes and finds him doing so.”<verse eID="Matt.24.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.47" osisID="Matt.24.47" n="47"/>“Truth I say to you that he grants him authority over everything that he has.”<verse eID="Matt.24.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.48" osisID="Matt.24.48" n="48"/>“If this evil servant says and thinks in his heart, “’My master delays his coming,’”<verse eID="Matt.24.48"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.49" osisID="Matt.24.49" n="49"/>“and he began to eat at the meal, and was eating and drinking with the drunkards,”<verse eID="Matt.24.49"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.50" osisID="Matt.24.50" n="50"/>“then his master will come on a day that he does not think, and in that hour he did not know.”<verse eID="Matt.24.50"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.24.51" osisID="Matt.24.51" n="51"/>“And he will divide and set their portion with the deniers: <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="385" osisRef="Matt.24.51" osisID="Matt.24.51!note.385">Can also mean “unbelievers,” “infidels,” or “apostates.”</note>and they will be weeping and gnashing their teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.24.51"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.24"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.25" osisID="Matt.25" n="25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.1" osisID="Matt.25.1" n="1"/>“Then it will be like the ten virgins who took burning lamps, and went out to meet the bridegroom and bride.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="386" osisRef="Matt.25.1" osisID="Matt.25.1!note.386">The Cochin manuscript includes the word וכלָה (v’kallah), meaning “and the bride.” While this reading aligns with textual traditions in Eastern sources such as the Classical Syriac Peshitta, this agreement should not be taken to suggest that the Cochin text is derived from or translated from the Peshitta. The Cochin Cambridge text reflects a Judean Semitic context, whereas the Peshitta is preserved in a Syriac dialect, representing a different linguistic and regional tradition. Rather than indicating dependence, the shared reading is best understood as evidence of a common or overlapping Semitic tradition at certain points within the textual history. This correspondence highlights the complexity of the manuscript’s transmission while still demonstrating how Semitic textual traditions preserved elements of first-century Jewish wedding customs.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.2" osisID="Matt.25.2" n="2"/>“And five of them were intelligent, and five foolish.”<verse eID="Matt.25.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.3" osisID="Matt.25.3" n="3"/>“And the foolish took burning lamps <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="390" osisRef="Matt.25.3" osisID="Matt.25.3!note.390">This term translates literally as "torches.” The Cochin Hebrew Matthew 25 passage employs the term לפיד (lappīd) meaning "torch," drawing a deliberate connection to Genesis 15:17, where Yehovah manifests as a "flaming torch" to affirm His covenant with Abraham.</note>and did not take oil with them.” The scriptures: Those who were foolish, having taken their lamps, took no oil with them,<verse eID="Matt.25.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.4" osisID="Matt.25.4" n="4"/>“That they, the intelligent, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="391" osisRef="Matt.25.4" osisID="Matt.25.4!note.391">The term נבונים (nevonim) in the Cochin Cambridge Matthew used to mean "discerning" or "intelligent," rather than simply "wise." In Hebrew, a navon actively anticipates future needs, emphasizing that readiness involves spiritual foresight rather than passive knowledge.</note>took the oil in vessels with the burning lamps.”<verse eID="Matt.25.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.5" osisID="Matt.25.5" n="5"/>“Since the bridegrooms were delayed, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="392" osisRef="Matt.25.5" osisID="Matt.25.5!note.392">She-nit’achar chatanim ( שנתאחר חתנים ): This phrase appears to contain a grammatical inconsistency. The verb she-nit’achar (“he was delayed”) is singular, suggesting that the bridegroom’s delay is intentional—a deliberate act meant to test and strengthen faith. By contrast, the noun chatanim (“bridegrooms”) appears in the plural, likely reflecting a minor scribal error by a copyist who anticipated the plural forms that follow. The verb belongs to the Nit’pael stem, a form that blends features of the Nif’al and Hitpa’el stems and was commonly used in Mishnaic Hebrew to express reflexive action. The Nit’pael was in use during the first century CE and later disappeared. In this context, the intensive-reflexive force of the form conveys the sense that “he delayed himself” or “he lingered intentionally.” Rather than describing a delay caused by outside circumstances, the grammar presents the Bridegroom as fully in control of the timing.</note>they all dozed and slept.”<verse eID="Matt.25.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.6" osisID="Matt.25.6" n="6"/>“And at midnight, there was a cry, ‘The bridegroom has come, go out to meet him!’”<verse eID="Matt.25.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.7" osisID="Matt.25.7" n="7"/>“Then all the virgins rose up and trimmed the burning lamps.”<verse eID="Matt.25.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.8" osisID="Matt.25.8" n="8"/>“The fools said to the intelligent, ‘Give us of your oil,’ because the burning lamps have gone out.”<verse eID="Matt.25.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.9" osisID="Matt.25.9" n="9"/>“The intelligent ones answered them and said, ‘Why? <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="394" osisRef="Matt.25.9" osisID="Matt.25.9!note.394">Both Byzantine and Alexandrian Greek manuscript traditions use “No” instead of “Why”. Shem Tov’s Hebrew Matthew does not contain either word.</note>There is not enough for you and for us. Rather, go to those who sell, and buy for yourselves.’”<verse eID="Matt.25.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.10" osisID="Matt.25.10" n="10"/>“And when they were going to buy, the bridegroom came, and those who were intelligent entered with him in the house of the chuppah,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="395" osisRef="Matt.25.10" osisID="Matt.25.10!note.395">Figuratively speaking, this can also be interpreted as 'entering the wedding place.”</note> and they closed the doors.”<verse eID="Matt.25.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.11" osisID="Matt.25.11" n="11"/>“Then in the end, the other virgins also came and said, ‘My Master, my Master, open for us!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="397" osisRef="Matt.25.11" osisID="Matt.25.11!note.397">Petach lan פתח לן , meaning “open [the door] for us," showcases a notable linguistic feature within the Cochin manuscript tradition, where the Aramaic prepositional pronoun לן (lan) replaces the Hebrew לנו (lanu). This reflects the bilingual Jewish scribal environment and is consistent with the interaction between Hebrew and Aramaic, rather than an error. The plea petach lan echoes familiar Jewish liturgical language, especially the Ne’ilah prayers of Yom Kippur ( פתח לנו שער ), but in an Aramaicized form, possibly preserving an early Semitic idiom rooted in first-century traditions.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.12" osisID="Matt.25.12" n="12"/>“He answered this and said, ‘Truth I say to you, that I do not know you.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="398" osisRef="Matt.25.12" osisID="Matt.25.12!note.398">See Matthew 7:21-23. The Hebrew phrasing is somewhat awkward and directly translates to, "Among you.”</note><verse eID="Matt.25.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.13" osisID="Matt.25.13" n="13"/>“All should be awake. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="399" osisRef="Matt.25.13" osisID="Matt.25.13!note.399">This word יתעוררו (yit’oreru) is in the yiqtol voice, but acts as a Jussive. Therefore, the translation reflects the Jussive voice by saying “...should be awake,” “Let them wake up,” or “everyone should be awakened.”</note>You do not know it, that day, nor its <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="400" osisRef="Matt.25.13" osisID="Matt.25.13!note.400">This can also translate to “his hour.” 20 yit’orru ha kol lo yod’in atem ( יתעוררו הכל לא יודעין אתם ) — Lit., “let everyone awaken themselves; you do not know.” This climactic exhortation reflects a layered and characteristically Semitic mode of expression. The use of the Hitpa’el Jussive yit’orru ( יתעוררו ) echoes the language of prophetic calls to awakening (cf. Isa 51:9; 52:1), framing the warning in a manner consistent with classical Israelite exhortation. At the same time, the participial form yod ’in ( יודעין ), with its Aramaic plural ending, reflects the natural interplay between Hebrew and Aramaic in the period's linguistic environment. The combination of a classical Hebrew verbal form with an Aramaic-influenced participle suggests a setting in which these languages functioned together seamlessly, consistent with patterns known from the late Second Temple and early Rabbinic periods. Additionally, refer to Matthew 24:36; this phrase is frequently associated with the Feast of Trumpets.</note>hour.”<verse eID="Matt.25.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.14" osisID="Matt.25.14" n="14"/>“Like a rich man who called his servants, and handed over his possessions to them.”<verse eID="Matt.25.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.15" osisID="Matt.25.15" n="15"/>“There was to one, he gave five talents, and there was to another, he gave two, each man according to his strength.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="403" osisRef="Matt.25.15" osisID="Matt.25.15!note.403">This passage demonstrates the linguistic sophistication of the scribe through its complex morpho-syntactic structure, which reflects a strategic integration of various historical language layers. The term "kicharin"—referring to the ancient Near Eastern unit of weight known as a talent (derived from the root ר-כ-כ)—contains the late Aramaic plural suffix "-in," indicative of the socio-economic context of the post-exilic and Second Temple periods. Additionally, the idiomatic use of איש איש "ish ish," a classical Mosaic legal expression signifying individual responsibility, underscores the archaic and authoritative tone of the passage (see Lev 15:2, 22:18; Num 5:12). Economically, a single talent measured approximately 75 pounds of precious metals, representing a substantial capital equivalent to nearly two decades of typical daily wages. .</note> Then he went away quickly.”<verse eID="Matt.25.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.16" osisID="Matt.25.16" n="16"/>“This one who received five talents went and traded with them, and profited five other talents.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="405" osisRef="Matt.25.16" osisID="Matt.25.16!note.405">This phrase, "Halach she-lakach" ( הלך זה שלקח ), literally means “This same one who took... went.” The passage is anchored in the first-century context by two clear marketplace references from the late Second Temple period. The first is the phrase "v'asah schorah" ( ועשה סחורה ), meaning "and he conducted trade," which indicates post-exilic commerce activity. The second is the verb "v'hirviach" ( והרויח ), meaning "and he gained profit." This term reflects a shift in meaning from physical breath to financial gain that occurred around that era.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.17" osisID="Matt.25.17" n="17"/>“And so it was, the one with two also made a trade with them, and he gained two other talents.”<verse eID="Matt.25.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.18" osisID="Matt.25.18" n="18"/>“This one who received one, went and dug in the earth and buried his master’s silver."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="408" osisRef="Matt.25.18" osisID="Matt.25.18!note.408">Hu zeh she-lakach... v'hitmin kesef... ( והטמין כסף ... הוא זה שלקח ...): This phrase, meaning “This same person who took and hid money,” uses a stylistic structure that directly echoes the previous verse. The choice of the causative verb v'hitmin ( והטמין / "and he buried/concealed") is significant. Early Israelite legal traditions, especially around the turn of the era (see Mishnah Bava Metzia 3:11), recognized burying entrusted money deeply in the ground as the only legally accepted way to prevent theft. If funds were stored in any other manner and theft occurred, the trustee was personally responsible. By specifying that the servant hid the money in the ground, the text effectively reflects the legal practices and societal mindset of a first-century Israelite trustee.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.19" osisID="Matt.25.19" n="19"/>“After a great time, the master of those servants, he came and took of them their account.”<verse eID="Matt.25.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.20" osisID="Matt.25.20" n="20"/>“And he who had received five talents approached, and he brought five others, and he said, ‘My master, five talents you gave to me, and this, five others I have gained upon them.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="410" osisRef="Matt.25.20" osisID="Matt.25.20!note.410">V'karav hu... v’hikriv... hirvachti aleyhem ( הרוחתי עליהם ... והקריב ... וקרב הוא ): Lit., “And he approached... and brought near... I gained profit upon them.” Scripturally, the passage moves with an elegant, inner-Semitic root repetition, shifting from the simple active Pa'al verb v'karav (“he approached”) directly into its causative Hif'il counterpart v'hikriv (“he caused to draw near / presented”). Furthermore, the financial phrase hirvachti aleyhem employs the definitive turning-of-the-era marketplace vocabulary, in which the root ח-ו-ר shifts from denoting classical “breathing space” to indicating monetary yield. This verb is paired with the analytical spatial preposition aleyhem (“upon them”) to structurally denote accumulated interest—a grammatical formulation highly characteristic of transactional records, commercial receipts, and legal phrasing common to the first-century Israelite milieu.</note> The scriptures: And he who had received five talents came and brought five other talents, saying, ‘Master, you delivered to me five talents. See, I have gained five more talents besides them.’<verse eID="Matt.25.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.21" osisID="Matt.25.21" n="21"/>“His master said to him, 'Good! Good and faithful servant; over a small matter you were faithful, and I am establishing you over much. Enter into the joy of your master.'”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="411" osisRef="Matt.25.21" osisID="Matt.25.21!note.411">This Hebrew phrase, 'Tov eved... al davar kal... ani mekim otcha', which translates to “Well done, servant... over a light matter... I am establishing you.” The language reflects a first-century rhetorical and legal style, particularly in the phrase 'al davar kal,' meaning 'over a light matter,' which is rooted in the well-known legal concept of 'kal va-chomer' or 'light and heavy.' The statement 'ani mekim,' meaning 'I am establishing,' uses a present-causative form to convey a formal, legally binding decree of promotion.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.22" osisID="Matt.25.22" n="22"/>“And he, having two talents approached, and said, 'My master, two talents you gave to me, and behold, two other talents I have gained upon them.”<verse eID="Matt.25.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.23" osisID="Matt.25.23" n="23"/>“His master said to him, ‘Good! Good and faithful servant; you were faithful over a small matter, and over much I am establishing you. Enter into the joy of your master!’” The scriptures: His master said to him, ‘Well done, good and trustworthy servant. You were trustworthy over a little, I shall set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master.’<verse eID="Matt.25.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.24" osisID="Matt.25.24" n="24"/>“And he who had received one talent also approached, and he said, ‘My master, let it be a known thing to you that you are a harsh man; you reaped from a place that you did not sow, and you want to gather from a place that you did not scatter.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="412" osisRef="Matt.25.24" osisID="Matt.25.24!note.412">This passage, 'V'karav gam hu... yedi'ah yihyeh lecha... ( ידיעה יהיה לך ... וקרב גם הוא ...): Literal translation: “And he also approached... let knowledge be to you...,” serves as an important textual marker. The wording features the complex pronoun phrase hu she-lakach (“he who took”), a stylistic device common in late-antiquity literature. This is supported by the formal legal phrase yedi'ah yihyeh lecha, a standard expression used in Second Temple period contractual disputes.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.25" osisID="Matt.25.25" n="25"/>“‘And I was afraid, and I went, and I hid it in a pit in the earth; so look, that was yours!’”<verse eID="Matt.25.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.26" osisID="Matt.25.26" n="26"/>“The master answered and said to him, ‘Evil servant! From where did you know me, and I having reaped from a place from which I did not sow, and I bought from a place which I did not scatter?’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="413" osisRef="Matt.25.26" osisID="Matt.25.26!note.413">Eved ra... me'heichan yadata... ( מהיכן ידעת ... עבד רע ...): Lit., “Evil servant... from where did you know...” Scripturally, the passage is anchored by the compound interrogative me'heichan (“from where”), a definitive dialectal shift of the late Second Temple period that replaces the classical biblical me'ayin.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.27" osisID="Matt.25.27" n="27"/>“‘And was it not for you to give my silver to the money-changers? And I would have come and demanded from him my mammon with interest?’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="415" osisRef="Matt.25.27" osisID="Matt.25.27!note.415">V'ani hayiti ba v'tove'a... mamon sheli... ( ממון שלי ... ואני הייתי בא ותובע ...): The phrase literally means, "Then I would have come and demanded my own capital." The passage uses a very advanced grammatical structure called a periphrastic past counterfactual, which combines the past tense auxiliary with active participles to express a situation that did not happen but was considered possible. Syntactically, the key part of the argument is the word "tove'a," meaning "demanding" or "claiming," which works together with the loanword "mamon" for "capital" or "wealth," and the phrase "sheli" meaning "my own."</note><verse eID="Matt.25.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.28" osisID="Matt.25.28" n="28"/>“‘Take the talent from him, and give it to the man who has ten talents!’”<verse eID="Matt.25.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.29" osisID="Matt.25.29" n="29"/>“‘To whomever has, it is given to him, and it will be added to him; but this one who doesn’t have, even what he has is taken from him.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="417" osisRef="Matt.25.29" osisID="Matt.25.29!note.417">This verse states, "To whoever has... is given... is taken from him.” The passage uses a symmetrical pattern with three passive verbs: nitan, nosaf, and nilkach. In Second Temple-period Israelite literature, this use of the passive voice often functions as a divine passive, emphasizing God's ultimate authority and judgment. The repetition of root words (ח-ק-ל and ן-ת-נ) in reverse order creates a clever internal wordplay.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.30" osisID="Matt.25.30" n="30"/>“‘This servant who is idle, expel him to Gehinnom (Gehenna), and there he is weeping and gnashing his teeth!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="418" osisRef="Matt.25.30" osisID="Matt.25.30!note.418">This text discusses a particular phrase: "This servant who is worthless... cast him out...” The phrase uses the Hebrew word "batel," meaning "idle" or "worthless," which was common in late Second Temple times to describe someone holding a trust without producing legal effect. The command "hotzi'uhu," meaning "cast him out," is an imperative directed at attendants, typical of first-century Israeli legal stories. Textually, this wording is very important: while Greek versions introduce the idea of "outer darkness" as a new theological concept, the Jewish tradition from Cochin stays clear and grounded in early Israelite beliefs by explicitly mentioning Gehinnom, the fiery punishment. This ties the judgment to the historic Hinnom Valley, preserving clarity and consistency in the narrative.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.31" osisID="Matt.25.31" n="31"/>“After this Son of Man comes in his glories, and all the holy messengers with him, then he will sit upon the throne of the kingdom.”<verse eID="Matt.25.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.32" osisID="Matt.25.32" n="32"/>“All the nations were assembled near him, and he shall separate them one from another like the shepherd who separates sheep from the goats.”<verse eID="Matt.25.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.33" osisID="Matt.25.33" n="33"/>“And he will place the sheep upon his right, and the goats upon his left.”<verse eID="Matt.25.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.34" osisID="Matt.25.34" n="34"/>“Then the King said to those who are on his right, ‘Come, blessed ones of my Father, and inherit the kingdom that is prepared for you from before the world came to be.”<verse eID="Matt.25.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.35" osisID="Matt.25.35" n="35"/>“I was hungry, and you gave me food; and I thirsted, and you caused me to drink water; I was a stranger, and you brought me into your houses.”<verse eID="Matt.25.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.36" osisID="Matt.25.36" n="36"/>“I was naked, and you covered me, I was sick, and you visited me, and I was in the prison house, and you came near me.”<verse eID="Matt.25.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.37" osisID="Matt.25.37" n="37"/>“Then those righteous said, ‘Our Master, when did we see you that you were hungry, and we fed you, or you were thirsty, and we caused you to drink?’”<verse eID="Matt.25.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.38" osisID="Matt.25.38" n="38"/>“And when did we see you a stranger, and we brought you in, or you were naked, and we covered you?”<verse eID="Matt.25.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.39" osisID="Matt.25.39" n="39"/>“And when did we see you sick, or in the prison house, and we came near you?”<verse eID="Matt.25.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.40" osisID="Matt.25.40" n="40"/>“And the king responded and said to them, ‘Truth I say to you, just as you did it to one of these, my smallest brothers, you did it to me.’”<verse eID="Matt.25.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.41" osisID="Matt.25.41" n="41"/>“Then he said to those who are on his left, ‘Go away from me, lost ones, to Gehinnom (Gehenna),<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="423" osisRef="Matt.25.41" osisID="Matt.25.41!note.423">This phrase, "l'gehinnom l'olam..." ( לגיהנם לעולם ...), translates to “into Gehenna forever.” In textual analysis, the use of the word gehinnom ( גיהנם ) serves as an important marker for determining the original text. Comparing this with George Howard's edition of the Shem Tov Hebrew Gospel of Matthew provides valuable insights. Although the Shem Tov tradition does not explicitly include the word Gehinnom in its parallel verse (Matthew 25:41), it uses the phrase אש לעולם (“eternal fire”), which was a common way of describing Gehenna in early Israelite apocalyptic thought. Howard, George. Hebrew Gospel of Matthew. 2nd ed. Cleanwater, MD: Independent Publishers, 1995.</note> whose fire burns forever, and to the Satans, and to the messengers!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="424" osisRef="Matt.25.41" osisID="Matt.25.41!note.424">The Hebrew phrase, v'la-sh'tanim v'la-shluchim ( ולשטנים ולשולחים ), translates to "and for the satans, and for the messengers.” Rather than using a singular noun for a cosmic being, the text uses the masculine plural ולשטנים (v'la-sh'tanim), meaning "the accusers" or "adversaries." This plural form aligns with early Second Temple and apocalyptic Jewish literature, which viewed celestial prosecutors collectively as "the satans" (see 1 Enoch 40:7). The grammatical structure forms a vivid parallel with ולשולחים (v'la-shluchim), translating to "the dispatched messengers" or the “sent ones” dispatched by Satan. This imagery evokes a hierarchical, court-like realm akin to a royal or judicial system of first-century Israelite culture, where cosmic beings are cast in roles reminiscent of courtiers and officials within a grand, authoritative court.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.42" osisID="Matt.25.42" n="42"/>“Because I was hungry, and you did not give me food; and I was thirsty, and you did not cause me to drink.”<verse eID="Matt.25.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.43" osisID="Matt.25.43" n="43"/>“And I was a stranger, and you did not bring me in; and I was sick, and in the prison house, and you did not come near me.”<verse eID="Matt.25.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.44" osisID="Matt.25.44" n="44"/>Then they also answered and said, “Our Master, when did we see you hungry, or thirsty, or a stranger, or naked, or sick, or in the prison house, and we did not minister to you.”<verse eID="Matt.25.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.45" osisID="Matt.25.45" n="45"/>“Then he answered and said to them, ‘Truth I say to you, just as you did not do it to one of these small ones, even to me, you did not do it.”<verse eID="Matt.25.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.25.46" osisID="Matt.25.46" n="46"/>“And these will go away to Gehinnom (Gehenna) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="425" osisRef="Matt.25.46" osisID="Matt.25.46!note.425">This phrase, 'l'gehinnom l'olam...' ( לגיהנם לעולם ...), translates to “into Gehenna forever.” A noteworthy comparison appears in George Howard’s edition of the Shem-Tov Hebrew Gospel of Matthew at Matthew 25:46. While the Cochin tradition explicitly names the destination as gehinnom ( גיהנם ), the Shem-Tov version uses לדראון עולם (l'deraon olam), which Howard translates as “eternal abhorrence.'</note>forever, the righteous to eternal life.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="426" osisRef="Matt.25.46" osisID="Matt.25.46!note.426">The Hebrew phrase, v'yelechu elu l'gehinnom... ( וילכו אלו לגיהנם ...): literally translated as "And these shall go into Gehenna...” From a textual criticism standpoint, the choice of the noun gehinnom ( גיהנם ) is particularly significant. While Greek manuscripts often introduce the idea of "eternal punishment" (kolasin aiōnion), an abstract theological concept that concludes the discussion, the Cochin tradition stays true to early Israelite thought by connecting the judgment to a specific geographical location. By retaining the term gehinnom, the text emphasizes the physical and final nature of judgment within the landscape of the Hinnom Valley, avoiding foreign theological concepts and preserving an authentic ancient perspective on punishment.</note><verse eID="Matt.25.46"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.25"/>
      </div>
  </osisText>
</osis>
